/**
 * @fn std::string SageInterface::get_name(const SgNode* node)
 *
 * @brief Returns a string representation of the name for an IR node.
 *
 * This function is useful for debugging purposes. It provides a general way to 
 * obtain a name for any Intermediate Representation (IR) node in the ROSE AST. 
 * The function recursively traverses the IR tree, handling different types of nodes,
 * such as statements, expressions, symbols, and types, to extract their names.
 *
 * @param node A pointer to an SgNode object representing the IR node for which a name is desired.
 *             Must not be NULL. If the node is not recognized or does not have a name, 
 *             the function may print an error message and abort.
 *
 * @return A string containing the name of the input IR node. The returned string may be "undefined_name" 
 *         if the node's type is unknown or does not support naming.
 *
 * @warning This function uses recursive function calls to traverse the IR tree, which may lead to a stack overflow
 *          for very large inputs. Additionally, the function may print error messages and abort if it encounters an unrecognized node.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be non-NULL and point to a valid SgNode object in the ROSE AST.
 *
 * @post The returned string is a valid name for the input IR node or "undefined_name" if no name can be determined.
 *
 * @note This function provides a general way to obtain names for IR nodes but may not cover all possible cases. 
 *       It is intended primarily for debugging purposes and should be used judiciously in production code.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* node = ...; // obtain an SgNode object from the ROSE AST
 *           std::string name = SageInterface::get_name(node);
 *           printf("Name of node: %s\n", name.c_str());
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn string SageInterface::get_name(const SgStatement* stmt)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves a name associated with a given statement.
 *
 * This function computes a name for the input statement. The algorithm used
 * is based on the type of the statement and its properties. If the statement
 * is a declaration, it uses the name of the declaration. Otherwise, if the 
 * statement has a scope, it uses the name of the scope. For other types of 
 * statements, it relies on a switch-case logic to determine the appropriate 
 * name.
 *
 * @param stmt The input statement for which the name is to be retrieved.
 *          Must not be NULL and should be an instance of SgStatement or its
 *          subclasses.
 *
 * @return A string representing the name associated with the input statement.
 *         If no suitable name can be found, returns "undefined_name".
 *
 * @warning The function relies on a switch-case logic for some types of 
 * statements and may not cover all possible cases. Also, it assumes that 
 * the input statement is valid and does not perform extensive error checking.
 *
 * @pre stmt != NULL
 * @post The returned string represents a name associated with the input statement.
 *
 * @note This function uses various helper functions to determine the type of 
 * the statement and its properties. These functions may have their own 
 * preconditions and postconditions that should be respected.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // assume a valid statement
 *           string name = SageInterface::get_name(stmt);
 *           cout << "Name: " << name << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
