/**
 * @fn string SageInterface::get_name(const SgC_PreprocessorDirectiveStatement* directive)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the name of a given preprocessor directive statement.
 *
 * This function takes a pointer to an SgC_PreprocessorDirectiveStatement object and
 * returns its associated name as a string. The function uses the class_name() method
 * of the input directive statement to determine its name. If the directive statement is
 * NULL, the function asserts and terminates the program.
 *
 * @param directive A pointer to an SgC_PreprocessorDirectiveStatement object.
 *                   Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The name of the input preprocessor directive statement as a string.
 *
 * @warning If the input directive statement is NULL, the function will assert and
 *          terminate the program.
 *
 * @pre The input directive statement must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned string represents the name of the input directive statement.
 *
 * @note This function uses a switch statement to handle different types of directives.
 *       However, it appears that all cases simply return the class_name() of the
 *       directive, making the switch statement redundant. Additionally, the function
 *       includes a default case that prints a warning message and aborts the program,
 *       but this can be avoided by properly handling all possible directive types.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgC_PreprocessorDirectiveStatement* directive = ...;
 *           string name = SageInterface::get_name(directive);
 *           cout << "The name of the directive is: " << name << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
