 * @param None
 *
 * @return True if the current language may contain non-declarations in scope,
 *         false otherwise.
 *
 * @warnings This function assumes that the interface has already been set up
 *           correctly for the language being processed. If not, results are
 *           undefined.
 *
 * @preconditions The SageInterface object must have been initialized and set
 *                up for a specific programming language before calling this
 *                function.
 *
 * @postconditions None
 *
 * @notes This function is primarily used internally by the interface to guide
 *        its parsing and analysis of source code. It may also be useful in some
 *        cases for external clients of the interface, but such use should be
 *        carefully considered as it assumes a good understanding of the
 *        interface's internal workings.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           // Set up the interface for some language...
 *           if (sage.language_may_contain_nondeclarations_in_scope()) {
 *              // Handle languages that may contain non-declarations in scope...
 *           } else {
 *              // Handle languages that do not allow this...
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::resetScopeNumbers(SgFunctionDefinition* functionDefinition)
 *
 * @brief Resets the scope numbers for a given SgFunctionDefinition.
 *
 * This function resets the scope numbers for a given SgFunctionDefinition by performing
 * a preorder traversal of the abstract syntax tree (AST) and assigning unique numbers to each scope.
 * The scope numbers are stored in a map within the SgFunctionDefinition object.
 *
 * @param functionDefinition A pointer to an SgFunctionDefinition object. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning If the input SgFunctionDefinition is empty or ill-formed, this function may produce undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input SgFunctionDefinition must not be NULL and its scope number list must be empty.
 *
 * @post The scope numbers for the given SgFunctionDefinition are reset and stored in a map within the object.
 *
 * @note This function uses a preorder traversal of the AST to assign unique numbers to each scope.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an SgFunctionDefinition object
 *           SgFunctionDefinition* funcDef = ...;
 *
 *           // Reset the scope numbers for the function definition
 *           SageInterface::resetScopeNumbers(funcDef);
 *
 *           // Now the scope numbers are reset and stored in a map within the funcDef object
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::clearScopeNumbers(SgFunctionDefinition* functionDefinition)
 *
 * @brief Clears the scope numbers stored in a given SgFunctionDefinition object.
 *
 * This function clears the cache of stored (scope,integer) pairs within a 
 * given SgFunctionDefinition object. It ensures that the scope number list is 
 * reset to an empty state, allowing for new calculations or updates to be made.
 *
 * @param functionDefinition The SgFunctionDefinition object whose scope numbers are to be cleared.
 *                            Must not be NULL and must contain a valid scope number list.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error checking on the input 
 *          parameter beyond ensuring it is not NULL. It assumes that the 
 *          provided SgFunctionDefinition object has been properly initialized 
 *          and contains a valid scope number list.
 *
 * @pre The input functionDefinition object must not be NULL and must contain a 
 *      valid scope number list.
 *
 * @post The scope number list within the input functionDefinition object will 
 *       be cleared, containing no elements.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the internal state of the provided 
 *       SgFunctionDefinition object by clearing its scope number list. It does 
 *       not return any value and does not throw exceptions for invalid input.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *             // Assuming SageInterface is a valid interface with access to clearScopeNumbers
 *             SgFunctionDefinition* funcDef = ...;  // Initialize or obtain a valid SgFunctionDefinition object
 *             SageInterface::clearScopeNumbers(funcDef);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement SageInterface::enclosingNamespaceScope( SgDeclarationStatement* declaration )
 *
 * @brief Returns the namespace associated with a given declaration.
 *
 * This function computes the enclosing namespace for a given declaration
 * statement. If the declaration is nested inside other scopes, it iterates
 * through these scopes to find the first namespace. This is important for
 * template handling in ROSE, ensuring that generated template specializations
 * are placed in the correct location (namespace).
 *
 * @param declaration The SgDeclarationStatement* for which the enclosing namespace is to be computed.
 *                     Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* associated with the input declaration. Returns nullptr if no namespace is found.
 *
 * @warning If the declaration is not in a namespace, this function returns nullptr.
 *
 * @pre The input declaration must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned namespace scope is either an SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement or nullptr.
 *
 * @note This function iterates through scopes until it finds a namespace or reaches the global scope.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgDeclarationStatement* declaration = ...;
 *           SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* namespaceScope = SageInterface::enclosingNamespaceScope(declaration);
 *           if (namespaceScope != nullptr) {
 *             // Use the enclosing namespace scope as needed
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isPrototypeInScope ( SgScopeStatement* scope, SgFunctionDeclaration* functionDeclaration, SgDeclarationStatement* startingAtDeclaration )
 *
 * @brief Checks if a prototype of a given template member function declaration is already present in the specified scope.
 *
 * This function searches for an existing previously inserted (or existing) template member function declaration 
 * within the provided scope and returns true if found, indicating that there's no need to add another one. 
 * The search starts from the specified startingAtDeclaration position in the scope's declaration list.
 *
 * @param scope The SgScopeStatement object representing the scope where the prototype is to be searched for.
 * @param functionDeclaration The SgFunctionDeclaration object representing the template member function declaration to be searched for.
 * @param startingAtDeclaration The SgDeclarationStatement object representing the position in the scope's declaration list 
 *                             from which the search should start.
 *
 * @return True if a prototype of the given template member function declaration is already present in the specified scope, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input parameters are valid and not null. It does not perform any error checking on them.
 *
 * @pre The scope parameter must be non-null and represent a valid SgScopeStatement object.
 * @pre The functionDeclaration parameter must be non-null and represent a valid SgFunctionDeclaration object.
 * @pre The startingAtDeclaration parameter must be non-null and represent a valid SgDeclarationStatement object.
 *
 * @post If the function returns true, it means that a prototype of the given template member function declaration 
 *       is already present in the specified scope, and there's no need to add another one. If the function returns false, 
 *       it means that no such prototype was found, and a new one can be added if needed.
 *
 * @note This function uses the get_declarationList() method of SgScopeStatement class which is only properly defined for certain scopes.
 * @note The search performed by this function starts from the specified startingAtDeclaration position in the scope's declaration list 
 *       and proceeds linearly until the end of the list. If a match is found, the loop exits early to optimize performance.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = ...; // Initialize scope object
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* functionDeclaration = ...; // Initialize function declaration object
 *           SgDeclarationStatement* startingAtDeclaration = ...; // Initialize starting declaration object
 *
 *           bool foundExistingPrototype = SageInterface::isPrototypeInScope(scope, functionDeclaration, startingAtDeclaration);
 *
 *           if (foundExistingPrototype)
 *             {
 *               printf("A prototype of the given template member function declaration is already present in the specified scope.\n");
 *             }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isAncestor(SgNode* node1, SgNode* node2)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given node is an ancestor of another node in the AST.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) upwards from node2 to
 * determine if node1 is an ancestor of node2. An ancestor is a node that contains
 * another node either directly or indirectly through other nodes.
 *
 * @param node1 The potential ancestor node.
 * @param node2 The node whose ancestors are being checked.
 *
 * @return True if node1 is an ancestor of node2, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that both input nodes are valid and exist in the AST.
 *          It does not perform any checks for null pointers or invalid node references.
 *
 * @pre Both node1 and node2 must be non-null pointers to SgNode objects.
 *
 * @post The function returns a boolean value indicating whether node1 is an ancestor of node2.
 *
 * @note This function uses a simple iterative approach to traverse the AST upwards from node2.
 *       It does not use any recursive algorithms, making it more memory-efficient for large ASTs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* node1 = ...; // some node in the AST
 *           SgNode* node2 = ...; // some other node in the AST
 *           if (SageInterface::isAncestor(node1, node2)) {
 *             cout << "node1 is an ancestor of node2" << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "node1 is not an ancestor of node2" << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::dumpPreprocInfo(SgLocatedNode* locatedNode)
 *
 * @brief Dumps preprocessing information attached to a given SgLocatedNode.
 *
 * This function prints detailed information about the preprocessing directives 
 * attached to a specific node in the abstract syntax tree (AST). It retrieves 
 * the attached preprocessing info from the input node and iterates over each 
 * directive, printing its classification, string format, and relative position.
 *
 * @param locatedNode The SgLocatedNode instance for which the preprocessing information is to be dumped.
 *                     Must not be NULL. The function asserts this precondition.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function uses printf() statements for output, which may not be suitable 
 *          for all use cases, especially in multi-threaded environments or when logging 
 *          mechanisms are preferred over direct console output.
 *
 * @pre The input node must not be NULL. This precondition is asserted within the function.
 *
 * @post If preprocessing information is attached to the node, it will be printed to the console.
 *
 * @note This function does not handle cases where the input node has no attached preprocessing 
 *       information gracefully; it simply prints a message indicating this condition.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgLocatedNode* myNode = ...; // Obtain or create an SgLocatedNode instance
 *           SageInterface::dumpPreprocInfo(myNode);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn PreprocessingInfo * SageInterface::findHeader(SgSourceFile * source_file, const std::string & header_file_name, bool isSystemHeader)
 *
 * @brief Finds the preprocessing information for a given header file in a source file.
 *
 * This function searches for the preprocessing information of a specified header file 
 * within a given source file. It checks if the header file has been included in the source 
 * file and returns the corresponding preprocessing information if found.
 *
 * The search is performed by iterating over all declarations in the global scope of the 
 * source file and checking their attached preprocessing information for include directives.
 *
 * @param source_file The source file to search for the header file inclusion.
 * @param header_file_name The name of the header file to search for.
 * @param isSystemHeader A boolean indicating whether the header file is a system header or not.
 *
 * @return The preprocessing information for the given header file if found, otherwise NULL.
 *
 * @warning If the header file has been included multiple times in the source file, this function 
 *          returns the preprocessing information of the first occurrence.
 *
 * @pre The source file and header file name must be valid and not empty.
 *
 * @post The returned preprocessing information is valid only if the header file was found in the source file.
 *
 * @note This function uses a cache (fileHeaderDict) to store the results of previous searches for performance optimization.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgSourceFile* source_file = ...; // initialize source file
 *            std::string header_file_name = "my_header.h";
 *            bool isSystemHeader = false;
 *            PreprocessingInfo* info = SageInterface::findHeader(source_file, header_file_name, isSystemHeader);
 *            if (info != NULL) {
 *              // process the preprocessing information
 *            }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn PreprocessingInfo* SageInterface::insertHeader(SgSourceFile * source_file, const std::string & filename, bool isSystemHeader , bool asLastHeader)
 *
 * @brief Inserts a header file into the given source file.
 *
 * This function inserts a header file with the specified filename into the given source file. 
 * The insertion point can be controlled by specifying whether it should be inserted before or after other declarations in the global scope.
 * If the source file is empty, an error message will be printed and the program will terminate.
 *
 * @param source_file The source file where the header will be inserted.
 * @param filename The name of the header file to be inserted.
 * @param isSystemHeader Whether the header file is a system header or not. 
 *          If true, the header file will be included using angle brackets; otherwise, it will be included using double quotes.
 * @param asLastHeader Whether the header should be inserted after all other declarations in the global scope or not.
 *          If true, the header will be inserted after all other declarations; otherwise, it will be inserted before them.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the PreprocessingInfo object representing the inserted header file, or NULL if insertion fails.
 *
 * @warning The function may terminate the program with an error message if the source file is empty.
 *
 * @pre The source_file parameter must not be NULL. 
 *      The filename parameter must be a valid string.
 *
 * @post If successful, the function will insert the header file into the source file and return a pointer to the PreprocessingInfo object representing the inserted header.
 *
 *       If unsuccessful (i.e., if the source file is empty), the function will print an error message and terminate the program.
 *
 * @note The function assumes that the source file has a valid global scope. 
 *       If the source file does not have a global scope, the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgSourceFile* source_file = ...; // assume some source file object
 *           PreprocessingInfo* result = SageInterface::insertHeader(source_file, "my_header.h", false, true);
 *           if (result != NULL) {
 *               cout << "Header inserted successfully!" << endl;
 *           } else {
 *               cerr << "Error inserting header!" << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::insertHeader (SgStatement* stmt, PreprocessingInfo* newheader, bool asLastHeader)
 *
 * @brief Inserts a preprocessing header into the given statement.
 *
 * This function inserts a preprocessing header into the given statement. The
 * insertion position is determined by the value of the third parameter,
 * asLastHeader. If true, the new header is inserted after any existing headers;
 * otherwise, it is inserted before any existing headers.
 *
 * @param stmt The statement where the header will be inserted. Must not be NULL.
 * @param newheader The preprocessing header to be inserted. Must not be NULL.
 * @param asLastHeader Determines whether the new header should be inserted after
 *                     (true) or before (false) any existing headers.
 *
 * @return None.
 *
 * @warning If the statement has no attached preprocessing information, the new
 *          header will be appended to the end of the statement.
 *
 * @pre The statement and newheader parameters must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The new header is inserted into the given statement according to the
 *       specified position.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the attached preprocessing information of the
 *       given statement. It does not modify the statement itself.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // obtain a statement pointer
 *           PreprocessingInfo* newheader = ...; // create a preprocessing header
 *           SageInterface::insertHeader(stmt, newheader, true);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::insertHeader(SgSourceFile * source_file, const string & header_file_name, bool isSystemHeader, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType position)
 *
 * @brief Inserts a header file into the given source file at the specified position.
 *
 * This function inserts a header file with the given name into the source file
 * at the specified position. If the header file is a system header, it is included
 * using angle brackets; otherwise, it is included using double quotes. The function
 * returns a PreprocessingInfo object representing the inserted #include directive.
 *
 * @param source_file The source file into which the header file is to be inserted.
 *                     Must not be NULL.
 * @param header_file_name The name of the header file to be inserted.
 * @param isSystemHeader A boolean indicating whether the header file is a system header.
 * @param position The position at which the header file is to be inserted, either
 *                  before or after the global scope.
 *
 * @return A PreprocessingInfo object representing the inserted #include directive.
 *
 * @warning If the source file uses token-based unparsing, this function may not work correctly.
 *          In such cases, a null declaration must be added to attach the #include directive directly to it.
 *
 * @pre The source file is not NULL, and the position is either before or after the global scope.
 *
 * @post A PreprocessingInfo object representing the inserted #include directive is returned.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the given source file by inserting a header file into it.
 *       If the source file uses token-based unparsing, additional steps may be necessary to ensure correct output.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgSourceFile* source_file = ...;
 *           string header_file_name = "my_header.h";
 *           bool isSystemHeader = false;
 *           PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType position = PreprocessingInfo::before;
 *           SageInterface::insertHeader(source_file, header_file_name, isSystemHeader, position);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn PreprocessingInfo* insertHeader(const std::string& filename)
 * @brief Inserts a header file into an AST.
 *
 * @param filename The name of the header file to be inserted.
 * @return A pointer to the newly created PreprocessingInfo object or NULL if insertion failed.
 */

/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::moveUpPreprocessingInfo(SgStatement * stmt_dst, SgStatement * stmt_src, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType src_position/*=PreprocessingInfo::undef*/, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType dst_position/*=PreprocessingInfo::undef*/, bool usePrepend /*= false */)
 *
 * @brief Moves preprocessing information from one statement to another.
 *
 * This function moves the preprocessing information associated with stmt_src
 * to stmt_dst. The movement is based on the relative positions specified by 
 * src_position and dst_position. If usePrepend is true, the preprocessing info is prepended;
 * otherwise, it is appended.
 *
 * @param stmt_dst The destination statement where the preprocessing information will be moved to.
 *                 Must not be NULL.
 * @param stmt_src The source statement from which the preprocessing information will be moved.
 *                 Must not be NULL and must be different from stmt_dst.
 * @param src_position The relative position of the preprocessing info in stmt_src. Defaults to PreprocessingInfo::undef.
 * @param dst_position The relative position where the preprocessing info will be placed in stmt_dst. Defaults to PreprocessingInfo::undef.
 * @param usePrepend If true, the preprocessing info is prepended; otherwise, it is appended. Defaults to false.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not check if the source and destination statements are valid or if they belong to the same translation unit.
 *
 * @pre stmt_dst and stmt_src must be non-NULL and different from each other.
 *
 * @post The preprocessing information associated with stmt_src is moved to stmt_dst based on the specified relative positions and usePrepend flag.
 *
 * @note This function calls movePreprocessingInfo() internally, which performs the actual movement of preprocessing information.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* src_stmt = ...; // source statement with preprocessing info
 *           SgStatement* dst_stmt = ...; // destination statement where preprocessing info will be moved
 *           SageInterface::moveUpPreprocessingInfo(dst_stmt, src_stmt);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void moveDeclarationToInnermostScope_test2015_123::movePreprocessingInfo(SgStatement* src, SgStatement* stmt_dst, bool usePrepend, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType src_position, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType dst_position, AttachedPreprocessingInfoType* infoToRemoveList)
 * @brief Moves preprocessing information from one statement to another.
 *
 * This function moves the specified preprocessing information from one
 * statement to another based on the provided parameters. It checks if the
 * desired source type is not specified or matching a specified desired source
 * type and moves all source preprocessing info accordingly.
 *
 * @param src The source statement to move the preprocessing information from.
 * @param stmt_dst The destination statement to move the preprocessing information to.
 * @param usePrepend Whether to prepend or append the preprocessing information.
 * @param src_position The relative position of the preprocessing information in the source statement.
 * @param dst_position The relative position of the preprocessing information in the destination statement.
 * @param infoToRemoveList A list of preprocessing information to remove from the source statement.
 */

/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::cutPreprocessingInfo(SgLocatedNode* src_node, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType pos, AttachedPreprocessingInfoType& save_buf)
 *
 * @brief Cuts preprocessing information from the given source node and saves it to a buffer.
 *
 * This function removes certain elements from the attached preprocessing information of the given source node
 * based on their relative position. The removed elements are copied to a provided buffer before being deleted.
 * 
 * @param src_node The source node whose attached preprocessing information is to be modified. Must not be null.
 * @param pos The relative position type that determines which elements to remove and copy.
 * @param save_buf The buffer where the removed elements will be saved.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the attached preprocessing information of the given source node in-place.
 *
 * @pre The src_node must not be null, and the pos must be a valid relative position type.
 *
 * @post The attached preprocessing information of the src_node has been modified according to the given relative position,
 *       and the removed elements have been copied to the save_buf.
 *
 * @note This function uses the isRelPos() predicate to determine which elements to remove and copy based on their relative position.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgLocatedNode* src_node = ...;
 *           PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType pos = ...;
 *           AttachedPreprocessingInfoType save_buf;
 *           SageInterface::cutPreprocessingInfo(src_node, pos, save_buf);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::pastePreprocessingInfo (SgLocatedNode* dst_node, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType pos, AttachedPreprocessingInfoType& save_buf)
 *
 * @brief Pastes preprocessing information into a node.
 *
 * This function pastes the preprocessing information stored in save_buf
 * into the destination node dst_node at a specified position. The position
 * is relative to the existing preprocessing information attached to the node.
 *
 * The function first creates an info list for the destination node if it does not already exist.
 * It then copies the preprocessing information from save_buf to the info list based on the specified position.
 *
 * @param dst_node The destination node where the preprocessing information is to be pasted.
 *                 Must be a non-null pointer to a valid SgLocatedNode object.
 *
 * @param pos      The relative position where the preprocessing information is to be pasted.
 *                 Can be one of PreprocessingInfo::before, PreprocessingInfo::after, or PreprocessingInfo::inside.
 *
 * @param save_buf The buffer containing the preprocessing information to be pasted.
 *                  Must be a non-empty container of AttachedPreprocessingInfoType objects.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning If pos is PreprocessingInfo::inside, a warning message will be printed to cerr indicating that this position is not supported.
 *
 * @pre     dst_node must be a non-null pointer to a valid SgLocatedNode object.
 * @pre     save_buf must be a non-empty container of AttachedPreprocessingInfoType objects.
 * @post    The preprocessing information in save_buf will be pasted into the info list of dst_node at the specified position.
 *
 * @note   If pos is PreprocessingInfo::before, the preprocessing information will be inserted at the beginning of the info list.
 *         If pos is PreprocessingInfo::after, the preprocessing information will be appended to the end of the info list.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgLocatedNode* node = ...; // obtain a valid SgLocatedNode object
 *           AttachedPreprocessingInfoType save_buf; // create and populate the buffer with preprocessing information
 *           SageInterface::pastePreprocessingInfo(node, PreprocessingInfo::before, save_buf);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::attachArbitraryText(SgLocatedNode* target, const std::string & text, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType position /*=PreprocessingInfo::before*/)
 *
 * @brief Attaches arbitrary text to a given SgLocatedNode in the AST as a preprocessing directive.
 *
 * This function is used to attach user-defined text to an AST node. The attached text
 * can be any string and will be treated as a preprocessing directive when the AST is 
 * unparsed. By default, the text is attached before the target node, but this behavior 
 * can be modified by specifying a different relative position.
 *
 * @param target The SgLocatedNode to which the text is to be attached. Must not be NULL.
 * @param text The arbitrary text to be attached as a preprocessing directive.
 * @param position The relative position at which the text is to be attached, with respect 
 *                  to the target node. Defaults to PreprocessingInfo::before if not specified.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the newly created PreprocessingInfo object representing the 
 *         attached text, or NULL on failure.
 *
 * @warning This function currently attaches the arbitrary text as a #if CPP declaration,
 *          which may not be the desired behavior. It is recommended to use this function
 *          with caution and consider alternative approaches if possible.
 *
 * @pre The target node must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post A new PreprocessingInfo object representing the attached text will be created 
 *       and added to the target node's attached preprocessing info list.
 *
 * @note This function is currently implemented as a workaround for attaching arbitrary
 *       text to an AST node. It may be revised or replaced in future versions of ROSE.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgLocatedNode* target = ...; // obtain the target node
 *            std::string text = "This is some arbitrary text.";
 *            PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType position = PreprocessingInfo::before;
 *            SageInterface::attachArbitraryText(target, text, position);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::replaceMacroCallsWithExpandedStrings(SgPragmaDeclaration* target)
 *
 * @brief Replaces macro calls with their expanded strings in a given SgPragmaDeclaration.
 *
 * This function iterates over the attached preprocessing information of a given 
 * SgPragmaDeclaration and replaces any macro calls found within its pragma text
 * with their corresponding expanded strings. It utilizes the Wave library to expand
 * the macros, if available.
 *
 * @param target The SgPragmaDeclaration for which the macro calls are to be replaced.
 *                Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input SgPragmaDeclaration by replacing its 
 *          pragma text with the modified version containing expanded macro calls.
 *
 * @pre The input SgPragmaDeclaration must have attached preprocessing information.
 *
 * @post The pragma text of the input SgPragmaDeclaration is modified to contain
 *       expanded macro calls, if any were found and replaced.
 *
 * @note This function is part of Wave support in ROSE and may not be available or 
 *       functional if the Wave library is not properly configured or compiled.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgPragmaDeclaration* pragmaDecl = ...; // obtain a valid SgPragmaDeclaration
 *           SageInterface::replaceMacroCallsWithExpandedStrings(pragmaDecl);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn PreprocessingInfo* SageInterface::attachComment(SgSourceFile * source_file, const std::string & content, PreprocessingInfo::DirectiveType directive_type, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType  position)
 *
 * @brief Attaches a comment to the specified source file.
 *
 * This function attaches a comment with the given content and directive type
 * to the specified source file at the specified relative position. The comment
 * is added as a preprocessing info node to the global scope of the source file.
 *
 * @param source_file The source file to which the comment will be attached.
 *                     Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @param content     The text content of the comment to be attached.
 *
 * @param directive_type The type of the directive (e.g., #define, #include, etc.).
 *
 * @param position    The relative position where the comment will be inserted,
 *                     either before or after the current preprocessing info
 *                     nodes in the global scope.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the newly created PreprocessingInfo node representing
 *          the attached comment, or NULL if an error occurs.
 *
 * @warning If the source file is NULL, this function will assert and terminate.
 *
 * @pre The source file must not be NULL.
 * @post The comment is successfully attached to the source file at the specified position.
 *
 * @note This function creates a new PreprocessingInfo node and adds it to the
 *       global scope of the source file. If an error occurs during this process,
 *       the function will return NULL.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgSourceFile* source_file = ...; // obtain a valid source file
 *           std::string content = "This is a comment.";
 *           PreprocessingInfo::DirectiveType directive_type = PreprocessingInfo::CStyleComment;
 *           PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType position = PreprocessingInfo::before;
 *
 *           PreprocessingInfo* comment = SageInterface::attachComment(source_file, content, directive_type, position);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn PreprocessingInfo* SageInterface::attachComment(SgLocatedNode* target, const string& content, PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType position = PreprocessingInfo::before, PreprocessingInfo::DirectiveType dtype = PreprocessingInfo::CpreprocessorUnknownDeclaration)
 *
 * @brief Attaches a comment to a given node in the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) with specific formatting based on the target's language and directive type.
 *
 * This function generates a comment string in the correct format for the target's programming language and attaches it to the specified SgLocatedNode. The comment is formatted according to the provided content, position, and directive type.
 *
 * @param target The node in the AST where the comment will be attached. Must not be NULL.
 * @param content The text that will be used as the comment.
 * @param position Specifies the relative position of the comment with respect to the target node (default is before).
 * @param dtype The type of directive for the comment (default is CpreprocessorUnknownDeclaration). If this is set to CpreprocessorUnknownDeclaration, the function will attempt to infer the language type from the target's context.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the newly created PreprocessingInfo object representing the attached comment. Returns NULL on failure.
 *
 * @warning The function may abort if it encounters an unknown programming language or directive type.
 *
 * @pre The target node must not be NULL, and the content string must be valid for the chosen directive type.
 * @post A new PreprocessingInfo object is created and attached to the target node with the correctly formatted comment text. If resetPositionInfo is true, the position information of the newly created PreprocessingInfo object will be set to match the statement.
 *
 * @note This function supports various programming languages, including C, C++, Java, Fortran, Ada, and Jovial, and can handle different directive types such as line declarations, ifdefs, and defines.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgLocatedNode* target = ...; // obtain a valid node in the AST
 *           string content = "This is an example comment.";
 *           PreprocessingInfo::RelativePositionType position = PreprocessingInfo::before;
 *           PreprocessingInfo::DirectiveType dtype = PreprocessingInfo::C_StyleComment;
 *
 *           PreprocessingInfo* comment = SageInterface::attachComment(target, content, position, dtype);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::addTextForUnparser ( SgNode* astNode, string s, AstUnparseAttribute::RelativePositionType inputlocation )
 *
 * @brief Adds text to be unparsed at a given IR node.
 *
 * This function adds the specified text to be unparsed at the provided IR node. 
 * It uses an attribute interface to store the text and its relative position.
 *
 * The function first checks if the IR node is of type SgType, in which case it prints
 * an error message and aborts, as adding text to shared nodes is not intended.
 * Then, it checks if the IR node already has an AstUnparseAttribute. If so, 
 * it adds the new string to the existing attribute at the specified relative position.
 * Otherwise, it creates a new AstUnparseAttribute with the given string and 
 * relative position, and adds it to the IR node.
 *
 * @param astNode The IR node where the text is to be added. Must not be NULL.
 * @param s The text to be added. Must not be empty.
 * @param inputlocation The relative position of the text with respect to the IR node.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the IR node and the AstUnparseAttribute are valid.
 *
 * @pre The IR node must exist and must not be of type SgType.
 * @post The text is added to the IR node at the specified relative position.
 *
 * @note The relative position is significant only when there are multiple strings 
 *       in the attribute. Otherwise, it defaults to e_before.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* astNode = ...; // obtain an IR node
 *           string text = "some text";
 *           AstUnparseAttribute::RelativePositionType position = AstUnparseAttribute::e_before;
 *           SageInterface::addTextForUnparser(astNode, text, position);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::guardNode(SgLocatedNode* target, std::string guard)
 *
 * @brief Adds preprocessor directives to guard a node in the abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * This function adds #if and #endif preprocessor directives around the specified
 * node in the abstract syntax tree. The condition for the #if directive is set
 * to the provided guard string.
 *
 * @param target The SgLocatedNode that needs to be guarded with preprocessor directives.
 *               Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @param guard The condition string used in the #if directive.
 *              Must not be empty.
 *
 * @return None.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the abstract syntax tree by adding new nodes for
 *          the preprocessor directives. It does not check if the node is already guarded.
 *
 * @pre The target node must exist and be a valid SgLocatedNode. The guard string must not be empty.
 *
 * @post The #if and #endif preprocessor directives are added to the abstract syntax tree,
 *       guarding the specified node.
 *
 * @note This function uses the PreprocessingInfo class to create and manage the preprocessor
 *       directives. It also sets the transformation flag on the added comments and CPP directives.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgLocatedNode* node = ...; // some node in the abstract syntax tree
 *           std::string guard = "some_condition";
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           sage.guardNode(node, guard);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn int movePreprocessingInfoToNode(SgNode* lnode)
 * @brief Move preprocessing information from a list of containers to the attached preprocessing info of a given node.
 *
 * This function iterates over a list of containers with preprocessing information and moves certain directives
 * (begin, middle, end) to the attached preprocessing info of a given node. The moved directives are also erased
 * from their original location.
 *
 * @param[in] lnode The node to which the preprocessing information will be moved.
 * @return The number of preprocessing information objects that were moved.
 */

/**
 * @fn int SageInterface::eraseNullPreprocessingInfo(SgLocatedNode* lnode)
 *
 * @brief Erases NULL preprocessing info entries from a given SgLocatedNode.
 *
 * This function iterates over all attached preprocessing info of the input node,
 * identifies and removes any NULL entries. It uses a reverse iterator to erase
 * entries from the end to avoid invalidating iterators during removal.
 *
 * The function returns the number of erased entries.
 *
 * @param lnode The SgLocatedNode for which NULL preprocessing info entries are to be erased.
 *              Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The number of erased NULL preprocessing info entries.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input node by removing attached preprocessing info.
 *
 * @pre The input node must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The input node's attached preprocessing info will have all NULL entries removed.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the abstract syntax tree (AST).
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgLocatedNode* node = ...; // obtain a valid SgLocatedNode instance
 *           int erasedCount = SageInterface::eraseNullPreprocessingInfo(node);
 *           cout << "Erased " << erasedCount << " NULL preprocessing info entries." << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::preOrderCollectPreprocessingInfo(SgNode* current, vector<PreprocessingInfo*>& infoList, int depth)
 *
 * @brief Collects preprocessing information from an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) node and its children in a pre-order traversal.
 *
 * This function recursively traverses the AST starting from the given node, collecting preprocessing information
 * attached to each node. It handles three possible locations for this information: before, inside, and after
 * the current node. Information located before or inside the current node is immediately added to the infoList,
 * while information located after the current node is delayed until its children have been processed.
 *
 * @param current The current AST node being processed.
 * @param infoList A list of preprocessing information objects to be populated by this function.
 * @param depth The current recursion depth, used to track the nesting level of nodes in the AST.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error checking on the input parameters. It is assumed that
 *          the caller has ensured that the current node and infoList are valid.
 *
 * @pre The current node must be a non-null pointer to an SgNode object, and the infoList must be a valid vector of PreprocessingInfo pointers.
 * @post The infoList will contain all preprocessing information attached to the current node and its children,
 *       with the information located before or inside the current node appearing first in the list, followed
 *       by the information located after the current node.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse the AST. It is possible that this could lead to stack overflows for very deeply nested ASTs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root node of the AST
 *           vector<PreprocessingInfo*> infoList;
 *           SageInterface::preOrderCollectPreprocessingInfo(root, infoList, 0);
 *           // infoList now contains all preprocessing information from the AST
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* SageInterface::getBoolType(SgNode* n)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the boolean type for a given node in the source file.
 *
 * This function determines whether the source file is written in C or another language
 * and returns the corresponding boolean type. In C, the boolean type is equivalent to int,
 * whereas in other languages, it is represented by a dedicated bool type.
 *
 * @param n The node for which the boolean type is to be retrieved.
 *          Must not be NULL and should represent a valid node in the source file.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgType object representing the boolean type for the given node.
 *         If the source file is written in C, returns an instance of SgTypeInt; otherwise,
 *         returns an instance of SgTypeBool.
 *
 * @warnings None
 *
 * @preconditions The input node n must not be NULL and should represent a valid node
 *                 in the source file. The source file should have been parsed successfully.
 *
 * @postconditions The returned boolean type is consistent with the language of the source file.
 *
 * @note This function relies on the TransformationSupport class to determine the output language
 *       of the source file and create the corresponding boolean type.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* node = ...; // assume a valid node is obtained
 *           SgType* boolType = SageInterface::getBoolType(node);
 *           if (boolType->isSgTypeInt()) {
 *             cout << "Boolean type in C: int" << endl;
 *           } else if (boolType->isSgTypeBool()) {
 *             cout << "Boolean type in non-C language: bool" << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isStrictIntegerType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given SgType represents a strict integer type.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided SgType corresponds to a
 * strict integer type, which includes short, int, long, and their signed and
 * unsigned variants. It does not consider char, wchar, or bool as strict integer types.
 *
 * The function performs this check by recursively resolving any typedefs until it reaches a base type,
 * then checks the variant of the SgType to determine if it matches one of the recognized integer types.
 *
 * @param t The SgType to be checked. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the SgType represents a strict integer type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle cases where the input SgType is NULL or ill-formed.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType must not be NULL and should be in a valid state for this operation.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the SgType represents a strict integer type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function does not consider char, wchar, or bool as strict integer types, unlike another similar
 *       function named SgType::isIntegerType().
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgProject* project = ...; // Assume a valid SgProject instance
 *            SgNode* node = ...; // Assume a valid SgNode instance representing a variable declaration
 *            SgType* type = node->get_type();
 *
 *            if (isStrictIntegerType(type)) {
 *              cout << "The variable is of a strict integer type." << endl;
 *            } else {
 *              cout << "The variable is not of a strict integer type." << endl;
 *            }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* getFirstVarType(SgVariableDeclaration* decl)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the type of the first variable in a given variable declaration.
 *
 * This function takes a pointer to an SgVariableDeclaration as input and returns
 * a pointer to the SgType of the first variable declared. It relies on the 
 * getFirstVarSym function to obtain the symbol for the first variable, then 
 * retrieves its type using the get_type method.
 *
 * @param decl A pointer to an SgVariableDeclaration instance. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgType of the first variable declared in the input declaration.
 *
 * @warning The caller is responsible for ensuring that the input declaration is valid
 *          and contains at least one variable declaration.
 *
 * @pre The input decl must not be NULL, and it must point to a valid SgVariableDeclaration instance.
 *
 * @post The returned type pointer will not be NULL if the function executes successfully.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the variable
 *       declaration beyond ensuring that the input pointer is non-NULL. It assumes
 *       that the caller has already verified the correctness of the declaration.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgVariableDeclaration* decl = ...; // assume a valid variable declaration
 *           SgType* type = getFirstVarType(decl);
 *           if (type != NULL) {
 *               // process the type of the first variable declared in decl
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isDefaultConstructible(SgType* type)
 *
 * @brief Determines if a given SgType is default constructible.
 *
 * This function checks whether a type can be default constructed, 
 * i.e., whether it has a default constructor or all its non-static data members are default constructible.
 *
 * It recursively traverses the type hierarchy and examines the type's declaration to make this determination.
 *
 * @param type The SgType to check for default constructibility. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the type is default constructible, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function may return incorrect results for certain types (e.g., V_SgArrayType) due to limitations in its implementation.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgType pointer.
 *
 * @post The function returns true if the type has a default constructor or all its non-static data members are default constructible, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse the type hierarchy and may cause stack overflows for deeply nested types.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Initialize myType to some SgType pointer
 *           if (isDefaultConstructible(myType)) {
 *             cout << "The type is default constructible." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "The type is not default constructible." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isCopyConstructible(SgType* type)
 *
 * @brief Determines whether a given type is copy constructible.
 *
 * This function checks if a given SgType object represents a type that can be
 * copied using the copy constructor. It recursively checks the base types of
 * compound types and returns false as soon as it encounters an uncopyable type.
 *
 * @param type The SgType object to check for copy constructibility.
 *
 * @return True if the given type is copy constructible, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function may return incorrect results for certain edge cases
 *          or unsupported types. It is recommended to manually verify the
 *          result in such cases.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType object must be valid and non-null.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to check the copy constructibility
 *       of compound types, which may lead to performance issues for deeply nested
 *       types.
 *
 * @sa isCopyConstructible(SgVariableDeclaration*)
 */

/**
 * @fn bool isAssignable(SgType* type)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given SgType can be assigned to another.
 *
 * This function determines whether an assignment operation between two variables of the same type
 * (or compatible types) is allowed by checking the properties of the given SgType. It considers various
 * cases such as arrays, modifiers, functions, references, and classes, among others.
 *
 * @param type The SgType to be checked for assignability.
 *
 * @return True if the assignment operation is allowed, false otherwise.
 *
 * @details This function uses a switch statement to handle different types of SgType. For each case,
 *          it checks specific properties and returns true or false accordingly. In the case of
 *          V_SgClassType, it checks for user-defined operator= and all non-static data members being assignable.
 *
 * @warnings This function assumes that the input type is valid and correctly formed. If the input type
 *           is invalid or ill-formed, this function may produce incorrect results or crash.
 *
 * @preconditions The input type must be a valid SgType.
 *
 * @postconditions The return value indicates whether the assignment operation is allowed for the given type.
 *
 * @note This function does not handle all possible cases of SgType and may need to be extended in the future
 *       to support additional types.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Initialize myType with a valid SgType
 *           bool isAssignableResult = isAssignable(myType);
 *           if (isAssignableResult) {
 *             std::cout << "Assignment operation allowed." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "Assignment operation not allowed." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isPureVirtualClass(SgType* type, const ClassHierarchyWrapper& classHierarchy)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given SgType represents a pure virtual class.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided SgType corresponds to a C++ 
 * class that has at least one pure virtual function and no concrete implementation 
 * of any of its virtual functions. The check is performed by traversing the class 
 * hierarchy, identifying all superclasses, and then examining their member 
 * functions to ascertain if they are pure virtual or have been implemented.
 *
 * @param type The SgType object representing the class to be checked.
 * @param classHierarchy A ClassHierarchyWrapper object providing information about the class relationships.
 *
 * @return True if the given SgType represents a pure virtual class, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the provided SgType and ClassHierarchyWrapper 
 *          objects are valid and correctly represent the C++ code being analyzed. 
 *          If these assumptions do not hold, the results may be incorrect or undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType object must be non-null and represent a class type. 
 *      The ClassHierarchyWrapper object must also be non-null and provide accurate 
 *      information about the class hierarchy of the input SgType.
 *
 * @post If the function returns true, it implies that the given SgType represents a pure virtual class; 
 *       otherwise (i.e., false), it indicates that the class is not pure virtual.
 *
 * @note This function relies on the ROSE compiler infrastructure and its internal representations. 
 *       Its usage might be limited to specific contexts or environments where these 
 *       dependencies are met.
 *
 * @example The following example illustrates how this function can be used:
 *          @code
 *           // Assuming we have an SgType object 'type' representing a class and 
 *           // a ClassHierarchyWrapper object 'classHierarchy'
 *           bool isPureVirtual = isPureVirtualClass(type, classHierarchy);
 *           if (isPureVirtual) {
 *               cout << "The class represented by 'type' is pure virtual." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *               cout << "The class represented by 'type' is not pure virtual." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool hasTrivialDestructor(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type has a trivial (built-in) destructor.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided SgType object represents
 * a type that does not require explicit destruction or deallocation. It uses
 * a combination of switch statements and recursive calls to inspect the type's
 * properties, including its variant type, base types, and declarations.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object representing the type to be checked for trivial destructor.
 * 
 * @return True if the given type has a trivial destructor; false otherwise.
 *
 * @details This function employs a recursive approach to handle complex types,
 *          such as arrays, modifiers, and typedefs. For class types, it checks
 *          whether a user-defined destructor is present in the class definition.
 *
 * @warnings The result may be incorrect if the input type is not properly initialized or represents an invalid state.
 *
 * @preconditions The input SgType object must be valid and represent a well-formed type.
 *
 * @postconditions None.
 *
 * @note This function does not modify the input type or any related data structures.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgProject* project = ...; // assume a valid ROSE project is loaded
 *           SgType* type = project->getTypeFromString("int"); // get the SgType object for 'int'
 *           if (hasTrivialDestructor(type)) {
 *             printf("The type '%s' has a trivial destructor.\n", type->getName().c_str());
 *           } else {
 *             printf("The type '%s' does not have a trivial destructor.\n", type->getName().c_str());
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isNonconstReference(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is a non-constant reference type.
 *
 * This function recursively checks the properties of a given SgType to determine
 * whether it represents a non-constant reference type. It handles various cases,
 * including SgReferenceType, SgTypedefType, and SgModifierType.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked for being a non-constant reference type.
 *
 * @return True if the given type is a non-constant reference type; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not check for null pointer inputs. It assumes that
 *          the input SgType object is valid and properly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType object must be valid and properly initialized.
 *
 * @post None.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive checks to handle nested type definitions.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            // Create a sample SgReferenceType object
 *            SgReferenceType* ref_type = new SgReferenceType();
 *            
 *            // Check if the reference type is non-constant
 *            bool result = isNonconstReference(ref_type);
 *            
 *            // Print the result
 *            std::cout << "Is non-constant reference: " << (result ? "true" : "false") << std::endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isReferenceType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Determines whether a given type is a constant or non-constant reference type.
 *
 * This function checks if the provided SgType object represents a reference type, 
 * either directly or indirectly through typedefs or modifiers. It recursively 
 * traverses the type hierarchy to determine this information.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked for being a reference type.
 *
 * @return True if the type is a constant or non-constant reference type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the input 
 *          SgType pointer. It assumes that the provided pointer is valid and points 
 *          to a properly initialized SgType object.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType pointer must be non-null and point to a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @post The function does not modify the state of the input SgType object or any other objects.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive calls to traverse the type hierarchy. 
 *       It may cause stack overflow for very deeply nested types.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Initialize myType with some valid SgType object
 *           if (isReferenceType(myType)) {
 *             std::cout << "The type is a reference type." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "The type is not a reference type." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isPointerType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Determines whether a given type is a pointer type.
 *
 * This function checks if a given SgType object represents a pointer type. It 
 * recursively traverses the type hierarchy to determine if the input type is 
 * ultimately a pointer type.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked for being a pointer type.
 *
 * @return True if the input type is a pointer type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not check for null pointers. It assumes that the 
 *          input SgType object is valid and non-null.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType object must be a valid and non-null pointer to an 
 *      SgType object.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the input type is a pointer type, false 
 *       otherwise. No side effects are guaranteed.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive traversal of the type hierarchy to make its 
 *       determination. It may not be suitable for very deeply nested types due 
 *       to potential stack overflow risks.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgPointerType* ptrType = ...; // some pointer type
 *           if (isPointerType(ptrType)) {
 *             std::cout << "The type is a pointer type." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "The type is not a pointer type." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isPointerToNonConstType(SgType* type)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given SgType represents a pointer to a non-const type.
 *
 * This function recursively checks the base type of a given SgType to determine
 * if it represents a pointer to a non-const type. It handles various types such as
 * SgTypedefType, SgPointerType, and SgModifierType.
 *
 * @param type The SgType to check for being a pointer to a non-const type.
 *
 * @return True if the given SgType represents a pointer to a non-const type,
 *         false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the
 *          input SgType. It assumes that the input is a valid SgType.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType must be a valid instance of an SgType subclass.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the given SgType represents a pointer to a non-const type.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive calls to check the base types of complex
 *       SgTypes. It may cause a stack overflow for very deeply nested types.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgPointerType* pointerType = ...; // obtain an SgPointerType instance
 *           if (isPointerToNonConstType(pointerType)) {
 *               std::cout << "The type is a pointer to a non-const type." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The type is not a pointer to a non-const type." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isConstType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is const-qualified.
 *
 * This function recursively checks if a given SgType object represents a
 * const-qualified type. It handles various cases, including typedefs and 
 * modifier types, to determine the presence of const qualification.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked for const qualification. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the given type is const-qualified, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle reference types explicitly, as they
 *          are usually not cv-qualified. However, it may still work correctly 
 *          in such cases due to its recursive nature.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType object t must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the given type is const-qualified.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to handle nested types and
 *       modifiers. It may cause stack overflows for very deeply nested types.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* type = ...; // assume some SgType object is created or obtained
 *           if (isConstType(type)) {
 *             std::cout << "The type is const-qualified." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "The type is not const-qualified." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* removeConst(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Removes a top-level const from the type, if present.
 *
 * This function takes an input of type SgType* and returns the same type with any
 * top-level const modifier removed. It does this by recursively checking for
 * typedef types and removing the const modifier from any found.
 *
 * The implementation uses a combination of ROSE's built-in AST traversal functions
 * and custom logic to identify and remove the const modifier.
 *
 * @param t The input type from which the top-level const is to be removed.
 *
 * @return The modified type with any top-level const removed. If no const was present,
 *         or if the input type was not a valid candidate for const removal, the original
 *         type is returned unchanged.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input type is a valid ROSE AST node and does
 *          not perform any additional error checking on this assumption. If an invalid
 *          type is passed in, undefined behavior may result.
 *
 * @pre The input type t must be a non-null pointer to a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @post The returned type will have the same underlying structure as the input type,
 *       but with any top-level const modifier removed. If no such modifier was present,
 *       the original type is returned unchanged.
 *
 * @note This function does not modify the original AST, but instead creates a new copy
 *       of the modified type and returns that.
 *
 * @example Here's an example usage of this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* inputType = ...; // assume some valid ROSE AST node
 *           SgType* outputType = removeConst(inputType);
 *           if (outputType != inputType) {
 *             cout << "Const removed successfully!" << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "No const present, or removal failed." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isVolatileType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is volatile.
 *
 * This function recursively checks if a given SgType is volatile. It handles
 * typedef types by checking their base type and modifier types by examining
 * the const-volatile modifier.
 *
 * @param t The SgType to be checked for volatility. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the type is volatile, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not check for other modifiers that might affect
 *          the volatility of a type, such as const or restrict.
 *
 * @precondition t must be a valid SgType pointer.
 *
 * @postcondition The return value indicates whether the type is volatile or not.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive calls to handle nested typedefs and modifiers.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgTypedefType* typedefType = ...; // Initialize with some SgTypedefType
 *           bool isVolatile = isVolatileType(typedefType);
 *           if (isVolatile) {
 *             // Handle volatile type
 *           } else {
 *             // Handle non-volatile type
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isRestrictType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is restrict.
 *
 * This function recursively checks if a given SgType is restrict. It handles
 * SgTypedefType and SgModifierType specifically, as they can contain nested types.
 *
 * @param t The SgType to check for the restrict property.
 *
 * @return True if the type is restrict, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle all possible SgType subclasses. It only
 *          checks SgTypedefType and SgModifierType specifically. Other types are not
 *          considered.
 *
 * @pre The input parameter t must be a valid SgType pointer.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the type is restrict or not.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive calls to handle nested types. It may cause a
 *       stack overflow if the type hierarchy is too deep.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Initialize myType
 *           bool isRestrict = isRestrictType(myType);
 *           if (isRestrict) {
 *             std::cout << "The type is restrict." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "The type is not restrict." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isScalarType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is a scalar type.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given SgType object represents a scalar
 * type, which includes character types (char, signed char, unsigned char),
 * integer types (short, int, long), floating point types (float, double,
 * long double), and other specific types such as void, wchar_t, char16_t,
 * char32_t, complex, imaginary, string, and bool.
 *
 * The function first strips off any typedef chains and modifiers from the
 * input type. It then checks the variant of the stripped type to determine if it
 * is a scalar type.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the input type represents a scalar type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type has already been validated
 *          and is a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @pre t != NULL
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the input type is a scalar type or not.
 *
 * @note This function does not check for pointer types, reference types, array
 *       types, or other aggregate types. It only checks for basic scalar types.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* t = ...; // assume some valid SgType object
 *           if (isScalarType(t)) {
 *             cout << "The type is a scalar type." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "The type is not a scalar type." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isStructType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Determines whether a given SgType represents a struct type.
 *
 * This function checks if the provided SgType is an instance of SgClassType and
 * then verifies if its declaration statement corresponds to a struct declaration.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the SgType represents a struct type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgType is valid and correctly
 *          initialized. Passing an invalid or NULL SgType may result in undefined
 *          behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the input SgType represents a struct type.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to ensure that the input SgType is
 *       not NULL. If this assertion fails, the program will terminate.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an example SgClassType representing a struct
 *           SgClassType* class_type = ...;
 *           
 *           // Check if the class type represents a struct
 *           bool is_struct = isStructType(class_type);
 *           
 *           // Print the result
 *           std::cout << "Is struct: " << (is_struct ? "true" : "false") << std::endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn string mangleType(SgType* type)
 *
 * @brief Generates a mangled string representation of the given SgType.
 *
 * This function computes a unique string representation for a given SgType,
 * which can be used for various purposes such as debugging, serialization,
 * or caching. The mangling algorithm is based on the structure and properties
 * of the input type, including its kind (scalar, pointer, array, etc.), modifiers,
 * and base types.
 *
 * @param type The SgType instance to be mangled into a string representation.
 *             Must not be NULL or ill-formed.
 *
 * @return A unique string representation of the input SgType. If the type is
 *         unknown or cannot be mangled, returns "*Unhandled*".
 *
 * @warning This function uses a cache (type_string_map) to store previously
 *          computed manglings for efficiency. However, this cache is not
 *          thread-safe and may lead to incorrect results in multi-threaded
 *          environments.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType instance must be valid and properly initialized.
 *
 * @post The returned string representation is unique for the given SgType,
 *       unless an error occurs or the type is unknown.
 *
 * @note This function does not support rvalue references yet, as they are not
 *       supported in EDG/ROSE/GCC. Additionally, some types (e.g., function-type,
 *       class-enum-type, pointer-to-member-type, template-param) are not handled
 *       and will return "*Unhandled*".
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* type = ...; // obtain an SgType instance
 *           string mangled_type = mangleType(type);
 *           cout << "Mangled type: " << mangled_type << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn string mangleScalarType(SgType* type)
 *
 * @brief Mangles a scalar type into a string.
 *
 * This function takes a scalar type as input and returns a string representing
 * the mangled type. The mangling is based on the Itanium ABI specification.
 *
 * @param type The scalar type to be mangled. Must be a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @return A string representing the mangled scalar type.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is a scalar type and does
 *          not perform any checks for this condition.
 *
 * @preconditions The input type must be a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @postconditions The returned string represents the mangled scalar type.
 *
 * @note This function uses a switch statement to determine the correct mangling
 *       based on the variant of the input type. If the input type is not recognized,
 *       the function returns an error message.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* type = ...; // obtain a scalar type
 *           string mangled_type = mangleScalarType(type);
 *           cout << "Mangled type: " << mangled_type << endl;
 *          @endcode
 *
 * @see Itanium ABI specification for more information on the mangling scheme.
 */
/**
 * @fn string mangleModifierType(SgModifierType* type)
 *
 * @brief Mangles a modifier type into a string representation.
 *
 * This function takes an SgModifierType pointer as input and generates a unique
 * string representation of the modifier type. The resulting string is used to
 * identify the type in a mangled name.
 *
 * The string representation includes information about const, volatile,
 * restrict, UPC shared, and UPC strict shared modifiers. If the input type is
 * UPC shared or UPC strict shared, the block size is also included in the
 * string representation.
 *
 * @param type A pointer to an SgModifierType object representing the modifier type to be mangled.
 *
 * @return A string representation of the input modifier type.
 *
 * @warning This function does not check if the input type is valid or well-formed.
 *          It assumes that the input type has been properly initialized and
 *          validated before calling this function.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgModifierType object.
 *
 * @post The returned string representation is unique for each modifier type.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to mangle the base type of the
 *       input modifier type. If the input type has a complex inheritance hierarchy,
 *       this function may overflow the stack or cause performance issues.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgModifierType* type = ...; // initialize the modifier type
 *           string mangledName = mangleModifierType(type);
 *           cout << "Mangled name: " << mangledName << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn size_t getArrayElementCount(SgArrayType* t)
 *
 * @brief Calculates the number of elements in an array type.
 *
 * This function computes the total number of elements in a given array type,
 * taking into account both single and multi-dimensional arrays. It also handles
 * UPC (Unified Parallel C) arrays with dimensions specified using THREADS.
 * The calculation is based on the index expression associated with the array type.
 *
 * @param t A pointer to an SgArrayType object representing the array type for which the element count is to be calculated.
 *          Must not be NULL. If the input array type is invalid, the function may produce incorrect results or crash.
 *
 * @return The total number of elements in the array type. Returns 1 if the index expression is NULL or a null expression, as this implies a default dimension of 1.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the input array type beyond ensuring it is non-NULL. It assumes that the input array type has been previously validated.
 *
 * @pre The input array type must be a valid SgArrayType object, and its index expression must be either NULL, a null expression, or an SgUnsignedLongVal or SgIntVal expression.
 *
 * @post The returned element count is guaranteed to be non-negative. However, it may overflow the size_t data type for very large arrays.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursion to handle multi-dimensional arrays.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgArrayType* arrayType = ...; // Initialize arrayType to a valid SgArrayType object
 *           size_t elementCount = getArrayElementCount(arrayType);
 *           cout << "Number of elements in the array: " << elementCount << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn int getDimensionCount(SgType* mytype)
 *
 * @brief Calculates the number of dimensions of an array type.
 *
 * This function determines the dimension count of a given SgType by recursively
 * stripping away modifiers, typedefs, and references until it reaches the base type.
 * The dimension count is incremented for each array type encountered during this process.
 *
 * @param mytype A pointer to the SgType object representing the array type. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The number of dimensions in the input array type.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgType is valid and correctly formed.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned dimension count is non-negative.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to strip away modifiers, typedefs, and references.
 *       It also handles the special case where the base type is a string.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* mytype = ...; // Initialize mytype with an array type
 *           int dimension_count = getDimensionCount(mytype);
 *           printf("Number of dimensions: %d\n", dimension_count);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* getArrayElementType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the element type of an array type.
 *
 * This function takes a type as input and recursively strips away any 
 * modifiers, typedefs, and references to obtain the base type. If the 
 * resulting type is an array type, it continues to strip away the outermost 
 * array dimension until a non-array type is reached. The function then 
 * returns this non-array type as the element type of the original input type.
 *
 * @param t The input type for which the element type is to be retrieved.
 *          Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The element type of the input array type, or the input type itself 
 *         if it is not an array type. If the input type is a string type, returns 
 *         a char type instead.
 *
 * @warning If the input type is an array type and its base type is also an array 
 *          type, this function will return the innermost non-array type. However, 
 *          if the input type itself is not an array type but contains nested array 
 *          types, the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgType object and must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned element type will always be a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to strip away array dimensions. 
 *       If the input type has a very large number of nested array dimensions, 
 *       this function may cause a stack overflow.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* elementType = getArrayElementType(someArrayType);
 *           if (elementType != NULL) {
 *             // Use the element type for further processing...
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* getElementType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the element type of a given type.
 *
 * This function takes a pointer to an SgType object as input, strips away any
 * modifiers, typedefs, and references, and returns a pointer to the underlying
 * element type. The element type is determined based on the variant type of the
 * input SgType object.
 *
 * @param t A pointer to an SgType object representing the type for which the
 *          element type is to be retrieved. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgType object representing the element type, or NULL
 *         if the input type does not have a determinable element type (e.g., a
 *         primitive type).
 *
 * @warning The caller is responsible for ensuring that the returned SgType
 *           object remains valid for as long as it is needed.
 *
 * @pre t must be a non-NULL pointer to an SgType object.
 *
 * @post If the input type has a determinable element type, the returned SgType
 *        object represents that element type; otherwise, NULL is returned.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any checks for overflow or underflow.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an SgPointerType object representing a pointer to int
 *           SgPointerType* ptrType = new SgPointerType(SgTypeInt::createType());
 *
 *           // Get the element type of the pointer type (i.e., int)
 *           SgType* elementType = getElementType(ptrType);
 *
 *           // Print the name of the element type
 *           printf("%s\n", elementType->get_name().c_str());
 *
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn std::pair<int, int> SageInterface::get_C_array_dimensions_aux(const SgArrayType& arrtype)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the dimensions of a C-style array.
 *
 * This function calculates the dimensions of a given C-style array type. It
 * traverses the array type to determine its rank (number of dimensions) and size
 * of each dimension.
 *
 * The implementation uses a recursive approach to handle arrays with multiple
 * dimensions.
 *
 * @param arrtype The SgArrayType object representing the C-style array.
 *
 * @return A pair containing the number of dimensions in the array and the size
 *         of each dimension. If the input array type is not valid, the function
 *         returns an empty pair.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input array type is well-formed and
 *          does not contain any syntax errors.
 *
 * @pre The input array type must be a valid SgArrayType object.
 *
 * @post The returned pair contains the correct dimensions of the input array
 *       type.
 *
 * @note This function is an auxiliary function used by get_C_array_dimensions.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgArrayType arrtype = ...; // Initialize arrtype with a valid C-style array
 *           std::pair<int, int> dimensions = SageInterface::get_C_array_dimensions_aux(arrtype);
 *           if (!dimensions.empty()) {
 *             cout << "Array has " << dimensions.first << " dimensions." << endl;
 *             cout << "Size of each dimension: ";
 *             for (int i = 0; i < dimensions.second; ++i) {
 *               cout << arrtype.get_dimension(i).get_value() << " ";
 *             }
 *             cout << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cerr << "Invalid array type." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::get_C_array_dimensions(const SgArrayType& arrtype, const SgVarRefExp& varref)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the dimensions of a C-style array.
 *
 * This function computes the dimensions of a given C-style array using 
 * auxiliary functions. The dimensions are extracted from the array type and 
 * variable reference expression.
 *
 * @param arrtype The array type for which the dimensions are to be calculated.
 *                Must be a valid SgArrayType object.
 * @param varref  The variable reference expression associated with the array.
 *                Must be a valid SgVarRefExp object.
 *
 * @return A collection of dimensions (not explicitly defined in this context).
 *
 * @warning This function may throw exceptions if the input parameters are invalid.
 *
 * @pre The input parameters arrtype and varref must be valid objects.
 *
 * @post The returned dimensions will be a collection of values representing 
 *       the size of each dimension in the array.
 *
 * @note This function is part of the SageInterface class, which provides an interface
 *       to the SAGE compiler's internal data structures. The implementation details
 *       are specific to the SAGE compiler and may not be applicable to other compilers.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create a sample array type and variable reference expression
 *           SgArrayType arrtype = ...;
 *           SgVarRefExp varref = ...;
 *
 *           // Get the dimensions of the array
 *           auto dimensions = SageInterface::get_C_array_dimensions(arrtype, varref);
 *
 *           // Process the dimensions (e.g., print them)
 *           for (auto dim : dimensions) {
 *               std::cout << dim << " ";
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::get_C_array_dimensions(const SgArrayType& arrtype, SgInitializedName& initname)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the dimensions of a C-style array from an ArrayType node.
 *
 * This function takes an ArrayType node and an InitializedName node as input,
 * and returns the dimensions of the corresponding C-style array. The function
 * uses an auxiliary function get_C_array_dimensions_aux to perform the actual
 * computation, passing the varrefCreator result for the initname parameter.
 *
 * @param arrtype The ArrayType node representing the C-style array.
 * @param initname The InitializedName node associated with the array.
 *
 * @return A vector of integers representing the dimensions of the C-style array.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input nodes are valid and correctly
 *          represent a C-style array. If the input nodes are invalid or malformed,
 *          the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The arrtype node must be a valid ArrayType node, and the initname node must
 *      be a valid InitializedName node associated with the array.
 *
 * @post The returned vector contains the correct dimensions of the C-style array.
 *
 * @note This function uses an auxiliary function get_C_array_dimensions_aux to
 *       perform the actual computation. The varrefCreator result for the initname
 *       parameter is passed to this auxiliary function.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgArrayType* arrtype = ...; // obtain ArrayType node
 *           SgInitializedName* initname = ...; // obtain InitializedName node
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           std::vector<int> dimensions = sage.get_C_array_dimensions(*arrtype, *initname);
 *           // use the dimensions vector as needed
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isArrayReference(SgExpression* ref, SgExpression** arrayName/*=NULL*/, vector<SgExpression*>** subscripts/*=NULL*/)
 *
 * @brief Checks if an expression is an array access (SgPntrArrRefExp) and returns its name and subscripts if requested.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given expression represents an array access. If it does, the function can optionally return the name of the array being accessed and the subscripts used to index into the array.
 *
 * The function uses a recursive approach to handle cases where the array access is nested inside other expressions.
 *
 * @param ref The expression to be checked for array access.
 * @param arrayName A pointer to an SgExpression that will hold the name of the array being accessed, if requested. Defaults to NULL.
 * @param subscripts A pointer to a vector of SgExpressions that will hold the subscripts used to index into the array, if requested. Defaults to NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the expression represents an array access, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning If the input expression is not an array access, the function returns false and does not modify the output parameters.
 *
 * @pre The input expression must be a valid SgExpression object.
 *
 * @post If the function returns true, the output parameters will be modified to hold the name of the array being accessed and/or the subscripts used to index into the array, depending on which parameters were requested.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to handle nested array accesses. It also checks for cases where the input expression is not an array access and returns false in such cases.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* ref = ...; // some expression
 *           SgExpression* arrayName;
 *           vector<SgExpression*>* subscripts;
 *           if (isArrayReference(ref, &arrayName, &subscripts)) {
 *             cout << "Array name: " << arrayName->unparseToString() << endl;
 *             for (auto& subscript : *subscripts) {
 *               cout << "Subscript: " << subscript->unparseToString() << endl;
 *             }
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "Not an array access." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool hasUpcSharedType(SgType* sg_type,SgModifierType ** mod_type_out/*=NULL*/)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given SgType has a UPC shared type.
 *
 * This function recursively traverses the type hierarchy to determine if a 
 * given SgType has a UPC shared type. It checks for modifier types, pointer types,
 * reference types, array types, and typedef types.
 *
 * @param sg_type The SgType to be checked for a UPC shared type.
 *                Must not be NULL.
 * @param mod_type_out A pointer to store the found SgModifierType if any. 
 *                     Defaults to NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the SgType has a UPC shared type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function performs recursive calls and may cause stack overflow
 *          for deeply nested types.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post If the return value is true, mod_type_out will point to the found 
 *       SgModifierType if it was provided. Otherwise, it will be set to NULL.
 *
 * @note This function uses ROSE_ASSERT to ensure that the input SgType is valid.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* type = ...; // assume some SgType instance
 *           SgModifierType* mod_type;
 *           if (hasUpcSharedType(type, &mod_type)) {
 *             // type has a UPC shared type, mod_type points to it
 *           } else {
 *             // type does not have a UPC shared type
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isUpcSharedType(SgType* t, SgModifierType ** mod_type_out)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a type is a UPC shared type.
 *
 * This function determines whether the input type represents a UPC shared type,
 * including shared arrays and shared pointers. It uses the hasUpcSharedType()
 * function to check for shared types and also verifies that the type is not
 * private to shared using the isUpcPrivateToSharedType() function.
 *
 * @param t The type to be checked for being a UPC shared type.
 * @param mod_type_out An optional output parameter to store the modifier type if the input type is found to be a UPC shared type. Defaults to NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the input type represents a UPC shared type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input type is valid and does not perform any error checking on it.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be non-NULL.
 *
 * @post If the return value is true, then the modifier type stored in mod_type_out (if provided) will represent the UPC shared type of the input type t.
 *
 * @note This function uses the hasUpcSharedType() and isUpcPrivateToSharedType() functions internally to perform the necessary checks. These functions are not shown here for brevity.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* my_type = ...; // assume some valid type
 *           SgModifierType* mod_type;
 *           if (isUpcSharedType(my_type, &mod_type)) {
 *               printf("Type %s is a UPC shared type with modifier %s\n", my_type->unparseToString().c_str(), mod_type->unparseToString().c_str());
 *           } else {
 *               printf("Type %s is not a UPC shared type\n", my_type->unparseToString().c_str());
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isUpcSharedModifierType(SgModifierType* mod_type)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given modifier type represents an UPC shared modifier.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided SgModifierType object
 * corresponds to an UPC shared modifier. It does this by examining the
 * upcModifier field of the typeModifier within the input SgModifierType object.
 *
 * The function relies on the ROSE_ASSERT macro to ensure that a valid,
 * non-null pointer to an SgModifierType object is passed as an argument.
 *
 * @param mod_type A pointer to an SgModifierType object. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the input modifier type represents an UPC shared modifier;
 *         false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any additional checks on the
 *          input modifier type beyond verifying that it is non-null and
 *          examining its upcModifier field. It assumes that the input object
 *          has been properly initialized and configured prior to being passed
 *          to this function.
 *
 * @pre The input mod_type must not be NULL, as verified by ROSE_ASSERT.
 *
 * @post None; this is a read-only inspection function that does not modify its
 *        input or have any side effects.
 *
 * @note This function is specific to UPC (Unified Parallel C) and may not be
 *       applicable or relevant in other contexts.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgModifierType* mod_type = ...; // assume some valid initialization
 *           if (isUpcSharedModifierType(mod_type)) {
 *             // handle the case where mod_type represents an UPC shared modifier
 *           } else {
 *             // handle the case where mod_type does not represent an UPC shared modifier
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isUpcSharedArrayType(SgArrayType* array_type)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given SgArrayType is of UPC shared array type.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided SgArrayType represents a UPC (Unified Parallel C) shared array type. 
 * It checks the base type of the array to see if it's a modifier type and then verifies if this modifier type is indeed a UPC shared type.
 *
 * @param array_type The SgArrayType object to be checked for being a UPC shared array type. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the array type is a UPC shared array type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input array_type is valid and properly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input array_type must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The return value will indicate whether the array type is of UPC shared type or not.
 *
 * @note This function relies on the implementation details of SgArrayType and SgModifierType, which might change in future versions of ROSE.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Assuming 'array_type' is a valid SgArrayType object
 *           if (isUpcSharedArrayType(array_type)) {
 *             std::cout << "The array type is UPC shared." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "The array type is not UPC shared." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isUpcStrictSharedModifierType(SgModifierType* mod_type)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given modifier type represents a UPC strict shared type.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided SgModifierType object
 * corresponds to a UPC strict shared type. The UPC (Unified Parallel C) programming
 * model is designed for high-performance computing on large-scale parallel machines.
 *
 * A strict shared variable in UPC is a shared variable that can be accessed by all threads,
 * but its value is always consistent across all threads, i.e., changes made by one thread are
 * immediately visible to all other threads.
 *
 * This function relies on the ROSE (Robust, Open, Scalable, and Extensible) compiler infrastructure
 * for its implementation. It first asserts that the input modifier type represents a UPC shared
 * type using the isUpcSharedModifierType() function. Then it checks whether this shared type is strict.
 *
 * @param mod_type A pointer to an SgModifierType object representing the modifier type to be checked.
 *                 Must not be NULL and must represent a UPC shared type, as asserted by the function.
 *
 * @return True if the input modifier type represents a UPC strict shared type; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any additional checks on the input parameter beyond
 *          asserting that it represents a UPC shared type. It is the caller's responsibility to ensure
 *          that the input parameter is valid and correctly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input modifier type must represent a UPC shared type, as asserted by the function.
 *
 * @post None.
 *
 * @note This function is part of the ROSE compiler infrastructure and is intended for use within
 *       the context of this infrastructure. Its behavior may not be well-defined or predictable when
 *       used outside of this context.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgModifierType* mod_type = ...; // assume this is a valid UPC shared modifier type
 *           if (isUpcStrictSharedModifierType(mod_type)) {
 *               cout << "The modifier type represents a UPC strict shared type." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *               cout << "The modifier type does not represent a UPC strict shared type." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn size_t getUpcSharedBlockSize(SgModifierType* mod_type)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the shared block size for UPC (Unified Parallel C) modifier types.
 *
 * This function determines the shared block size specified by a given UPC modifier type.
 * It first checks if the provided modifier type is indeed of UPC shared type using the ROSE_ASSERT macro.
 * If the assertion passes, it retrieves the layout value from the modifier type's UPC attributes. 
 * A return value of -1 indicates an unspecified block size, which is treated as a block size of 1 for consistency.
 *
 * @param mod_type The SgModifierType object containing the UPC shared modifier information.
 *                 Must be non-null and represent a valid UPC shared modifier type.
 *
 * @return The shared block size specified by the input UPC modifier type. Returns 1 if the block size is unspecified (-1).
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the provided SgModifierType object has been properly initialized and represents a valid UPC shared modifier.
 *
 * @pre The input SgModifierType pointer must not be null, and it must represent a valid UPC shared modifier type as verified by ROSE_ASSERT.
 *
 * @post The returned value is either the explicitly specified block size or 1 if the block size was unspecified (-1).
 *
 * @note This function relies on the internal representation of SgModifierType objects for UPC shared modifiers. 
 *       Any changes to this representation may affect the correctness of this function.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an instance of SgModifierType representing a UPC shared modifier
 *           SgModifierType* upc_shared_mod_type = ...; 
 *
 *           // Retrieve the shared block size using the getUpcSharedBlockSize function
 *           size_t block_size = getUpcSharedBlockSize(upc_shared_mod_type);
 *
 *           // Use the retrieved block size for further processing or output
 *           std::cout << "UPC Shared Block Size: " << block_size << std::endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn size_t getUpcSharedBlockSize(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the block size of a UPC shared type.
 *
 * This function calculates the block size for UPC shared types, including modifier
 * types and arrays of modifier types (shared arrays), based on the input SgType object.
 *
 * The calculation involves determining if the input type is a UPC shared type using
 * the @ref isUpcSharedType function. If it is, the function then calls itself recursively
 * with the SgModifierType object to obtain the block size. 
 *
 * @param t A pointer to an SgType object representing the UPC shared type.
 *
 * @return The calculated block size for the input UPC shared type.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgType object represents a valid
 *          UPC shared type. If this is not the case, undefined behavior may result.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType object must represent a valid UPC shared type.
 *
 * @post The returned block size will be greater than or equal to 0.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive calls to calculate the block size for modifier
 *       types and arrays of modifier types. 
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* type = ...; // Initialize a UPC shared type object
 *           size_t blockSize = getUpcSharedBlockSize(type);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(blockSize >= 0);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isUpcPhaseLessSharedType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is an UPC phase-less shared type.
 *
 * This function determines whether the input type represents a UPC (Unified Parallel C) phase-less shared type. 
 * A phase-less shared type in UPC means that the block size of the shared array is either 1, 0, or unspecified (-1).
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked.
 *          Must not be NULL and should represent a valid type in the UPC context.
 *
 * @return True if the input type represents an UPC phase-less shared type; false otherwise.
 *
 * @details This function first checks if the input type has an associated UPC shared type modifier. 
 * If this check fails, it immediately returns false. Otherwise, it extracts the block size from the UPC shared type modifier and checks whether it is 1, 0, or -1. 
 * If any of these conditions are met, the function returns true; otherwise, it returns false.
 *
 * @warnings This function assumes that the input SgType object has already been validated in terms of its syntax and semantics. 
 * It does not perform any additional checks for the correctness of the input type.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType object must be valid and represent a UPC shared type.
 *
 * @post If the function returns true, it guarantees that the input type represents an UPC phase-less shared type.
 *
 * @note This function is specific to the UPC programming model and may not work correctly for other parallel programming models.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* my_type = ...; // assume a valid UPC shared type object
 *           if (isUpcPhaseLessSharedType(my_type)) {
 *             cout << "The type is an UPC phase-less shared type." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "The type is not an UPC phase-less shared type." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isUpcPrivateToSharedType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type represents a UPC private-to-shared type.
 *
 * This function determines whether the input SgType represents a UPC 
 * (Unified Parallel C) private-to-shared type by examining its structure. It 
 * checks for the presence of an SgUPC_AccessModifier and verifies that it appears 
 * after any SgPointerType in the type's hierarchy.
 *
 * @param t The SgType to check for being a UPC private-to-shared type.
 *
 * @return True if the input type represents a UPC private-to-shared type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input type has already been validated as 
 *       having an SgUPC_AccessModifier. If not, it will return false immediately.
 *
 * @warning The function does not perform any additional validation on the input type.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgType.
 *
 * @post None.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an SgType representing a UPC private-to-shared type
 *           SgType* upcType = ...;
 *           
 *           // Check if the type is a UPC private-to-shared type
 *           bool isUpcPrivateToShared = isUpcPrivateToSharedType(upcType);
 *           
 *           // Print the result
 *           std::cout << "Is UPC private-to-shared type: " << (isUpcPrivateToShared ? "true" : "false") << std::endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool isUpcArrayWithThreads(SgArrayType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if an array type has UPC threads.
 *
 * This function examines the given array type to determine whether it represents
 * a UPC (Unified Parallel C) array with threads. It does this by checking the
 * array's index expression for the presence of a multiplication operation involving
 * a UPC thread variable or an integer value, which is indicative of static compilation
 * of UPC threads.
 *
 * @param t The SgArrayType object to be examined. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the array type represents a UPC array with threads; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input array type is valid and has an index expression.
 *          If this is not the case, undefined behavior may result.
 *
 * @pre t != NULL
 * @post The return value will be true if the array type represents a UPC array with threads; false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to verify that the input parameter is non-NULL.
 *       If this assertion fails, an error message will be printed and the program may terminate unexpectedly.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgArrayType* arrayType = ...; // Initialize array type object
 *           if (isUpcArrayWithThreads(arrayType)) {
 *              cout << "Array has UPC threads." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *              cout << "Array does not have UPC threads." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* SageInterface::lookupNamedTypeInParentScopes(const std::string& type_name, SgScopeStatement* scope/*=NULL*/)
 *
 * @brief Looks up a named type in the parent scopes of the given scope.
 *
 * This function searches for a named type in the parent scopes of the provided scope,
 * starting from the topmost scope. If no scope is provided, it defaults to the top scope
 * on the stack.
 *
 * The search is performed by looking up the symbol with the given type name in the
 * parent scopes. If the symbol is found and it has a type associated with it, that type
 * is returned. Otherwise, NULL is returned.
 *
 * @param type_name The name of the type to look up.
 * @param scope     The scope in which to start looking for the type. Defaults to the top scope on the stack if not provided.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the looked-up type, or NULL if no such type was found.
 *
 * @warning If the input scope is NULL and there are no scopes on the stack, this function will return NULL.
 *          Also, be aware that this function performs a recursive search, which may lead to performance issues
 *          for very deep scope hierarchies.
 *
 * @pre The input type name must not be empty. The input scope must be either NULL or a valid SgScopeStatement pointer.
 *
 * @post If the looked-up type is found, its associated symbol will have been accessed and may have side effects.
 *
 * @note This function uses the lookupSymbolInParentScopes function to perform the actual search.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = SageBuilder::topScopeStack();
 *           SgType* type = SageInterface::lookupNamedTypeInParentScopes("MyType", scope);
 *           if (type != NULL) {
 *               // Use the looked-up type...
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle the case where no such type was found...
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* getAssociatedTypeFromFunctionTypeList(SgExpression* actual_argument_expression)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the type of a formal function parameter associated with an actual argument expression.
 *
 * This function takes an actual argument expression and returns the corresponding type
 * from the list of formal function parameters. It traverses up the abstract syntax tree (AST) to find
 * the parent expression list, then iterates through it to determine the index of the input expression,
 * which is used to access the associated type in the function parameter list.
 *
 * @param actual_argument_expression The expression for which the associated formal function parameter type is to be retrieved. Must not be NULL and must be a direct argument of a function call expression.
 *
 * @return The type of the formal function parameter associated with the input actual argument expression, or NULL if an error occurs.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input expression is a direct argument of a function call expression and does not perform additional checks. It also does not handle cases where the function reference is indirect (e.g., through a pointer).
 *
 * @pre The input expression must be non-NULL and must be a direct argument of a function call expression.
 * @post The returned type will be non-NULL if the function executes successfully, or NULL otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses ROSE_ASSERT statements to enforce preconditions and postconditions. It also prints error messages in case of failures.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* actual_argument_expression = ...; // Initialize with a valid expression
 *           SgType* associated_type = getAssociatedTypeFromFunctionTypeList(actual_argument_expression);
 *           if (associated_type != NULL) {
 *             printf("Associated type: %s\n", associated_type->class_name().c_str());
 *           } else {
 *             printf("Error retrieving associated type\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::templateArgumentEquivalence(SgTemplateArgument * arg1, SgTemplateArgument * arg2)
 *
 * @brief Checks if two template arguments are equivalent.
 *
 * This function compares the equivalence of two template arguments based on their types and values. 
 * It handles different cases such as type arguments, non-type arguments, and template-template arguments.
 *
 * The comparison is done recursively for complex types and uses a mechanism to handle the case where
 * the input pointers are different but might still point to the same type or value.
 *
 * @param arg1 The first template argument to be compared.
 * @param arg2 The second template argument to be compared.
 *
 * @return True if the two template arguments are equivalent, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input pointers are not null and the arguments are well-formed.
 *          It does not perform any error checking on the inputs.
 *
 * @pre The input pointers must not be null and the arguments must be well-formed.
 * @post The function returns a boolean value indicating whether the two template arguments are equivalent.
 *
 * @note This function is used internally by the ROSE compiler and should not be called directly by users.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgTemplateArgument* arg1 = ...; // Initialize arg1
 *            SgTemplateArgument* arg2 = ...; // Initialize arg2
 *            bool result = SageInterface::templateArgumentEquivalence(arg1, arg2);
 *            if (result) {
 *              printf("The two template arguments are equivalent.\n");
 *            } else {
 *              printf("The two template arguments are not equivalent.\n");
 *            }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::templateArgumentListEquivalence(const SgTemplateArgumentPtrList & list1, const SgTemplateArgumentPtrList & list2)
 *
 * @brief Checks if two lists of template arguments are equivalent.
 *
 * This function compares the elements of two lists of template arguments and returns true if they are equivalent, false otherwise.
 * The comparison is done by checking if the sizes of the lists are equal, then comparing each pair of corresponding elements using the templateArgumentEquivalence() function.
 *
 * @param list1 The first list of template arguments to compare.
 * @param list2 The second list of template arguments to compare.
 *
 * @return True if the two lists are equivalent, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input lists are valid and properly formed. If the lists are not valid or properly formed, the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input lists must be non-empty and have the same size.
 *
 * @post The function returns a boolean value indicating whether the two lists are equivalent.
 *
 * @note This function uses the templateArgumentEquivalence() function to compare individual template arguments. If that function returns false for any pair of corresponding elements, this function immediately returns false.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgTemplateArgumentPtrList list1 = ...;
 *           SgTemplateArgumentPtrList list2 = ...;
 *           bool areEquivalent = SageInterface::templateArgumentListEquivalence(list1, list2);
 *           if (areEquivalent) {
 *               printf("The two lists are equivalent.\n");
 *           } else {
 *               printf("The two lists are not equivalent.\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */

/**
 * @fn bool isEquivalentType(SgType* X, SgType* Y)  
 * @brief Checks if two types are equivalent.
 *
 * This function checks if two SgType objects represent equivalent types. It takes into account type modifiers,
 * type chains, and other factors that can affect the equivalence of types in C++.
 *
 * The function uses a recursive approach to traverse the type chains of both input types and compare their
 * corresponding elements.
 *
 * @param X The first SgType object to compare.
 * @param Y The second SgType object to compare.
 * @return True if the two types are equivalent, false otherwise.
 */

/**
 * @fn SgFunctionType* SageInterface::findFunctionType (SgType* return_type, SgFunctionParameterTypeList* typeList)
 *
 * @brief Finds a function type in the global function type table based on the given return type and parameter types.
 *
 * This function searches for a matching function type in the global function type table
 * using the provided return type and parameter types. It utilizes a static member function to
 * construct a mangled name, which is then used to look up the corresponding function type in the table.
 *
 * @param return_type The return type of the function to be found. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @param typeList The list of parameter types for the function to be found. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the matching SgFunctionType if found, otherwise NULL.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the global function type table has been properly initialized and populated.
 *
 * @pre The return_type and typeList parameters must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned function type is valid and corresponds to the given return type and parameter types.
 *
 * @note This function relies on the static member function SgFunctionType::get_mangled() to construct a mangled name for the lookup.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* returnType = ...; // obtain return type
 *           SgFunctionParameterTypeList* paramTypes = ...; // obtain parameter types
 *           SgFunctionType* funcType = SageInterface::findFunctionType(returnType, paramTypes);
 *           if (funcType != NULL) {
 *             // use the found function type
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isEquivalentFunctionType (const SgFunctionType* lhs, const SgFunctionType* rhs)
 *
 * @brief Checks if two function types are equivalent.
 *
 * This function compares the return types and argument lists of two function types
 * to determine if they are equivalent. The comparison is done recursively for each
 * type in the argument list.
 *
 * @param lhs The first function type to compare.
 * @param rhs The second function type to compare.
 *
 * @return True if the two function types are equivalent, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input function types are not null.
 *
 * @pre Both input function types must be non-null.
 *
 * @post The result is true if and only if the return types and argument lists of
 *       the two function types are equivalent.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to compare the types in the
 *       argument list. It also assumes that the types in the argument list are
 *       not null.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgFunctionType* func1 = ...;
 *           SgFunctionType* func2 = ...;
 *           if (SageInterface::isEquivalentFunctionType(func1, func2)) {
 *             // The two function types are equivalent.
 *           } else {
 *             // The two function types are not equivalent.
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::addStepToLoopBody(SgScopeStatement* loopStmt, SgStatement* step)
 *
 * @brief Adds a statement to the body of a loop.
 *
 * This function modifies the loop body by adding a new statement and updating
 * the control flow accordingly. It takes into account the existing statements in
 * the loop body and ensures that the new statement is properly linked with the
 * rest of the code.
 *
 * The function first retrieves the enclosing procedure of the given loop statement,
 * then it creates a new basic block to serve as the updated loop body. It also
 * generates a unique label for this new basic block.
 *
 * Next, it updates the existing loop body by changing any continue statements to
 * goto statements that target the newly created label. The old loop body is then
 * appended to the new basic block, followed by the new statement and finally the
 * label statement.
 *
 * @param loopStmt A pointer to the SgScopeStatement representing the loop whose body is to be modified.
 * @param step     A pointer to the SgStatement that is to be added to the loop body.
 *
 * @return None. This function modifies its inputs in-place and does not return any value.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input loop statement is valid and has an existing body.
 *          It also assumes that the new statement being added does not introduce any semantic errors
 *          into the code, such as undefined variables or type mismatches.
 *
 * @pre The input loop statement must have a valid enclosing procedure.
 *      The new statement to be added must be a valid SgStatement object.
 *
 * @post The loop body of the input loop statement will have been modified to include the new statement.
 *       Any continue statements in the original loop body will have been updated to goto statements targeting
 *       the newly created label.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to update the existing loop body, which may lead to performance
 *       issues for very large loop bodies. Additionally, this function does not perform any semantic checks on the
 *       new statement being added, so it is up to the caller to ensure that the code remains valid after modification.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* loopStmt = ...; // some existing loop statement
 *           SgStatement* step = buildAssignStatement(...); // some new assignment statement
 *           SageInterface::addStepToLoopBody(loopStmt, step);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::moveForStatementIncrementIntoBody(SgForStatement* f)
 *
 * @brief Moves the increment statement of a for loop into its body.
 *
 * This function takes a for loop statement as input and moves its increment
 * statement into the loop's body. It does this by creating an expression
 * statement from the increment, adding it to the loop's body, and then replacing
 * the original increment with a null expression.
 *
 * @param f The for loop statement whose increment is to be moved into its body.
 *          Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None. This function modifies the input for loop statement in-place.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input for loop statement has an
 *          increment statement that can be safely moved into the loop's body.
 *          If this is not the case, the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input for loop statement must not be NULL.
 * @post The increment statement of the input for loop statement will have been
 *       moved into its body.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the input for loop statement in-place. It does
 *       not create a new copy of the statement.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* forStmt = ...; // Create or obtain a for loop statement
 *           SageInterface::moveForStatementIncrementIntoBody(forStmt);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::convertForToWhile(SgForStatement* f)
 *
 * @brief Converts a for loop into an equivalent while loop.
 *
 * This function transforms a given SgForStatement into a semantically
 * equivalent SgWhileStmt. The conversion involves moving the increment
 * statement from the for loop into its body, creating a new basic block,
 * and replacing the original for loop with the newly constructed while loop.
 *
 * @param f The SgForStatement to be converted. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST by replacing the original
 *          for loop with the equivalent while loop.
 *
 * @pre The input SgForStatement must have a valid parent statement and a
 *      non-NULL loop body.
 *
 * @post The original for loop is replaced by an equivalent while loop in
 *       the AST, and all necessary updates are made to ensure consistency.
 *
 * @note This function uses the SageBuilder to construct new AST nodes as needed.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* forStmt = ...; // assume a valid for loop statement
 *           SageInterface::convertForToWhile(forStmt);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::convertAllForsToWhiles(SgNode* top)
 *
 * @brief Converts all for loops to while loops in a given AST.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) rooted at the given node
 * and converts all for loops to their equivalent while loop representations.
 * The conversion is performed by calling convertForToWhile() on each for statement found.
 *
 * @param top The root node of the AST to be processed. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST in-place. If the original AST structure is needed,
 *          a copy should be made before calling this function.
 *
 * @pre The input AST must be valid and well-formed.
 *
 * @post All for loops in the input AST have been converted to while loops.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the AST, which may lead to stack overflows
 *       for very large inputs. However, this is unlikely to occur in practice due to the typical size of C++ programs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* astRoot = ...; // obtain the root node of an AST
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           sage.convertAllForsToWhiles(astRoot);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::changeContinuesToGotos(SgStatement* stmt, SgLabelStatement* label)
 *
 * @brief Replaces continue statements with goto statements in the given statement.
 *
 * This function iterates over all continue statements within the provided statement
 * and replaces each one with a corresponding goto statement that targets the specified label.
 *
 * The replacement is performed using the LowLevelRewrite mechanism, which ensures that the
 * resulting code is semantically equivalent to the original.
 *
 * @param stmt The statement in which to replace continue statements. Must not be NULL.
 * @param label The target label for the goto statements. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function may modify the original statement and its children.
 *
 * @pre The provided statement and label must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post All continue statements within the given statement have been replaced with goto statements targeting the specified label.
 *
 * @note This function is only available when ROSE_USE_INTERNAL_FRONTEND_DEVELOPMENT is not defined. Otherwise, it will print an error message and abort.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // obtain a statement
 *           SgLabelStatement* label = ...; // obtain a label
 *           SageInterface::changeContinuesToGotos(stmt, label);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgInitializedName* SageInterface::getLoopIndexVariable(SgNode* loop)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the index variable of a for loop.
 *
 * This function takes a loop statement as input and returns the declaration
 * of the index variable used in that loop. It supports both C/C++ style for
 * loops and Fortran Do loops.
 *
 * The function iterates through the initialization statement of the loop to find
 * the index variable, checking for various cases such as C99-style declarations,
 * assignment statements, and comma-separated expressions. If the loop is not a
 * for loop or if the initialization statement does not match any of the expected
 * forms, the function returns NULL.
 *
 * @param loop The loop statement from which to retrieve the index variable.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the declaration of the index variable if found,
 *         otherwise NULL.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input loop is a for loop or a Fortran
 *          Do loop. If the loop is not one of these types, the behavior is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input loop must be non-NULL and have an initialization statement.
 *
 * @post The returned index variable declaration will be non-NULL if found.
 *
 * @note This function does not handle while loops or do-while loops. It also
 *       assumes that the loop's initialization statement is a single statement,
 *       either a variable declaration, an assignment statement, or an expression
 *       statement with a comma-separated list of expressions.
 *
 * @example The following example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* for_loop = ...;
 *           SgInitializedName* index_var = SageInterface::getLoopIndexVariable(for_loop);
 *           if (index_var != NULL)
 *             cout << "Index variable: " << index_var->get_name() << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isLoopIndexVariable(SgInitializedName* ivar, SgNode* subtree_root)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given variable is an index variable of a loop.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided initial named variable (ivar) 
 * serves as the index variable for any loop within the specified subtree rooted 
 * at subtree_root. The function traverses through the loops enclosing the subtree 
 * root and checks if the loop's index variable matches the given ivar.
 *
 * @param ivar The initial named variable to be checked as a potential loop index variable.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @param subtree_root The root of the subtree within which to search for loops containing the index variable.
 *                      Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the given variable is an index variable of any loop within the specified subtree, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the provided ivar and subtree_root are valid and correctly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input parameters ivar and subtree_root must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post If the function returns true, it guarantees that the given variable is an index variable of a loop within the specified subtree.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse through the loops enclosing the subtree root.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgInitializedName* ivar = ...; // Initialize ivar
 *           SgNode* subtree_root = ...; // Initialize subtree_root
 *           if (SageInterface::isLoopIndexVariable(ivar, subtree_root)) {
 *             cout << "The variable is an index variable of a loop." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "The variable is not an index variable of any loop." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::hasMultipleInitStatmentsOrExpressions (SgForStatement* loop)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a for loop has multiple initialization statements or expressions.
 *
 * This function examines the initialization statement of a given for loop and
 * determines whether it contains multiple statements or expressions. It checks
 * for cases where there are two variable declaration statements, a single
 * statement with a comma expression (e.g., i=0, j=0), or other scenarios that may
 * indicate the presence of multiple initialization statements or expressions.
 *
 * @param loop The SgForStatement object representing the for loop to be examined.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the for loop has multiple initialization statements or expressions,
 *         false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input for loop is valid and well-formed.
 *          If the loop is malformed, the results of this function are undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input SgForStatement object must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the for loop has multiple initialization
 *       statements or expressions, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses various ROSE library functions to examine the structure
 *       of the for loop and its initialization statement.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* loop = ...; // assume a valid SgForStatement object
 *           if (SageInterface::hasMultipleInitStatmentsOrExpressions(loop)) {
 *             cout << "Loop has multiple initialization statements or expressions." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "Loop does not have multiple initialization statements or expressions." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::getLoopBody(SgScopeStatement* loopStmt)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the body of a loop statement.
 *
 * This function takes a loop statement as input and returns its corresponding body.
 * It supports different types of loops, including while, for, and do-while statements.
 *
 * The function uses a series of conditional checks to determine the type of loop
 * and then calls the appropriate method to retrieve the loop body. If the loop
 * type is not recognized, an assertion failure occurs.
 *
 * @param loopStmt A pointer to the loop statement for which the body is to be retrieved.
 *                 Must be a valid SgScopeStatement object representing a while,
 *                 for, or do-while loop.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the loop body statement. Returns NULL if the input loop
 *          statement is not recognized or if an error occurs.
 *
 * @warning The function uses assertions to check for invalid input. If an assertion
 *          failure occurs, the program will terminate abruptly.
 *
 * @pre The input loop statement must be a valid SgScopeStatement object representing
 *      a while, for, or do-while loop.
 *
 * @post The returned loop body statement is valid and can be used for further processing.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any semantic checks on the input loop statement.
 *       It assumes that the input is valid and well-formed.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* loopStmt = ...; // assume a valid loop statement
 *           SgStatement* loopBody = SageInterface::getLoopBody(loopStmt);
 *           if (loopBody != NULL) {
 *               // process the loop body
 *           } else {
 *               // handle error or unrecognized loop type
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::setLoopBody(SgScopeStatement* loopStmt, SgStatement* body)
 *
 * @brief Sets the loop body of a given loop statement.
 *
 * This function updates the loop body of a provided loop statement with a new statement. 
 * It supports three types of loop statements: while, for, and do-while loops. The function checks
 * the type of loop statement using the isSgWhileStmt(), isSgForStatement(), and isSgDoWhileStmt()
 * functions, and then updates the corresponding body accordingly.
 *
 * @param loopStmt A pointer to an SgScopeStatement representing the loop statement whose body needs to be updated.
 *                 Must not be NULL. The loop statement can be a while, for, or do-while loop.
 *
 * @param body A pointer to an SgStatement representing the new loop body. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the provided loop statement and its body.
 *          It assumes that the input is valid and can lead to undefined behavior if this assumption is violated.
 *
 * @pre The loopStmt parameter must point to a valid SgScopeStatement object, and the body parameter must point to a valid SgStatement object.
 *
 * @post The loop body of the provided loop statement will be updated with the new statement.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the AST by updating the loop body. It does not create any new nodes or edges in the AST.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* loopStmt = ...; // obtain a valid loop statement
 *           SgStatement* body = ...; // obtain a valid statement for the loop body
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           sage.setLoopBody(loopStmt, body);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::getLoopCondition(SgScopeStatement* loopStmt)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the loop condition statement of a given loop.
 *
 * This function takes an SgScopeStatement pointer representing a loop and returns
 * the corresponding loop condition statement. The type of loop is determined at runtime,
 * and the appropriate method is called to retrieve the condition statement.
 *
 * @param loopStmt A pointer to an SgScopeStatement object, which must represent a valid loop.
 *                Supported loop types include while, for, and do-while loops.
 *
 * @return The loop condition statement of the input loop. If the loop type is not supported,
 *         or if there is an internal error, this function returns NULL.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the input loopStmt
 *          beyond its type. It is the caller's responsibility to ensure that the loop statement
 *          is valid and properly initialized before passing it to this function.
 *
 * @pre The input loopStmt must be a non-NULL pointer to an SgScopeStatement object, which must represent a valid loop.
 *
 * @post If the function returns successfully, the returned condition statement will be non-NULL.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to handle internal errors. If such an error
 *       occurs, this function will terminate the program and display an error message.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* loopStmt = ...; // Initialize the loop statement
 *           SgStatement* conditionStmt = SageInterface::getLoopCondition(loopStmt);
 *           if (conditionStmt != NULL) {
 *               // Process the condition statement as needed
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle the error or unsupported loop type
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::setLoopCondition(SgScopeStatement* loopStmt, SgStatement* cond)
 *
 * @brief Sets the loop condition for a given loop statement.
 *
 * This function updates the condition of a loop statement (while, for, or do-while) 
 * with a new condition statement. It checks the type of the loop statement and 
 * sets the condition accordingly.
 *
 * @param loopStmt The loop statement whose condition is to be updated. Must be one of SgWhileStmt, SgForStatement, or SgDoWhileStmt.
 * @param cond The new condition statement to be set for the loop statement.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning If the loop statement is not one of the supported types (SgWhileStmt, SgForStatement, or SgDoWhileStmt), a ROSE_ASSERT failure will occur.
 *
 * @pre The loop statement and condition statement must be valid and properly initialized.
 *
 * @post The loop condition of the loop statement will be updated with the new condition statement.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input loop statement and condition statement are correctly formed and do not contain any syntax errors.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* loopStmt = ...; // Initialize loop statement
 *           SgStatement* cond = ...; // Initialize condition statement
 *           SageInterface::setLoopCondition(loopStmt, cond);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool analyzeForLoop(SgNode* fs, SgFunctionSymbol* ivarname = NULL, SgExpression** lb = NULL, SgExpression** ub = NULL, SgExpression** step = NULL, SgStatement** body = NULL)
 * @brief Analyzes a for loop to extract information about the loop variable, lower bound, upper bound, step size, and body.
 *
 * This function checks if the given loop is in the standard form of a C-style for loop, where the initialization statement
 * declares a single integer variable, the condition is a comparison between this variable and another expression,
 * and the increment statement increments or decrements this variable by some amount. The upper bound can be either inclusive or exclusive.
 *
 * @param fs The for loop to analyze.
 * @param ivarname A pointer to store the name of the loop variable (optional).
 * @param lb A pointer to store the lower bound expression (optional).
 * @param ub A pointer to store the upper bound expression (optional).
 * @param step A pointer to store the step size expression (optional).
 * @param body A pointer to store the loop body statement (optional).
 * @return True if the loop is in the standard form and false otherwise.
 */

/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isCanonicalDoLoop(SgFortranDo* loop,SgInitializedName** ivar/*=NULL*/, SgExpression** lb/*=NULL*/, SgExpression** ub/*=NULL*/, SgExpression** step/*=NULL*/, SgStatement** body/*=NULL*/, bool *hasIncrementalIterationSpace/*= NULL*/, bool* isInclusiveUpperBound/*=NULL*/)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a Fortran Do loop is in canonical form.
 *
 * This function checks if a given Fortran Do loop is in canonical form, 
 * i.e., it has an integer index variable that is incremented by 1 or -1, 
 * and the loop's bounds are constant expressions. It also returns information 
 * about the loop, such as the lower bound, upper bound, step size, and body.
 *
 * @param loop The Fortran Do loop to check.
 * @param ivar [out] The loop index variable if the loop is in canonical form.
 * @param lb [out] The lower bound of the loop if it is in canonical form.
 * @param ub [out] The upper bound of the loop if it is in canonical form.
 * @param step [out] The step size of the loop if it is in canonical form.
 * @param body [out] The body of the loop if it is in canonical form.
 * @param hasIncrementalIterationSpace [out] Whether the loop has an incremental iteration space.
 * @param isInclusiveUpperBound [out] Whether the upper bound of the loop is inclusive.
 *
 * @return True if the loop is in canonical form, false otherwise.
 */

/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::setLoopLowerBound(SgNode* loop, SgExpression* lb)
 *
 * @brief Sets the lower bound of a for loop or Fortran do statement.
 *
 * This function sets the lower bound of a for loop or Fortran do statement
 * by modifying its initialization expression. The new lower bound is set to
 * the value specified in the second argument, and any existing lower bound
 * is replaced. If the loop is not a valid for loop or Fortran do statement,
 * an error message is printed.
 *
 * @param loop A pointer to the SgNode representing the loop whose lower bound is to be set.
 *             Must be non-null and represent either a SgForStatement or SgFortranDo node.
 *
 * @param lb   A pointer to the new lower bound expression. Must be non-null.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input loop statement in place, so use with caution.
 *
 * @pre The input loop must be a valid for loop or Fortran do statement, and the input lower bound expression must be non-null.
 *
 * @post The lower bound of the input loop is set to the value specified in the second argument.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input loop has an initialization expression,
 *       which may not always be the case (e.g. for Fortran do statements with no initial
 *       clause).
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create a sample for loop
 *           SgForStatement* forstmt = new SgForStatement();
 *           forstmt->set_init_stmt(new SgExprStatement());
 *
 *           // Set the lower bound of the loop
 *           SageInterface::setLoopLowerBound(forstmt, new SgIntVal(10));
 *
 *           // Print the modified loop statement
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(forstmt != NULL);
 *           cout << forstmt->class_name() << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::setLoopUpperBound(SgNode* loop, SgExpression* ub)
 *
 * @brief Sets the upper bound of a loop statement.
 *
 * This function updates the upper bound expression of a given loop statement.
 * It supports both Fortran-style DO loops and C-style FOR loops. The new upper
 * bound expression is set as the right-hand side operand of the loop's test
 * expression, or as the bound attribute of the DO statement, depending on the
 * type of loop.
 *
 * @param loop A pointer to the SgNode representing the loop statement.
 *             Must not be NULL and must represent a valid loop statement.
 *
 * @param ub   A pointer to the SgExpression representing the new upper bound.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any semantic checks on the input
 *          parameters. It is up to the caller to ensure that the loop statement
 *          and upper bound expression are valid and compatible.
 *
 * @pre The input loop statement must be a valid SgForStatement or SgFortranDo node.
 *
 * @post The upper bound expression of the loop statement is updated according
 *       to the input parameters.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the abstract syntax tree (AST) in place. It does
 *       not create a new AST.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* loop = ...; // obtain a pointer to a loop statement node
 *           SgExpression* ub = ...; // obtain a pointer to an expression node
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           sage.setLoopUpperBound(loop, ub);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::setLoopStride(SgNode* loop, SgExpression* stride)
 *
 * @brief Sets the stride of a loop in the abstract syntax tree (AST) to the given expression.
 *
 * This function updates the increment statement of a for/do loop with the provided stride expression.
 * It supports various forms of increment statements and handles different types of loops.
 *
 * The supported forms of increment statements include:
 *   - i++ or i-- (post-increment/decrement)
 *   - i+=X or i-=X (addition/subtraction assignment)
 *   - i=i+X or i=X+i (addition)
 *   - i=i-X or i=X-i (subtraction)
 *
 * The function also handles Fortran DO loops and updates their increment statements accordingly.
 *
 * @param loop A pointer to the SgNode representing the loop in the AST. Must not be NULL.
 * @param stride A pointer to the SgExpression representing the new stride value. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warnings If the loop is not a for/do loop or a Fortran DO loop, an error message is printed and the function asserts false.
 *
 * @pre The input loop and stride must be valid and non-NULL.
 * @post The increment statement of the loop is updated with the new stride expression.
 *
 * @example
 *   // Create a for loop with an initial value of 0 and a final value of 10
 *   SgForInitStatement* init = buildForInit(0, 10);
 *   SgForLoop* loop = buildForLoop(init, buildExprStatement(buildAssignOp(buildVarRef("i"), buildAddOp(buildVarRef("i"), buildIntVal(1)))));
 *
 *   // Create a stride expression with the value of 2
 *   SgExpression* stride = buildIntVal(2);
 *
 *   // Set the stride of the loop to the new value
 *   setLoopStride(loop, stride);
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::normalizeForLoopInitDeclaration(SgForStatement* loop)
 *
 * @brief Normalizes a for-loop initialization declaration by separating variable declarations from the loop condition.
 *
 * This function transforms a for-loop with an initialization declaration into a standard form where the variable declaration 
 * is moved outside of the loop and only the assignment part remains in the loop. For example, it converts "for (int i = 0; ...)"
 * to "int i; for (i = 0; ...)". This transformation helps to improve code readability and maintainability.
 *
 * @param loop The input SgForStatement node representing the for-loop that needs normalization.
 *             It must not be NULL. If it is, a ROSE_ASSERT will fail.
 *
 * @return True if the normalization was successful, false otherwise. 
 *         A return value of true means the function has modified the input AST (Abstract Syntax Tree). 
 *         The original variable declaration statement is replaced with an assignment statement and a new variable declaration
 *         statement is prepended to the enclosing function's body.
 *
 * @warning This normalization may potentially cause name pollution if the newly introduced variable declaration collides with existing variables in the same scope. It also assumes that the loop initialization does not have any side effects other than assigning values to variables.
 *
 * @pre The input SgForStatement node must be valid and non-NULL.
 *      The function has write access to the input AST.
 *
 * @post If true is returned, the input AST has been modified. 
 *       A new variable declaration statement has been prepended to the enclosing function's body.
 *       The original variable declaration statement in the loop initialization has been replaced with an assignment statement.
 *
 * @note This normalization does not handle cases where the loop initialization contains complex declarations such as Decomposition::Iterator ditr(&decomp).
 *       Such loops are considered non-canonical and this normalization will not be applied to them.
 *
 * @example An example usage of this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* loop = ...; // assume a valid for-loop statement is created or retrieved from the AST
 *           if (SageInterface::normalizeForLoopInitDeclaration(loop)) {
 *             // the input loop has been normalized and its AST modified accordingly
 *           } else {
 *             // normalization failed, likely due to complex declarations in the loop initialization
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::unnormalizeForLoopInitDeclaration(SgForStatement* loop)
 *
 * @brief Reverts a previously normalized for-loop initialization declaration to its original state.
 *
 * This function takes a pointer to an SgForStatement as input and reverts any previous normalization 
 * of the loop's initialization declaration. The normalization process involves replacing the original 
 * declaration with a new one, and this function restores the original declaration and updates any 
 * variable references accordingly.
 *
 * @param loop A pointer to the SgForStatement whose initialization declaration is to be unnormalized.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the loop's initialization declaration was successfully unnormalized, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input loop has been previously normalized using the 
 *          normalizeForLoopInitDeclaration() function. If this is not the case, the behavior of this 
 *          function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input loop must be non-NULL and have a valid initialization declaration.
 *
 * @post The loop's initialization declaration is restored to its original state, and any variable 
 *       references are updated accordingly.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the input loop's AST in-place.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* loop = ...; // obtain a pointer to an SgForStatement
 *           if (SageInterface::unnormalizeForLoopInitDeclaration(loop)) {
 *             // the loop's initialization declaration has been successfully unnormalized
 *           } else {
 *             // an error occurred during unnormalization
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::forLoopNormalization(SgForStatement* loop, bool foldConstant /*= true*/)
 *
 * @brief Normalizes a for loop by rearranging its components to conform to a standard form.
 *
 * This function normalizes the initialization statement, test expressions, and increment
 * expression of a given for loop. It also ensures that the loop body is contained within
 * a basic block. Additionally, it performs constant folding on the loop controlling
 * expressions if requested.
 *
 * @param loop The SgForStatement node representing the for loop to be normalized.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 * @param foldConstant A boolean flag indicating whether to perform constant folding
 *                     on the loop controlling expressions. Defaults to true.
 *
 * @return True if the normalization is successful, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning The function may modify the original AST.
 *
 * @pre The input SgForStatement node must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The for loop will be normalized and its components rearranged to conform
 *       to a standard form. The loop body will be contained within a basic block.
 *       If constant folding is requested, it will be performed on the loop controlling
 *       expressions.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input SgForStatement node represents a valid for loop.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           SgForStatement* loop = ...; // obtain a for loop statement node
 *           if (!sage.forLoopNormalization(loop)) {
 *             // handle error
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::normalizeForLoopTest(SgForStatement* loop)
 *
 * @brief Normalizes the test expression of a for loop.
 *
 * This function normalizes the test expression of a given for loop by transforming
 * certain binary operations into their equivalent forms. The normalization is
 * done to facilitate further analysis or transformation of the loop.
 *
 * The function checks if the test expression is a binary operation and, if so,
 * applies the following transformations:
 *   - Less-than (<) operations are transformed into less-or-equal (<=) operations
 *     by subtracting 1 from the right operand.
 *   - Greater-than (>) operations are transformed into greater-or-equal (>=)
 *     operations by adding 1 to the right operand.
 *
 * If the test expression is not a binary operation or if it cannot be normalized,
 * the function returns false. Otherwise, it returns true.
 *
 * @param loop The for loop whose test expression is to be normalized.
 *             Must be non-null and have a valid test expression.
 *
 * @return True if the test expression was successfully normalized, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input loop's test expression. If the original
 *          expression needs to be preserved, a copy of the loop should be made before
 *          calling this function.
 *
 * @pre The input loop is non-null and has a valid test expression.
 *
 * @post The input loop's test expression has been normalized if possible.
 *
 * @note This function does not handle all possible binary operations. It only
 *       handles less-than, greater-than, less-or-equal, and greater-or-equal operations.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* loop = ...; // create or obtain a for loop
 *           if (SageInterface::normalizeForLoopTest(loop)) {
 *             // the test expression has been normalized
 *           } else {
 *             // the test expression could not be normalized
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */

