 *           the first one is returned. This may lead to unexpected behavior if
 *           the AST contains multiple project nodes.
 *
 * @preconditions The Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) must have been constructed prior
 *                 to calling this function.
 *
 * @postconditions The returned project node pointer remains valid until the AST
 *                  is modified or destroyed.
 *
 * @note This function uses a traversal mechanism, which may incur performance
 *       overhead for large ASTs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgProject* project = SageInterface::getProject();
 *           if (project != NULL) {
 *             // Use the project node as needed
 *           } else {
 *             // Handle the case where no project node is found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::getProject(const SgNode * node)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing project of a given node.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) to find the project
 * that encloses the specified node. It uses a recursive approach, checking if
 * the current node is itself a project or contains one as an ancestor.
 *
 * @param node The SgNode for which the enclosing project needs to be found.
 *             Must not be NULL and must be part of a valid AST structure.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgProject that encloses the input node, or NULL if
 *         no such project exists. In case of error, it may also return an invalid
 *         pointer.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the provided SgNode is part of a valid AST.
 *          If the node is not properly initialized or does not belong to any AST,
 *          undefined behavior may occur.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be non-NULL and belong to a well-formed AST structure.
 *
 * @post The returned project pointer, if not NULL, points to a valid SgProject
 *       that directly or indirectly encloses the input node.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm which may lead to stack overflow
 *       for very deep AST structures. However, typical ASTs generated from source
 *       code are expected to have manageable depths.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgProject* project = SageInterface::getProject(someNode);
 *           if (project != NULL) {
 *               // Use the enclosing project as needed
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle the case when no enclosing project is found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn template <typename NodeType> std::vector<NodeType*> getSgNodeListFromMemoryPool()
 *
 * @brief Retrieves a list of SgNode objects from memory pool using traversal.
 *
 * This function utilizes a custom traversal class to visit and collect
 * SgNode objects of type NodeType from the memory pool. It is designed to work
 * specifically with the ROSE IR nodes, leveraging their traverseMemoryPoolNodes
 * method for efficient node retrieval.
 *
 * @param None
 *
