 * @return A pointer to the SgFunctionDeclaration representing the main function,
 *         or 0 if no main function is found in the AST. The returned
 *         SgFunctionDeclaration must be checked for its defining declaration to
 *         ensure it's not a non-defining main() declaration.
 *
 * @warning This function performs recursive traversal of the AST, which may lead
 *          to stack overflow for very deep trees.
 *
 * @pre The input node 'n' is a valid SgNode in an AST.
 *
 * @post If a main function declaration is found, its defining declaration is
 *       verified and returned; otherwise, 0 is returned.
 *
 * @note This function uses the isMain() helper function to identify the main
 *       function node. The implementation of isMain() is not shown here but it's
 *       assumed to correctly identify the main function based on some criteria.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...; // assume 'root' points to a valid AST node
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* mainDecl = SageInterface::findMain(root);
 *           if (mainDecl) {
 *             cout << "Found main function declaration!" << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "No main function found in the AST." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::findLastDeclarationStatement(SgScopeStatement * scope, bool includePragma/* = false */ )
 *
 * @brief Finds the last declaration statement within a given scope.
 *
 * This function searches for the last declaration statement in the provided
 * scope. It iterates through all statements in the scope and returns the last
 * encountered declaration statement, excluding pragmas unless specified otherwise.
 *
 * The search is performed by generating a list of all statements in the scope
 * and then iterating through this list from start to finish. The first declaration
 * statement found (excluding pragmas if includePragma is false) sets the return value,
 * which remains unchanged until the end of the iteration, thus ensuring that the last
 * encountered declaration statement is returned.
 *
 * @param scope The scope within which to search for the last declaration statement.
 *              Must not be NULL. This parameter defines the boundaries of the search.
 *
 * @param includePragma A boolean flag indicating whether to include pragma declarations
 *                      in the search (default is false). If true, pragmas are considered valid
 *                      declaration statements and can be returned as the result.
 *
 * @return The last declaration statement found within the specified scope. Returns NULL if no
 *         declaration statements were found or if an error occurred during the search.
 *
 * @warning This function may return a pragma declaration statement if includePragma is true.
 *          The caller should handle this possibility accordingly.
 *
 * @pre The provided scope must not be NULL, and it must contain at least one statement.
 *
 * @post The returned statement will be a valid declaration statement within the given scope,
 *       or NULL if no such statement was found. The function does not modify the input scope.
 *
 * @note This function uses an iterative approach to find the last declaration statement,
 *       which may have performance implications for large scopes.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create a sample scope with some statements
 *           SgScopeStatement* myScope = new SgScopeStatement();
 *           
 *           // Add some statements to the scope, including at least one declaration statement
 *           myScope->appendStatement(new SgVariableDeclaration());
 *           
 *           // Find the last declaration statement in the scope
 *           SgStatement* lastDeclStmt = SageInterface::findLastDeclarationStatement(myScope);
 *           
 *           // Use or process the found declaration statement as needed
 *           if (lastDeclStmt != NULL) {
 *               // Process the declaration statement here...
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn std::vector<SgVariableSymbol*> SageInterface::getSymbolsUsedInExpression(SgExpression* expr)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves a list of variable symbols used in the given expression.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) of the input expression 
 * and collects all variable symbols referenced within it. The traversal is performed
 * using a preorder strategy, ensuring that each node is visited before its children.
 *
 * @param expr The SgExpression object representing the AST to be traversed.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A vector of SgVariableSymbol pointers referencing the variables used in the expression.
