 *
 * @post The returned vector contains all variable symbols used in the input expression, 
 *        but does not guarantee any specific order or uniqueness of the symbols.
 *
 * @note This function uses a preorder traversal strategy to visit each node in the AST. 
 *       As such, it may not be suitable for expressions with deeply nested structures.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* expr = ...; // Initialize an expression object
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           std::vector<SgVariableSymbol*> symbols = sage.getSymbolsUsedInExpression(expr);
 *           for (SgVariableSymbol* symbol : symbols) {
 *             // Process the variable symbol as needed
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn vector<SgBreakStmt*> SageInterface::findBreakStmts(SgStatement* code, const std::string& fortranLabel)
 *
 * @brief Finds break statements in a given statement.
 *
 * This function searches for SgBreakStmt nodes within the provided SgStatement
 * and returns them as a vector. The search is limited to the scope of the input
 * statement, stopping at any construct that defines a new scope for break statements.
 *
 * @param code      The SgStatement to search for break statements.
 *                  Must be a valid pointer to an SgStatement object.
 * @param fortranLabel  A string representing the Fortran label associated with this search.
 *                      May be empty if no specific label is required.
 *
 * @return A vector of SgBreakStmt pointers found within the input statement.
 *         The returned vector may be empty if no break statements are found.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any validation on the input parameters.
 *          It assumes that the provided code and fortranLabel are valid and correctly
 *          initialized. Passing invalid or null pointers may result in undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must be a valid SgStatement object, and the fortranLabel must be a non-empty string if a specific label is required.
 *
 * @post The returned vector of break statements is guaranteed to contain only valid SgBreakStmt objects found within the scope of the input statement.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive helper function (findBreakStmtsHelper) to perform the actual search.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           SgStatement* code = ...; // Initialize code with some statement
 *           std::string fortranLabel = "some_label";
 *           vector<SgBreakStmt*> breaks = sage.findBreakStmts(code, fortranLabel);
 *           for (SgBreakStmt* breakStmt : breaks) {
 *               // Process each found break statement
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn vector<SgContinueStmt*> SageInterface::findContinueStmts(SgStatement* code, const std::string& fortranLabel)
 *
 * @brief Finds all continue statements in a given statement that match the specified Fortran label.
 *
 * This function searches through the provided SgStatement and returns a list of
 * SgContinueStmt pointers that correspond to continue statements matching the given
 * Fortran label. The search is performed recursively, stopping at any construct that
 * defines a new scope for continue statements.
 *
 * @param code The statement in which to find continue statements. This should typically be
 *             the body of a loop, as the function stops searching at any construct that
 *             defines a new scope for continue statements.
 *
 * @param fortranLabel The Fortran label to match when finding continue statements.
 *
 * @return A list of SgContinueStmt pointers representing the found continue statements.
 *
 * @warning This function may not work correctly if the provided statement is not a valid
 *          loop body or if the Fortran label is empty.
 *
 * @pre The input code and fortranLabel must be non-null and valid.
 *
 * @post The returned list of SgContinueStmt pointers will contain all matching continue statements.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive helper function to perform the search. It may not work
 *       correctly if the provided statement is very deeply nested or contains many scopes.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* loopBody = ...; // assume some valid loop body
 *           std::string fortranLabel = "my_label";
 *           vector<SgContinueStmt*> continueStmts = SageInterface::findContinueStmts(loopBody, fortranLabel);
 *           for (SgContinueStmt* stmt : continueStmts) {
 *             // process the found continue statement
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn vector<SgGotoStatement*> SageInterface::findGotoStmts(SgStatement* scope, SgLabelStatement* l)
 *
 * @brief Finds all goto statements within a given scope that target a specific label.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) rooted at the provided
 * scope and identifies all SgGotoStatement nodes that have a label matching the
 * specified SgLabelStatement. The search is performed using NodeQuery's querySubTree
 * method, which returns a container of SgNode pointers representing the goto statements.
 *
 * @param scope The statement serving as the root of the AST subtree to be searched for goto statements.
 *              Must not be NULL.
 * @param l     The label statement that the sought goto statements must target.
 *              Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A vector of SgGotoStatement pointers representing all goto statements found within
 *          the specified scope that target the given label. If no matching goto statements
 *          are found, an empty vector is returned.
 *
 * @warning The function does not perform any checks on the validity or existence of the provided
 *          scope and label statement. It assumes they are both valid and exist within the AST.
 *
 * @pre The caller must ensure that the provided scope and label statement are valid and exist within the AST.
 *
 * @post The returned vector is guaranteed to contain only SgGotoStatement pointers that target the specified label.
 *
 * @note This function uses NodeQuery's querySubTree method, which may have performance implications for large ASTs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            // Assume 'scope' is a valid SgStatement pointer and 'labelStmt' is a valid SgLabelStatement pointer
 *            vector<SgGotoStatement*> gotos = SageInterface::findGotoStmts(scope, labelStmt);
 *
 *            for (vector<SgGotoStatement*>::const_iterator i = gotos.begin(); i != gotos.end(); ++i) {
 *              // Process the found goto statements as needed
 *            }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn vector<SgStatement*> SageInterface::getSwitchCases(SgSwitchStatement* sw)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves a list of switch cases from a given SgSwitchStatement.
 *
 * This function takes an SgSwitchStatement as input and returns a vector of 
 * SgStatement pointers representing the individual switch cases. It utilizes 
 * a helper function, getSwitchCasesHelper, to recursively traverse the statement 
 * body and collect the relevant statements.
 *
 * @param sw The SgSwitchStatement from which to extract the switch cases.
 *
 * @return A vector of SgStatement pointers containing the extracted switch cases.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgSwitchStatement is valid and 
 *          has a non-null body. Failure to meet these preconditions may result 
 *          in undefined behavior or runtime errors.
 *
 * @pre The input SgSwitchStatement must be valid and have a non-null body.
 *
 * @post The returned vector contains all switch cases from the input statement.
 *
 * @note This function is part of the SageInterface class, which provides an interface 
 *       for interacting with the ROSE compiler infrastructure. It is intended to 
 *       facilitate code analysis and manipulation tasks.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgSwitchStatement* sw = ...; // obtain a switch statement
 *           vector<SgStatement*> cases = SageInterface::getSwitchCases(sw);
 *           for (const auto& case_ : cases) {
 *               // process the individual switch cases
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::collectVarRefs(SgLocatedNode* root, std::vector<SgVarRefExp* > & result)
 *
 * @brief Collects variable references in an abstract syntax tree (AST) rooted at the given node.
 *
 * This function traverses the AST and gathers all variable references found within the subtree rooted at the input node. The collected variable references are stored in the output vector.
 *
 * The implementation uses a recursive approach to traverse the AST, leveraging ROSE's NodeQuery functionality to efficiently query the tree for nodes of type SgVarRefExp.
 *
 * @param root The root node of the AST subtree to be traversed and searched for variable references. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @param result A reference to a vector that will store the collected variable references. The function appends found variable references to this vector.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input root node is valid and correctly initialized. Failure to ensure this may lead to undefined behavior or errors during execution.
 *
 * @pre The input root node must not be NULL, and the output result vector must be properly initialized before calling this function.
 *
 * @post Upon completion, the result vector will contain all variable references found within the AST subtree rooted at the input node. The contents of the original AST are left unchanged.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any semantic analysis or validation on the collected variable references; it merely gathers and stores them for further processing or analysis.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgLocatedNode* root = ...; // assume a valid AST node is created
 *           std::vector<SgVarRefExp*> result;
 *           SageInterface::collectVarRefs(root, result);
 *           for (size_t i = 0; i < result.size(); ++i) {
 *             SgVarRefExp* vRef = result[i];
 *             // process or analyze the collected variable reference
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn template <typename T> T* findDeclarationStatement(SgNode* root, std::string name, SgScopeStatement* scope, bool isDefining)
 *
 * @brief Finds a declaration statement in the abstract syntax tree (AST) that matches the given name and scope.
 *
 * This function searches for a declaration statement of type T in the AST rooted at 'root' with the specified 'name' 
 * within the given 'scope'. If 'isDefining' is true, it returns the defining declaration; otherwise, it returns the first 
 * non-defining declaration. The search is performed recursively by traversing the successor container of each node.
 *
 * @param root The root node of the AST to start searching from. Must be a valid pointer.
 * @param name The name of the declaration statement to search for.
 * @param scope The scope in which to search for the declaration statement. Can be NULL.
 * @param isDefining Whether to return the defining declaration (true) or the first non-defining declaration (false).
 *
 * @return A pointer to the found declaration statement, or NULL if not found.
 *
 * @warning If 'root' is NULL, the function returns immediately with a NULL result. Consider adding an assertion to ensure 
 *          that 'root' is always valid.
 *
 * @pre The input parameters must be valid: 'root' must be a non-NULL pointer, and 'name' must be a valid string.
 * @post If a matching declaration statement is found, the returned pointer points to a valid node in the AST; otherwise, 
 *       the function returns NULL.
 *
 * @note The search algorithm used by this function may not work correctly for function members with defining declarations 
 *       outside of their class. Also, the function does not handle cases where multiple declarations have the same name within 
 *       the given scope.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root node of the AST
 *           std::string name = "myVariable"; // set the name to search for
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = ...; // obtain the scope in which to search
 *           bool isDefining = true; // whether to return the defining declaration
 *
 *           T* decl = findDeclarationStatement<T>(root, name, scope, isDefining);
 *           if (decl != NULL) {
 *               printf("Found declaration statement: %s\n", decl->get_name().str());
 *           } else {
 *               printf("No matching declaration statement found.\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgFunctionDeclaration* SageInterface::findFunctionDeclaration(SgNode* root, std::string name, SgScopeStatement* scope, bool isDefining)
 *
 * @brief Searches for a function declaration in the given AST node.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) rooted at the given
 * node to find a function declaration with the specified name and scope. It returns
 * a pointer to the found SgFunctionDeclaration node if successful, or NULL otherwise.
 *
 * The search is performed recursively, starting from the root node down to its
 * children nodes. If the isDefining flag is set to true, the search will only consider
 * function declarations that are defining instances (i.e., non-forward declarations).
 *
 * @param root The root node of the AST to start searching from.
 * @param name The name of the function declaration to search for.
 * @param scope The scope statement within which the function declaration should be found.
 * @param isDefining Whether to only consider defining instances of the function declaration.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgFunctionDeclaration node if found, or NULL otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse the AST. While this
 *       approach allows for efficient searching, it may lead to stack overflow errors
 *       for very large ASTs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root node of the AST
 *           std::string funcName = "myFunction";
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = ...; // obtain the scope statement
 *           bool isDefining = true;
 *
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* decl = SageInterface::findFunctionDeclaration(root, funcName, scope, isDefining);
 *           if (decl != NULL) {
 *               // found function declaration, process it further
 *           } else {
 *               // function declaration not found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */

/**
 * @fn template <typename NodeType> NodeType* getEnclosingNode(SgNode* astNode, bool includingSelf = false) 
 * @brief Gets the enclosing node of a specified type.
 *
 * This function traverses up the AST from the given node until it finds a parent of the specified type. It handles cycles in the ancestry to prevent infinite loops. If no such parent is found, it returns NULL.
 *
 * @param astNode The starting node for the traversal.
 * @param includingSelf Whether to consider the starting node itself as a potential match.
 * @return A pointer to the first ancestor of the specified type, or NULL if none is found.
 */

/**
 * @fn SgNode* SageInterface::getEnclosingSourceFile(SgNode* n, bool includingSelf)
 *
 * @brief Returns the enclosing source file of a given node.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) to find the
 * enclosing source file of a given node. It uses the SAGE compiler's
 * infrastructure to navigate the AST and locate the source file.
 *
 * The search for the enclosing source file starts from the given node and
 * moves upwards in the AST, considering all parent nodes until it finds
 * an instance of SgSourceFile or reaches the root of the AST. If the
 * includingSelf parameter is true, the function also considers the given
 * node itself as a potential result.
 *
 * @param n The node for which to find the enclosing source file.
 *          Must be a valid pointer to an instance of SgNode or one of its subclasses.
 *
 * @param includingSelf Whether to consider the given node itself as a potential result.
 *                       If true, the function returns the given node if it is an instance
 *                       of SgSourceFile. Otherwise, the function only considers parent nodes.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the enclosing source file of the given node, or nullptr if no such file exists.
 *
 * @warning The result may be nullptr if the given node does not have a parent that is an instance of SgSourceFile.
 *          In this case, it is up to the caller to handle the situation accordingly.
 *
 * @pre n must be a valid pointer to an instance of SgNode or one of its subclasses.
 *
 * @post The result is either a valid pointer to an instance of SgSourceFile or nullptr.
 *
 * @note This function uses the SAGE compiler's infrastructure and may only work correctly
 *       within the context of the SAGE compiler framework.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* node = ...; // obtain a node somehow
 *           SgSourceFile* file = SageInterface::getEnclosingSourceFile(node, false);
 *           if (file != nullptr) {
 *               // do something with the source file
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::getScope( const SgNode* astNode )
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the scope of a given Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) node.
 *
 * This function determines the scope associated with an input AST node. The
 * scope is defined as the region of the program where the variable or function
 * declaration is valid and accessible. It traverses through the parents to find
 * the first scope, which is used for name qualification support of template arguments.
 *
 * @param astNode The Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) node for which the scope is to be determined.
 *                Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgScopeStatement representing the scope associated with the input AST node,
 *         or NULL if an error occurs while locating the scope.
 *
 * @warning This function may return NULL if it fails to locate a valid scope for the given AST node.
 *          The caller should check for this condition and handle it accordingly.
 *
 * @pre astNode must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post If a valid scope is found, the returned SgScopeStatement pointer will not be NULL.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse through the parents of the input AST node
 *       until it finds the first scope. It also performs checks for specific types of nodes (e.g., SgSymbol,
 *       SgInitializedName, SgTemplateArgument) and returns their scopes directly if applicable.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an instance of the SageInterface class
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           
 *           // Assume 'node' is a valid Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) node
 *           const SgNode* astNode = node;
 *           
 *           // Retrieve the scope associated with the AST node
 *           const SgScopeStatement* scope = sage.getScope(astNode);
 *           
 *           // Check if a valid scope was found
 *           if (scope != NULL) {
 *               // Process the scope as needed
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle the error condition
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgScopeStatement* SageInterface::getEnclosingScope(SgNode* n, const bool includingSelf/* =false*/)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing scope statement of a given node.
 *
 * This function computes the enclosing scope statement of a given node in an abstract syntax tree (AST).
 * It traverses up the AST from the input node to find the nearest enclosing scope statement.
 *
 * @param n The node for which the enclosing scope is to be retrieved. Must not be NULL.
 * @param includingSelf A flag indicating whether the input node itself should be considered as a potential enclosing scope.
 *                      If true, the function returns the input node if it is already a scope statement; otherwise,
 *                      the function traverses up the AST to find the nearest enclosing scope statement.
 *
 * @return The enclosing scope statement of the input node. Returns NULL if no enclosing scope is found or
 *         if the input node itself is not within any scope statement.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input node is a valid node in an abstract syntax tree (AST).
 *          If the input node is invalid or corrupted, the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be non-NULL and part of a valid AST.
 *
 * @post The returned enclosing scope statement is guaranteed to be a valid SgScopeStatement object,
 *       but may be NULL if no enclosing scope is found.
 *
 * @note This function uses the Sage III compiler infrastructure, which provides an abstract syntax tree (AST)
 *       representation of source code. The AST is traversed recursively to find the nearest enclosing scope statement.
 *
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::getGlobalScope(const SgNode* astNode)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the global scope of a given abstract syntax tree (AST) node.
 *
 * This function returns the global scope associated with the provided AST node,
 * which represents the top-level scope in the source code. The global scope is
 * determined by traversing up the AST hierarchy from the input node until a
 * global node is found, including the possibility of returning the input node
 * itself if it is already at the global level.
 *
 * @param astNode A pointer to an SgNode representing the abstract syntax tree node
 *                for which the global scope is to be retrieved. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgGlobal node representing the global scope of the input AST node.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input AST node has been properly initialized and is part of a valid AST hierarchy.
 *
 * @pre The input AST node must exist within a well-formed AST hierarchy.
 *
 * @post The returned SgGlobal node represents the top-level scope in the source code associated with the input AST node.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse up the AST hierarchy, which may lead to performance issues for very deep hierarchies.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* astNode = ...; // obtain an AST node from somewhere
 *           SgGlobal* globalScope = SageInterface::getGlobalScope(astNode);
 *           if (globalScope != NULL) {
 *               // do something with the global scope
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::hasSameGlobalScope(SgStatement* statement_1, SgStatement* statement_2)
 *
 * @brief Checks if two statements have the same global scope.
 *
 * This function determines whether two given statements are enclosed by the same global scope. 
 * It traverses up the abstract syntax tree (AST) from each statement to find their respective global scopes, 
 * and then checks for equality between these scopes.
 *
 * @param statement_1 The first statement to check.
 * @param statement_2 The second statement to check.
 *
 * @return True if both statements have the same global scope; false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function is used to support the recognition of functions in header files from two different ASTs.
 *
 * @warning No error handling is performed on the input statements. It is assumed that they are valid and non-null.
 *
 * @pre The input statements must be valid and non-null.
 *
 * @post None
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* statement_1 = ...; // Initialize statement_1
 *           SgStatement* statement_2 = ...; // Initialize statement_2
 *           bool result = SageInterface::hasSameGlobalScope(statement_1, statement_2);
 *           if (result) {
 *               printf("Both statements have the same global scope.\n");
 *           } else {
 *               printf("The statements do not have the same global scope.\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgFunctionDefinition* SageInterface::getEnclosingProcedure(SgNode* n, bool includingSelf)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing procedure of a given node in the abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree to find the nearest enclosing procedure
 * (function or method) for a given node. The search can optionally include the input node itself.
 *
 * The implementation uses a recursive approach, starting from the input node and moving up the AST
 * until it finds a procedure that encloses the node.
 *
 * @param n The node in the abstract syntax tree for which to find the enclosing procedure.
 * @param includingSelf Flag indicating whether to include the input node itself in the search.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgFunctionDefinition representing the enclosing procedure, or NULL if none is found.
 *
 * @warning If the input node is not part of a valid abstract syntax tree, this function may return incorrect results.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be a valid SgNode instance and part of an abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * @post The returned SgFunctionDefinition pointer will remain valid until the underlying AST is modified or deleted.
 *
 * @note This function is used internally by the SageInterface to support various code analysis and transformation tasks.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* node = ...; // some node in the abstract syntax tree
 *           bool includeSelf = true;
 *           SgFunctionDefinition* enclosingProc = SageInterface::getEnclosingProcedure(node, includeSelf);
 *           if (enclosingProc != NULL) {
 *             // do something with the enclosing procedure
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgNode* SageInterface::getEnclosingFunctionDefinition(SgNode* n, bool includingSelf)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing function definition of a given node.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) to find the nearest 
 * enclosing function definition for a specified node. The search can optionally
 * include the node itself as a potential match.
 *
 * The implementation relies on the ROSE compiler infrastructure and utilizes the 
 * SgNode class hierarchy to navigate the AST.
 *
 * @param n The node for which the enclosing function definition is sought.
 *          Must be a valid pointer to an SgNode object.
 *
 * @param includingSelf Flag indicating whether the search should include the input
 *                       node itself as a potential match. If true, the function will 
 *                       return the node's own function definition if applicable; otherwise,
 *                       it will only consider parent nodes in the AST.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgNode representing the enclosing function definition, or NULL
 *         if no such definition is found.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input node has been properly initialized and 
 *          is part of a valid ROSE AST. Failure to meet these conditions may result in undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be non-NULL and part of a valid ROSE AST.
 *
 * @post The returned pointer, if not NULL, points to the nearest enclosing function definition for the input node.
 *
 * @note This function is primarily intended for use within the SageInterface class. External users should exercise caution when invoking this method directly.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to retrieve the enclosing function definition for a given node:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* myNode = ...; // assume 'myNode' points to some valid AST node
 *           bool includeSelf = true;
 *           SgNode* enclosingFunction = SageInterface::getEnclosingFunctionDefinition(myNode, includeSelf);
 *           if (enclosingFunction != NULL) {
 *               // process the enclosing function definition as needed
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::getEnclosingStatement(SgNode* n)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing statement of a given node in the abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * This function traverses up the parent nodes of the input node until it finds a node that represents a statement, 
 * and returns this statement. If no such statement is found (i.e., if the root node is reached), 
 * the function returns a null pointer.
 *
 * The algorithm used in this implementation simply checks each parent node to see if it is an instance of SgStatement,
 * which represents statements in the Sage++ abstract syntax tree. This approach has a time complexity of O(h),
 * where h is the height of the subtree rooted at the input node, since in the worst case we may need to traverse up
 * to the root node.
 *
 * @param n The node for which the enclosing statement is to be retrieved.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgStatement object representing the enclosing statement of the input node,
 *         or a null pointer if no such statement exists (which should not happen in a well-formed abstract syntax tree).
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error checking on its input parameter. 
 *          It assumes that the input node is valid and part of a well-formed abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be non-null and part of a well-formed abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * @post If a statement node is found, it will be returned; otherwise, a null pointer will be returned.
 *
 * @note This function does not modify the abstract syntax tree in any way. It simply traverses the existing tree structure.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Assume we have an SgNode* called my_node that represents a node in the AST.
 *           SgStatement* enclosing_stmt = SageInterface::getEnclosingStatement(my_node);
 *           if (enclosing_stmt != NULL) {
 *             // We found an enclosing statement; do something with it...
 *           } else {
 *             // No enclosing statement was found; handle this case as needed...
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgSwitchStatement* SageInterface::findEnclosingSwitch(SgStatement* s)
 *
 * @brief Finds the enclosing switch statement of a given statement in an abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * This function traverses up the parent nodes of the input statement until it finds a node that represents a switch statement. 
 * It uses the ROSE compiler infrastructure's abstract syntax tree (AST) data structure to navigate the AST and perform the search.
 *
 * @param s The input statement for which the enclosing switch statement is to be found. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgSwitchStatement node that encloses the input statement, or NULL if no such node exists.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input statement is part of a valid AST and that the ROSE compiler infrastructure has been properly initialized.
 *
 * @warning If the input statement does not have a parent node that represents a switch statement, this function will return NULL. 
 *          It is up to the caller to handle this situation accordingly.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must be non-NULL and part of a valid AST.
 *
 * @post The returned SgSwitchStatement pointer will either point to a valid node in the AST or be NULL.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an instance of SageInterface
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           
 *           // Assume we have a statement that is part of an AST
 *           SgStatement* statement = ...;
 *           
 *           // Find the enclosing switch statement
 *           SgSwitchStatement* enclosingSwitch = sage.findEnclosingSwitch(statement);
 *           
 *           // Check if an enclosing switch statement was found
 *           if (enclosingSwitch != NULL) {
 *               // Do something with the enclosing switch statement
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle the case where no enclosing switch statement was found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgOmpClauseBodyStatement* SageInterface::findEnclosingOmpClauseBodyStatement(SgStatement* s)
 *
 * @brief Finds the enclosing OpenMP clause body statement for a given statement.
 *
 * This function traverses the AST upwards from the input statement to find
 * the nearest enclosing OpenMP clause body statement. It uses a recursive
 * approach, checking each parent node until it finds an SgOmpClauseBodyStatement
 * or reaches the root of the AST.
 *
 * @param s The statement for which to find the enclosing OpenMP clause body statement.
 *          Must be a non-NULL pointer to an SgStatement object.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the enclosing SgOmpClauseBodyStatement if found, NULL otherwise.
 *
 * @warning If the input statement is not contained within an OpenMP clause body,
 *          this function will return NULL. It is up to the caller to handle this case.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must be a valid non-NULL pointer to an SgStatement object.
 *
 * @post The returned pointer, if non-NULL, points to a valid SgOmpClauseBodyStatement object.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any checks for the validity of the OpenMP clause
 *       or its contents. It simply traverses the AST and returns the first matching statement.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // some statement in the AST
 *           SgOmpClauseBodyStatement* enclosingStmt =
 *               SageInterface::findEnclosingOmpClauseBodyStatement(stmt);
 *           if (enclosingStmt != NULL) {
 *             // do something with the enclosing OpenMP clause body statement
 *           } else {
 *             // handle the case where no enclosing OpenMP clause body statement is found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgScopeStatement* SageInterface::findEnclosingLoop(SgStatement* s, const std::string& label, bool stopOnSwitches)
 *
 * @brief Finds the enclosing loop statement for a given statement in an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).
 *
 * This function traverses up the AST from a given statement to find the first enclosing loop statement. 
 * A loop statement is defined as any of the following: SgDoWhileStmt, SgForStatement, SgFortranDo, 
 * SgFortranNonblockedDo, or SgWhileStmt. If the label parameter is not empty, it will only consider loop statements
 * with a matching label. The stopOnSwitches parameter determines whether to stop at switch statements.
 *
 * @param s The statement from which to start searching for an enclosing loop statement.
 *          Must be non-null and valid in the AST.
 * @param label A string representing a Fortran or Java label that must match the label of the enclosing loop statement. 
 *              If empty, will consider any loop statement.
 * @param stopOnSwitches A boolean indicating whether to return immediately when encountering a switch statement.
 *
 * @return The first enclosing loop statement found in the AST, or NULL if none is found.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle cycles in the AST. It assumes that the input statement is part of a valid AST.
 *
 * @pre s must be non-null and valid in the AST.
 * @post If an enclosing loop statement is found, it will be returned; otherwise, NULL will be returned.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse up the AST. It may overflow the stack if the input statement
 *       is deeply nested in the AST.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // some statement in the AST
 *           std::string label = "my_label"; // a Fortran or Java label
 *           bool stopOnSwitches = true;
 *           SgScopeStatement* loopStmt = SageInterface::findEnclosingLoop(stmt, label, stopOnSwitches);
 *           if (loopStmt != NULL) {
 *               // do something with the enclosing loop statement
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::getEnclosingFunctionDeclaration(SgNode * astNode, bool includingSelf)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing function declaration of a given AST node.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) to find the nearest
 * enclosing function declaration for a specified node. It uses a recursive
 * approach to navigate up the AST until it encounters an SgFunctionDeclaration
 * node, which represents a function definition in the source code.
 *
 * @param astNode The node in the AST from which to start searching for the enclosing function declaration.
 *                Must be a valid pointer to an SgNode object.
 * @param includingSelf A flag indicating whether to consider the input node itself as a potential match.
 *                      If true, the function will return the input node if it is an SgFunctionDeclaration;
 *                      otherwise, it will continue searching up the AST.
 *
 * @return The enclosing function declaration of the input node, or NULL if no such declaration is found.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input node is part of a valid AST and does not perform any error checking on the input parameters.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be a valid SgNode object.
 *
 * @post The returned value will either be an SgFunctionDeclaration node or NULL, depending on whether an enclosing function declaration was found.
 *
 * @note This function is part of the SageInterface class and provides a convenient way to access AST nodes in the context of the Sage compiler infrastructure.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode *node = ...; // some node in the AST
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration *enclosingFuncDecl = 
 *               SageInterface::getEnclosingFunctionDeclaration(node, true);
 *           if (enclosingFuncDecl != NULL) {
 *               // do something with the enclosing function declaration
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgFile* SageInterface::getEnclosingFileNode(SgNode* astNode)
 *
 * @brief Returns the enclosing file node of a given abstract syntax tree (AST) node.
 *
 * This function traverses the AST upwards from the input node to find the
 * enclosing SgFile node. It supports both C/C++ and Java languages. For Java,
 * it uses the SgJavaPackageDeclaration to get the associated class declaration,
 * which is then used to match the filename in the project's list of files.
 *
 * In case of C/C++, it simply returns the parent node if it is an SgFile, or
 * nullptr otherwise.
 *
 * @param astNode The input AST node for which the enclosing file node is to be found.
 *                Must not be NULL. If the node is a project node, the function will return NULL.
 *
 * @return The enclosing file node of the input AST node, or NULL if it cannot be found.
 *
 * @warning This function may return NULL if the input node is a project node or
 *          if the AST traversal fails to find an SgFile node.
 *
 * @note For Java support, this function uses either the attribute mechanism (when available)
 *       or the filename matching approach to find the associated SgFile node.
 */
/**
 * @fn SgInitializer* SageInterface::getInitializerOfExpression(SgExpression* n)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the initializer of a given expression.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree upwards from the input
 * expression to find its corresponding initializer. It uses a while loop to
 * recursively check the parent nodes until it encounters an SgInitializer node.
 *
 * @param n The input expression for which the initializer is to be retrieved.
 *          Must not be NULL and must be of type SgExpression or one of its subclasses.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the initializer of the input expression, or NULL if no
 *         initializer is found. It is guaranteed to be an instance of SgInitializer.
 *
 * @warning The function does not check whether the input expression has a valid
 *          initializer; it only checks whether the node is an instance of
 *          SgInitializer. If the input expression does not have a valid initializer,
 *          the returned pointer may point to an invalid or unexpected location.
 *
 * @pre The input expression must be non-NULL and must be of type SgExpression or one
 *      of its subclasses.
 *
 * @post The returned pointer is guaranteed to be either NULL or a valid instance
 *       of SgInitializer. If the function returns NULL, it means that no initializer
 *       was found for the input expression.
 *
 * @note This function uses assertions to check the validity of the input expression.
 *       It also uses a while loop to recursively traverse the abstract syntax tree,
 *       which may cause performance issues if the tree is very deep.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* expr = ...; // some expression
 *           SgInitializer* initializer = SageInterface::getInitializerOfExpression(expr);
 *           if (initializer != NULL) {
 *               // do something with the initializer
 *           } else {
 *               // handle the case where no initializer is found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgClassDefinition* SageInterface::getEnclosingClassDefinition(SgNode* astNode, const bool includingSelf)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing class definition of a given AST node.
 *
 * This function navigates through the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) to find the
 * closest enclosing class definition for the provided SgNode. The search is
 * performed recursively upwards in the AST until a matching SgClassDefinition
 * is found or the root of the tree is reached.
 *
 * @param astNode The SgNode for which to retrieve the enclosing class definition.
 *                Must not be NULL.
 * @param includingSelf Flag indicating whether to include the node itself in the search.
 *                       If true, the function checks if the provided node is a
 *                       SgClassDefinition and returns it immediately. Default: false.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the enclosing SgClassDefinition or NULL if no enclosing class definition was found.
 *
 * @warning The returned SgClassDefinition may be NULL if the search fails or if the input AST node has no valid enclosing class.
 *
 * @pre astNode must not be NULL and must represent a valid node in the AST.
 * @post The returned SgClassDefinition is guaranteed to be non-NULL only if an enclosing class definition was found.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any syntax checks on the provided AST node. It assumes that the input node represents valid code.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *             SgNode* astNode = ...; // assuming a valid SgNode is created or retrieved
 *             SageInterface sage;
 *             SgClassDefinition* enclosingClassDef = sage.getEnclosingClassDefinition(astNode);
 *             if (enclosingClassDef != NULL) {
 *                 // Process the found class definition
 *             } else {
 *                 // Handle the case where no enclosing class was found
 *             }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::getEnclosingClassDeclaration(SgNode* astNode)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing class declaration for a given abstract syntax tree node.
 *
 * This function navigates through the abstract syntax tree to find the SgClassDeclaration
 * that directly or indirectly encloses the provided SgNode. The search is performed by
 * recursively traversing up the AST until an SgClassDeclaration is encountered, which
 * may involve navigating through intermediate nodes such as SgClassDefinition.
 *
 * @param astNode The abstract syntax tree node for which to find the enclosing class declaration.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgClassDeclaration that encloses the provided astNode. If no such
 *         declaration is found, or if an error occurs during traversal, a null pointer may be returned.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform extensive error checking on its input parameter.
 *          It assumes that astNode is a valid pointer to an SgNode within a well-formed AST.
 *
 * @pre The provided astNode must be part of a valid abstract syntax tree. This function
 *      does not verify the integrity or correctness of the AST; it relies on the caller
 *      to ensure this precondition.
 *
 * @post If successful, the returned SgClassDeclaration is guaranteed to enclose the input astNode,
 *       either directly or indirectly through intermediate nodes in the AST.
 *
 * @note This function may return a null pointer if no enclosing class declaration is found. This could
 *       occur due to an error in the AST structure or because the node is not enclosed by any SgClassDeclaration.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Assuming SageInterface and necessary types are defined and accessible
 *           SgNode* astNode = ...; // Obtain an SgNode from somewhere in your code
 *           SgClassDeclaration* enclosingDecl = SageInterface::getEnclosingClassDeclaration(astNode);
 *           if (enclosingDecl != nullptr) {
 *               // Use the enclosing class declaration as needed
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle the case where no enclosing class declaration was found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgNode* SageInterface::getEnclosingExprListExp(SgNode* astNode, const bool includingSelf)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the enclosing expression list for a given AST node.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) upwards from the specified
 * node to find the nearest enclosing SgExprListExp node. If includingSelf is true,
 * it also considers the input node itself as a potential match, effectively
 * allowing the search to start from the current node instead of its parent.
 *
 * @param astNode The node in the AST from which to start searching for the enclosing expression list.
 *                Must be a valid pointer to an SgNode object.
 * @param includingSelf A flag indicating whether to consider the input node itself as
 *                       a potential match. Defaults to false if not specified.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the nearest enclosing SgExprListExp node, or NULL if no such
 *         node is found in the AST above the given node.
 *
 * @warning The search may return NULL if the input node is not part of an expression list,
 *          or if includingSelf is false and the node itself is not an expression list.
 *
 * @pre astNode must be a valid, non-NULL pointer to an SgNode object in the AST.
 *
 * @post The returned node will be the nearest enclosing SgExprListExp node above the
 *       given astNode in the AST, or NULL if no such node exists.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the AST upwards from
 *       the input node. It assumes that the input node is part of a valid AST structure.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* astNode = ...; // obtain an AST node somehow
 *           bool includingSelf = true;
 *           SgExprListExp* enclosingList = SageInterface::getEnclosingExprListExp(astNode, includingSelf);
 *           if (enclosingList != NULL) {
 *               // do something with the enclosing expression list
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::isInSubTree(SgExpression* subtree, SgExpression* exp)
 *
 * @brief Checks if an expression is contained within a given subtree.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) of the given subtree
 * and checks if the specified expression is present within it. It utilizes a
 * visitor pattern to perform the traversal efficiently.
 *
 * The function uses a recursive approach to explore the AST, ensuring that all
 * nodes are examined thoroughly. If the target expression is found during the
 * traversal, the function immediately returns true; otherwise, it returns false.
 *
 * @param subtree The root node of the subtree to search within.
 * @param exp The target expression to search for within the subtree.
 *
 * @return True if the target expression is found within the subtree; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the provided subtree and expression are
 *          valid and non-null. Failure to meet these preconditions may result in
 *          undefined behavior or crashes.
 *
 * @pre The input subtree and expression must be valid, non-null pointers.
 *
 * @post The function will not modify the input subtree or expression.
 *
 * @note This function has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of nodes
 *       in the subtree. It is designed to be efficient for large ASTs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* subtree = ...; // Initialize subtree
 *           SgExpression* exp = ...;    // Initialize target expression
 *
 *           bool result = SageInterface::isInSubTree(subtree, exp);
 *
 *           if (result) {
 *               std::cout << "Target expression found within the subtree." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "Target expression not found within the subtree." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgFunctionDeclaration* SageInterface::getFunctionDeclaration (SgFunctionCallExp* functionCallExp)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the associated function declaration for a given function call expression.
 *
 * This function takes a pointer to an SgFunctionCallExp as input and returns a pointer to the
 * corresponding SgFunctionDeclaration. It supports extraction of the associated function
 * declaration from various types of expressions, including SgDotExp, SgFunctionRefExp, and
 * SgArrowExp.
 *
 * @param functionCallExp A pointer to an SgFunctionCallExp representing the function call
 *                        expression for which the associated function declaration is to be retrieved.
 *                        Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgFunctionDeclaration associated with the input function call
 *         expression, or NULL if no such declaration can be found.
 *
 * @warning The function may return NULL if the input function call expression does not have an
 *          associated function declaration, or if the declaration cannot be resolved due to
 *          ambiguity or other issues.
 *
 * @pre The input functionCallExp must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned SgFunctionDeclaration pointer is valid and points to a valid object,
 *       or NULL is returned to indicate that no such declaration can be found.
 *
 * @note This function uses various ROSE assertions to ensure the validity of the input
 *       expression and the correctness of the returned declaration. It also handles cases
 *       where the input expression may not have an associated function declaration, returning
 *       NULL in such cases.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgFunctionCallExp* functionCallExp = ...; // assume some valid function call expression
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* declaration = SageInterface::getFunctionDeclaration(functionCallExp);
 *           if (declaration != NULL) {
 *             // process the retrieved function declaration
 *           } else {
 *             // handle the case where no declaration can be found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isDataMemberReference(SgVarRefExp* varRefExp)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a variable reference expression is a data member reference.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given variable reference expression
 * represents a data member reference, which requires special handling for name
 * qualification. It checks the parent node of the variable reference expression
 * to ensure it is either an arrow or dot expression, and then verifies that the
 * referenced variable is not statically declared.
 *
 * @param varRefExp The variable reference expression to be checked.
 *                  Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the variable reference expression represents a data member reference,
 *         false otherwise. Returns false for static data members or expressions used in sizeof with objectless_nonstatic_data_member_reference == true.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input variable reference expression is valid and has been properly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input variable reference expression must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the input variable reference expression represents a data member reference.
 *
 * @note This function is used to support the name qualification of data member references separate from name qualification
 *       of variables that might be members. It also handles pointer-to-member expressions and static data members differently.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgVarRefExp* varRefExp = ...; // Initialize the variable reference expression
 *           bool isDataMemberReference = SageInterface::isDataMemberReference(varRefExp);
 *           if (isDataMemberReference) {
 *               // Handle data member reference case
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle non-data member reference case
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isAddressTaken(SgExpression* refExp)
 *
 * @brief Determines whether an address-of operator is taken on a given SgExpression.
 *
 * This function checks if the input expression is either an SgVarRefExp or an
 * SgMemberFunctionRefExp and then verifies if its parent node is an SgAddressOfOp,
 * indicating that the address of the variable or member function reference is being taken.
 *
 * @param refExp The input SgExpression, which must be a valid pointer to either
 *               an SgVarRefExp or an SgMemberFunctionRefExp. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the address-of operator is taken on the input expression,
 *         false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle cases where the input expression is
 *          neither an SgVarRefExp nor an SgMemberFunctionRefExp.
 *
 * @pre The input expression refExp must be a valid pointer to either an
 *      SgVarRefExp or an SgMemberFunctionRefExp. It must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the address-of operator is taken on the
 *       input expression, and false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input expression has already been checked
 *       for validity using ROSE_ASSERT statements.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgVarRefExp* varRefExp = ...;
 *           bool isAddressTaken = SageInterface::isAddressTaken(varRefExp);
 *           if (isAddressTaken) {
 *               // handle the case where address-of operator is taken
 *           } else {
 *               // handle the case where address-of operator is not taken
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isMemberFunctionMemberReference(SgMemberFunctionRefExp* memberFunctionRefExp)
 *
 * @brief Determines if a given SgMemberFunctionRefExp is a reference to a non-static member function of a class.
 *
 * This function checks whether the provided SgMemberFunctionRefExp refers to a non-static member function 
 * of a class. It performs this check by examining the parent node of the SgMemberFunctionRefExp and verifying 
 * that it is either an SgArrowExp or an SgDotExp, indicating a data member access. Additionally, it checks if 
 * the symbol associated with the SgMemberFunctionRefExp is not statically declared.
 *
 * @param memberFunctionRefExp A pointer to the SgMemberFunctionRefExp to be checked. This parameter must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the SgMemberFunctionRefExp refers to a non-static member function of a class, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning The result may be incorrect if the provided SgMemberFunctionRefExp is not properly initialized or 
 *          if the AST is not correctly constructed.
 *
 * @pre The input parameter memberFunctionRefExp must not be NULL and must point to a valid SgMemberFunctionRefExp object.
 *
 * @post None
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the provided SgMemberFunctionRefExp has already been parsed and is part of 
 *       a valid AST. If this assumption does not hold, the result may be incorrect or undefined.
 *
 * @example The following example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Assume 'memberFunctionRefExp' is a pointer to a SgMemberFunctionRefExp object
 *           bool isNonStaticMember = SageInterface::isMemberFunctionMemberReference(memberFunctionRefExp);
 *           if (isNonStaticMember)
 *             {
 *               printf("The member function reference refers to a non-static member function.\n");
 *             }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn std::list<SgClassType*> getReturnTypeChain(SgName symbolName, SgSymbolReference referenceSymbol,                                       std::list<SgClassType*> classChain, SgBinaryOp* binaryOperator)
 * @brief This function determines the return type chain for a given binary operator.
 *
 * It takes in several parameters: symbolName (the name of the symbol), referenceSymbol (a reference to the symbol),
 * classChain (a list of SgClassType pointers representing the class chain), and binaryOperator (a pointer to an
 * SgBinaryOp).
 *
 * The function iterates through the class chain, checking for ambiguities in the definition of each class. If an
 * ambiguity is detected, it adds the current class type to the saveList.
 *
 * Finally, it returns the returnTypeChain, which is a list of SgClassType pointers representing the return type chain.
 *
 * @param symbolName The name of the symbol.
 * @param referenceSymbol A reference to the symbol.
 * @param classChain A list of SgClassType pointers representing the class chain.
 * @param binaryOperator A pointer to an SgBinaryOp.
 * @return returnTypeChain A list of SgClassType pointers representing the return type chain.
 */

/**
 * @fn std::set<SgNode*> SageInterface::getFrontendSpecificNodes()
 *
 * @brief Retrieves a set of frontend-specific nodes from the memory pool.
 *
 * This function traverses the memory pool to identify frontend-specific nodes
 * and returns them as a set. It uses a recursive traversal algorithm to collect
 * all relevant nodes.
 *
 * The traversal process involves visiting each node in the memory pool, checking
 * if it is frontend-specific or not, and collecting the specific nodes while ignoring
 * non-specific ones. The function also handles cases where a node's frontend specificity
 * cannot be determined directly from its file information.
 *
 * @param None
 *
 * @return A set of SgNode pointers representing the frontend-specific nodes in the memory pool.
 *
 * @warning This function does not modify the original memory pool or any of its contents.
 *
 * @pre The memory pool must have been initialized and populated with nodes before calling this function.
 *
 * @post The returned set of nodes will contain only frontend-specific nodes, excluding non-specific ones.
 *
 * @note The traversal algorithm used in this function has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the memory pool.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           std::set<SgNode*> specificNodes = sage.getFrontendSpecificNodes();
 *           for (std::set<SgNode*>::iterator i = specificNodes.begin(); i != specificNodes.end(); ++i) {
 *             // Process the frontend-specific node here
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::outputSharedNodes(SgNode* node)
 *
 * @brief Displays shared nodes in the AST for debugging purposes.
 *
 * This function is used to traverse the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) and identify
 * shared nodes. It utilizes a custom traversal class, OutputSharedNodesTraversal,
 * which visits each node in the AST and checks if it has associated file information.
 * If the node's file information indicates that it is shared, the node's address and
 * class name are printed to the console for debugging purposes.
 *
 * @param node The root node of the AST to be traversed. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error handling on the input parameter.
 *          It assumes that the provided node is valid and can be safely traversed.
 *
 * @pre The input node must not be NULL, and it must represent a valid AST.
 *
 * @post The shared nodes in the AST will have been printed to the console for debugging purposes.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive traversal approach to visit all nodes in the AST.
 *       It does not modify the AST or its contents in any way.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root node of the AST
 *           SageInterface::outputSharedNodes(root);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::displayScope(SgScopeStatement* scope)
 *
 * @brief Displays the contents of a given SgScopeStatement for debugging purposes.
 *
 * This function outputs the statements contained within a provided SgScopeStatement
 * to the console, including their class names and names. It is intended for use in
 * debugging scenarios where visual inspection of the scope's contents is necessary.
 *
 * @param scope The SgScopeStatement whose contents are to be displayed. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function uses printf() statements to output its results, which may not be suitable for all environments or use cases.
 *
 * @pre The provided SgScopeStatement must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The contents of the scope are displayed on the console.
 *
 * @note This function is primarily intended for debugging purposes and should not be relied upon in production code.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* myScope = ...; // initialize myScope
 *           SageInterface::displayScope(myScope);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgGlobal* SageInterface::getFirstGlobalScope(SgProject* project)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the first global scope from a given SgProject.
 *
 * This function returns a pointer to the first global scope in the provided
 * SgProject. It is primarily used to prepare for Abstract Syntax Tree (AST)
 * construction under the global scope.
 *
 * The function iterates over the list of files in the project and returns the
 * global scope of the first file. If the project or its file list is empty, the
 * function asserts and terminates.
 *
 * @param project A pointer to an SgProject object representing the project from which to retrieve the first global scope.
 *                 Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the first global scope in the provided project. Returns NULL if the project or its file list is empty.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error checking on the input project beyond ensuring it is not NULL.
 *
 * @pre The input project must not be NULL and must have at least one file in its file list.
 *
 * @post The returned global scope is guaranteed to be non-NULL if the preconditions are met.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to iterate over the files in the project, but this recursion is limited to the number of files in the project and does not pose a risk of stack overflow.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgProject* project = ...; // Create or retrieve an SgProject object
 *           SgGlobal* globalScope = SageInterface::getFirstGlobalScope(project);
 *           if (globalScope != NULL) {
 *               // Use the retrieved global scope as needed
 *           } else {
 *               // Handle the case where the project or its file list is empty
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::getLastStatement(SgScopeStatement *scope)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the last statement within a given scope.
 *
 * This function traverses the statements within a provided scope and returns
 * the last statement found. The function handles scopes containing only
 * declarations, as well as those with mixed content.
 *
 * @param scope A pointer to an SgScopeStatement representing the scope to search.
 *              Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the last SgStatement within the given scope, or NULL if no statements are found.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input scope is valid and properly constructed.
 *          It does not perform any additional checks on the scope's contents.
 *
 * @pre The input scope must be a valid, non-NULL pointer to an SgScopeStatement.
 *
 * @post If a statement is found within the scope, it will be returned as-is; otherwise, NULL will be returned.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to check for a valid input scope.
 *       It also employs references to avoid unnecessary copies of lists.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* myScope = ...; // Initialize or obtain a scope
 *           SgStatement* lastStmt = SageInterface::getLastStatement(myScope);
 *           if (lastStmt != NULL) {
 *             // Process the last statement found within the scope
 *           } else {
 *             // Handle the case where no statements were found
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::getFirstStatement(SgScopeStatement *scope, bool includingCompilerGenerated/*=false*/)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the first statement within a given scope.
 *
 * This function searches for the first statement in the provided scope and returns it. 
 * It considers both declaration statements and non-declaration statements, depending on the value of the includingCompilerGenerated parameter.
 *
 * If the scope contains only declarations, the function iterates through them to find the first one that meets the criteria.
 * Otherwise, it searches through the list of statements in the scope to find the first valid statement.
 *
 * The search process skips compiler-generated statements unless explicitly instructed to include them.
 *
 * @param scope The SgScopeStatement object representing the scope within which to search for the first statement.
 *              Must be a non-null pointer to a valid SgScopeStatement object.
 * @param includingCompilerGenerated A boolean flag indicating whether to consider compiler-generated statements in the search. 
 *                                    Default value is false, meaning that such statements are skipped by default.
 *
 * @return The first statement found within the given scope according to the specified criteria, or NULL if no valid statement is found.
 *
 * @warning This function may return NULL if the input scope does not contain any valid statements.
 *
 * @pre The provided SgScopeStatement object must be non-null and represent a valid scope.
 *
 * @post The returned statement pointer will either point to a valid SgStatement object or be NULL, depending on whether a valid statement was found in the scope.
 *
 * @note This function has been optimized for performance by minimizing unnecessary copies of lists and using more efficient search algorithms.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an SgScopeStatement object representing the desired scope
 *           SgScopeStatement* myScope = ...;
 *           
 *           // Call getFirstStatement() with the default parameter value (false)
 *           SgStatement* firstStmt = SageInterface::getFirstStatement(myScope);
 *
 *           // Alternatively, call getFirstStatement() with includingCompilerGenerated set to true
 *           SgStatement* firstStmtWithCG = SageInterface::getFirstStatement(myScope, true);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgFunctionDeclaration* SageInterface::findFirstDefiningFunctionDecl(SgScopeStatement* scope)
 *
 * @brief Finds the first defining function declaration within a given scope.
 *
 * This function traverses the declarations or statements within a scope to find
 * the first defining function declaration. A defining function declaration is one
 * where the declaration itself defines the function, rather than just declaring it.
 *
 * The search is limited to declarations or statements that are in the same file as
 * the scope, or are part of a transformation and have output in code generation.
 *
 * @param scope The scope within which to search for the defining function declaration.
 *              Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The first defining function declaration found within the scope, or NULL if none is found.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input scope is valid and has been properly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input scope must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned function declaration is guaranteed to be a defining declaration,
 *       but may be NULL if no such declaration was found within the scope.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse the declarations or statements
 *       within the scope. It also relies on the Sg_File_Info class to determine whether
 *       a declaration or statement is in the same file as the scope, or is part of a transformation.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = ...; // obtain a valid scope
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* funcDecl = SageInterface::findFirstDefiningFunctionDecl(scope);
 *           if (funcDecl != NULL) {
 *             cout << "Found defining function declaration: " << funcDecl->unparseToString() << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "No defining function declaration found within the scope." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::getNextStatement(SgStatement * currentStmt)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the next statement in the abstract syntax tree (AST) from a given statement.
 *
 * This function is used to traverse the AST by retrieving the next statement
 * from a given statement. It relies on the implementation provided by the ROSE 
 * namespace, specifically the getNextStatement() function defined in src/roseSupport/utility_functions.C.
 *
 * @param currentStmt The current statement in the AST from which to retrieve the next statement.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the next SgStatement in the AST. If there is no next statement,
 *         a null pointer may be returned (implementation-dependent).
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error checking on its input parameter.
 *          It assumes that currentStmt is a valid, non-null pointer to an SgStatement object.
 *
 * @pre The AST has been properly constructed and populated with statements.
 *
 * @post The returned statement is the next one in sequence from the given statement,
 *       or null if there are no more statements.
 *
 * @note This function does not modify the AST; it only traverses it to find the next statement.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* currentStmt = ...; // obtain a valid statement pointer
 *           SgStatement* nextStmt = SageInterface::getNextStatement(currentStmt);
 *           if (nextStmt != NULL) {
 *               // process the next statement as needed
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgStatement* SageInterface::getPreviousStatement(SgStatement * currentStmt, bool climbOutScope /*= true*/)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the previous statement in the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) given a current statement.
 *
 * This function navigates the AST to find the preceding statement of the provided current statement. It utilizes the Rose framework's functionality for this purpose.
 *
 * The navigation process can optionally be restricted within the scope boundaries by setting the climbOutScope parameter accordingly.
 *
 * @param currentStmt The current SgStatement object from which to start searching for its previous statement in the AST.
 *                     Must not be NULL and should represent a valid node in the AST.
 * @param climbOutScope Optional boolean flag indicating whether to continue searching outside of the current scope boundaries. Defaults to true, meaning it will search beyond the current scope if necessary.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgStatement object representing the previous statement in the AST relative to the provided currentStmt. Returns NULL if no preceding statement is found or if currentStmt is not a valid node in the AST.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform checks on the validity of the provided currentStmt beyond being non-NULL. It assumes that currentStmt is a part of a well-formed AST and that its internal state allows for navigation.
 *
 * @pre The currentStmt must be a valid SgStatement object within an existing AST, and if climbOutScope is false, it should not represent a boundary condition where searching outside the scope would be required to find a preceding statement.
 *
 * @post The returned pointer (if not NULL) points to a valid SgStatement in the AST that precedes the original currentStmt. If NULL is returned, no such preceding statement exists or could be found under the given conditions.
 *
 * @note This function internally delegates its functionality to Rose::getPreviousStatement for actual navigation and result computation.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* currentStmt = ...; // Assume a valid statement is obtained
 *           bool climbOutScope = true;
 *           SgStatement* previousStmt = SageInterface::getPreviousStatement(currentStmt, climbOutScope);
 *           if (previousStmt != NULL) {
 *             // Process or utilize the previous statement as needed
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::listHeaderFiles ( SgIncludeFile* includeFile )
 *
 * @brief Lists header files in a given include file.
 *
 * This function performs a pre-order traversal of the abstract syntax tree
 * rooted at the given include file, printing information about header files 
 * encountered during the traversal. The traversal is performed using an 
 * instance of the PrefixTraversal class, which visits each node in the tree 
 * and prints its class name and filename if it represents an include file.
 *
 * @param includeFile The include file to be traversed.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error checking on the input 
 *          parameter. It is assumed that the input include file is valid and 
 *          correctly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input include file must be a valid, non-null pointer.
 *
 * @post The function prints information about header files encountered during
 *       the traversal to the standard output stream.
 *
 * @note This function uses an instance of the PrefixTraversal class to perform
 *       the pre-order traversal. The PrefixTraversal class is defined locally 
 *       within this function and is not accessible outside its scope.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgIncludeFile* includeFile = ...; // initialize include file
 *           SageInterface interface;
 *           interface.listHeaderFiles(includeFile);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::scopeHasStatementsFromSameFile(SgScopeStatement* scope)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given scope contains statements from the same file.
 *
 * This function determines whether a scope has any statements that must be unparsed
 * because they originate from the same file as the scope. It supports token-based
 * unparsing when used with header files and helps identify when a statement from a
 * header file may contain a transformation that requires individual unparsing of
 * statements.
 *
 * @param scope The SgScopeStatement object to check for statements from the same file.
 *              Must not be NULL. If the scope is empty or contains only declarations,
 *              it will return false unless a statement with the same file ID is found.
 *
 * @return True if there is at least one statement in the scope that has to be unparsed
 *         (i.e., originates from the same file as the scope), false otherwise. If the
 *         scope contains only declarations or no statements, it will return false.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgScopeStatement object is valid and
 *          not NULL. Passing an invalid or NULL pointer may result in undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input SgScopeStatement object must be a valid, non-NULL pointer to a scope.
 *
 * @post If the function returns true, it guarantees that there is at least one statement
 *       in the scope with the same file ID as the scope. If it returns false, it does not
 *       guarantee the absence of such statements but indicates that none were found.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to check each statement in the scope.
 *       It first checks if the scope contains only declarations and then iterates over
 *       the declaration list or statement list accordingly. If it finds a statement with
 *       the same file ID as the scope, it immediately returns true.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = ...; // obtain a valid SgScopeStatement object
 *           bool has_same_file_statements = SageInterface::scopeHasStatementsFromSameFile(scope);
 *           if (has_same_file_statements) {
 *               std::cout << "The scope contains statements from the same file." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The scope does not contain statements from the same file." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isEqualToIntConst(SgExpression* e, int value)
 *
 * @brief Checks if an SgExpression is equal to a given integer constant.
 *
 * This function verifies whether the provided SgExpression represents an
 * integer constant that is equal to the specified integer value. It relies on
 * the isSgIntVal() function to check if the expression is indeed an integer
 * constant and then compares its value with the given integer.
 *
 * @param e The SgExpression to be checked for equality with the integer constant.
 *         Must be a valid pointer to an SgExpression object.
 *
 * @param value The integer constant against which the SgExpression's value is compared.
 *              This parameter can take any integer value.
 *
 * @return True if the SgExpression represents an integer constant equal to the given value, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle cases where the expression may evaluate
 *          to a non-integer or null value. Ensure proper input validation before calling this function.
 *
 * @pre The provided SgExpression pointer must be valid and not null.
 *
 * @post The return value is determined solely by the comparison between the
 *       integer constant represented by the SgExpression (if any) and the given value.
 *
 * @note This function uses the isSgIntVal() function to verify if the expression is an
 *       integer constant. If this check fails, the function immediately returns false.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *              SgExpression* expr = ...; // Initialize with a valid SgExpression
 *              int valueToCompare = 10;
 *              if (SageInterface::isEqualToIntConst(expr, valueToCompare)) {
 *                  // The expression represents an integer constant equal to valueToCompare
 *              } else {
 *                  // Either the expression is not an integer constant or its value differs from valueToCompare
 *              }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isSameFunction(SgFunctionDeclaration* func1, SgFunctionDeclaration* func2)
 *
 * @brief Checks if two given functions are the same.
 *
 * This function compares two SgFunctionDeclaration objects to determine if they represent
 * the same function in different contexts. The comparison is based on the language type and
 * the name or qualified name of the functions.
 *
 * @param func1 The first SgFunctionDeclaration object to compare.
 * @param func2 The second SgFunctionDeclaration object to compare.
 *
 * @return True if the two functions are considered the same, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses language-specific rules for comparing function names and may not work
 *       correctly for all languages or edge cases. It is intended for use within the ROSE compiler
 *       infrastructure.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform a deep comparison of the functions' contents, but rather
 *          relies on their declarations and language-specific naming conventions.
 *
 * @pre Both func1 and func2 must be non-null pointers to valid SgFunctionDeclaration objects.
 *
 * @post The result of the comparison is returned as a boolean value.
 *
 * @note This function uses ROSE_ASSERT to ensure that both input parameters are non-null.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create two SgFunctionDeclaration objects
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* func1 = ...;
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* func2 = ...;
 *
 *           // Compare the functions using isSameFunction()
 *           bool result = SageInterface::isSameFunction(func1, func2);
 *
 *           // Use the result as needed in your code
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isLastStatement(SgStatement* stmt)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given statement is the last statement in its scope.
 *
 * This function determines whether a provided SgStatement object represents
 * the last statement within its enclosing SgScopeStatement. It does this by 
 * first retrieving the scope of the input statement, then generating a list of
 * statements within that scope and checking if the input statement is the 
 * last one in the list.
 *
 * @param stmt The SgStatement object to be checked. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the input statement is the last statement in its scope,
 *         false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input statement has a valid
 *          scope and does not perform extensive error checking.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must not be NULL and must have a valid scope.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the statement is last in its scope,
 *       false otherwise. No side effects are guaranteed.
 *
 * @note This function uses the generateStatementList() method of SgScopeStatement
 *       to get the list of statements, which may be an expensive operation for
 *       large scopes.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // assume some statement is created or retrieved
 *           if (sage.isLastStatement(stmt)) {
 *             cout << "The statement is the last one in its scope." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "The statement is not the last one in its scope." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::deleteAST(SgNode* n) 
 * @brief Deletes an abstract syntax tree (AST) in a post-order traversal.
 *
 * This function is used to delete an entire AST by traversing it in post-order
 * and deleting each node. It takes into account the different types of nodes
 * that can be present in the AST, such as SgFunctionDeclaration,
 * SgClassDefinition, SgTemplateInstantiationDecl, etc.
 *
 * @param n The root node of the AST to delete.
 */

/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::deleteAllNodes()
 *
 * @brief Deletes all nodes in the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) managed by the SageInterface.
 *
 * This function is responsible for deleting all IR nodes in the AST, including those 
 * that are not currently reachable from any SgNode pointer. It does this by traversing 
 * the memory pool used to manage the IR nodes and calling the delete operator on each 
 * node found. Additionally, it clears all memory pools after deletion.
 *
 * @param None
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function will cause undefined behavior if any SgNode pointers are accessed 
 *          after this function is called, as they will no longer be valid.
 *
 * @pre The SageInterface instance must have been properly initialized before calling this function.
 *
 * @post All IR nodes in the AST managed by the SageInterface will be deleted and their memory freed.
 *       Any SgNode pointers previously obtained from the SageInterface will be invalid after this function is called.
 *
 * @note This function uses a custom traversal class, MyTraversal, to visit all IR nodes in the AST. 
 *       It also calls the delete operator on each node found during traversal and clears all memory pools afterwards.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           // ... perform some operations on the AST ...
 *           sage.deleteAllNodes();
 *          @endcode
 */

/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::deleteExpressionTreeWithOriginalExpressionSubtrees(SgNode* root)
 *
 * @brief Deletes an expression tree along with any original expression subtrees.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) rooted at the given node,
 * deletes each node, and also recursively deletes any original expression subtrees
 * associated with SgExpression nodes. The traversal is performed in post-order to
 * ensure that child nodes are deleted before their parents.
 *
 * @param root The root node of the AST to be deleted. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function deletes the entire AST rooted at the given node, which may have unintended consequences if not used carefully.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be a valid SgNode and must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The entire AST rooted at the input node is deleted, including any original expression subtrees.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the AST, which may lead to stack overflow for very large trees.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root node of the AST to be deleted
 *           SageInterface::deleteExpressionTreeWithOriginalExpressionSubtrees(root);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::moveStatementsBetweenBlocks ( SgBasicBlock* sourceBlock, SgBasicBlock* targetBlock )
 *
 * @brief Moves statements between two basic blocks.
 *
 * This function moves statements from the source block to the target block. 
 * It uses the moveStatementsBetweenScopes function internally to perform the actual movement of statements.
 *
 * @param sourceBlock The source basic block from which the statements are to be moved.
 *                     Must not be NULL and must have at least one statement.
 *
 * @param targetBlock The target basic block to which the statements are to be moved.
 *                     Must not be NULL and must be different from the source block.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the internal state of the source and target blocks. 
 *          Use with caution and ensure that the blocks are in a valid state before calling this function.
 *
 * @pre The source and target blocks must exist and be non-NULL. 
 *      The source block must have at least one statement to move.
 *
 * @post The statements from the source block will be moved to the target block. 
 *       The source block may become empty after the movement of statements.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any validation on the blocks or their contents. 
 *       It assumes that the input is valid and will produce undefined behavior if the preconditions are not met.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgBasicBlock* sourceBlock = ...;  // Initialize source block with statements
 *           SgBasicBlock* targetBlock = ...;  // Initialize target block
 *           SageInterface::moveStatementsBetweenBlocks(sourceBlock, targetBlock);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::moveStatementsBetweenBlocks(SgAdaPackageSpec* sourceBlock, SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* targetBlock)
 *
 * @brief Moves statements between blocks in the abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * This function relocates statements from a source block to a target block in the
 * abstract syntax tree. It is used to reorganize the structure of the code and
 * ensure that declarations are properly nested within scopes.
 *
 * The function first moves declarations between scopes using the moveDeclarationsBetweenScopes
 * helper function. Then, it creates alias symbols for global definitions by calling
 * createAliasSymbols on the parent node of the target block, which is expected to be an
 * SgNamespaceDeclarationStatement.
 *
 * @param sourceBlock The source block from which statements are moved. Must not be NULL.
 * @param targetBlock The target block to which statements are moved. Must not be NULL and must be a valid SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement.
 *
 * @return None (void function)
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the abstract syntax tree in-place and does not perform any checks for validity of the resulting structure.
 *
 * @pre The sourceBlock and targetBlock parameters must not be NULL and must be valid nodes in the abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * @post The statements from the source block have been moved to the target block, and alias symbols have been created for global definitions if applicable.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input blocks are properly nested within scopes and does not perform any checks for this condition.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgAdaPackageSpec* packageSpec = ...; // obtain a valid package specification
 *           SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* namespaceDef = ...; // obtain a valid namespace definition statement
 *           SageInterface::moveStatementsBetweenBlocks(packageSpec, namespaceDef);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::moveStatementsBetweenBlocks(SgAdaPackageBody* sourceBlock, SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* targetBlock)
 *
 * @brief Moves statements between two blocks in the AST.
 *
 * This function migrates statements from a source block to a target block in the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).
 * It utilizes the moveStatementsBetweenScopes function to perform the actual migration and then creates alias symbols
 * in the global definition if the target block is an SgNamespaceDeclarationStatement.
 *
 * @param sourceBlock The source block from which statements are to be moved. Must be a valid SgAdaPackageBody pointer.
 * @param targetBlock The target block to which statements are to be moved. Must be a valid SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement pointer.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the AST and may have unintended consequences if not used carefully.
 *
 * @pre sourceBlock and targetBlock must be valid pointers to existing blocks in the AST.
 *
 * @post The statements from the source block are moved to the target block, and alias symbols are created in the global definition if necessary.
 *
 * @note This function is part of the SageInterface class and should be used in conjunction with other functions that manipulate the AST.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgAdaPackageBody* sourceBlock = ...; // initialize source block
 *           SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* targetBlock = ...; // initialize target block
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           sage.moveStatementsBetweenBlocks(sourceBlock, targetBlock);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::moveStatementsBetweenBlocks(SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* sourceBlock, SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* targetBlock)
 *
 * @brief Moves statements between two blocks in the AST.
 *
 * This function relocates statements from one block to another within the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).
 * It utilizes a helper function moveDeclarationsBetweenScopes() to facilitate this process.
 * The relocation is achieved by updating the scope information of the declarations involved.
 *
 * @param sourceBlock The block from which the statements are to be moved. Must not be NULL.
 * @param targetBlock The block to which the statements are to be moved. Must not be NULL and should be different from sourceBlock.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the AST structure and does not perform any checks for validity or consistency of the resulting AST.
 *
 * @pre The sourceBlock and targetBlock must exist in the AST, and they should represent valid blocks (i.e., SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement).
 *
 * @post After the relocation, the statements originally under sourceBlock will be moved to targetBlock. Other parts of the AST remain unchanged.
 *
 * @note This function does not handle any potential side effects or conflicts arising from moving declarations between scopes.
 *
 * @example
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* src = ...; // initialize source block
 *           SgNamespaceDefinitionStatement* tgt = ...; // initialize target block
 *           sage.moveStatementsBetweenBlocks(src, tgt);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isLambdaFunction(SgFunctionDeclaration* func)
 *
 * @brief Determines whether a given SgFunctionDeclaration represents a lambda function.
 *
 * This function checks if the provided SgFunctionDeclaration is a lambda function
 * by examining its parent node and verifying if it matches with the lambda function
 * of a SgLambdaExp node. It returns true if the input function declaration corresponds
 * to a lambda function, false otherwise.
 *
 * @param func The SgFunctionDeclaration to be checked for being a lambda function.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the input function declaration represents a lambda function, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgFunctionDeclaration has a valid parent node.
 *          If this assumption is violated, undefined behavior may occur.
 *
 * @pre The input SgFunctionDeclaration must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the input function declaration represents a lambda function.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to enforce its preconditions. 
 *       If these assertions fail, an error message is printed and the program terminates.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* funcDecl = ...; // assume a valid SgFunctionDeclaration is created
 *           if (SageInterface::isLambdaFunction(funcDecl)) {
 *             cout << "The function declaration represents a lambda function." << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cout << "The function declaration does not represent a lambda function." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isLambdaCapturedVariable(SgVarRefExp* varRef)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a variable reference expression is a captured variable in a lambda function.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given SgVarRefExp node represents a captured
 * variable within the context of a lambda function. It does this by traversing the
 * abstract syntax tree (AST) upwards from the input varRef to identify if it's part
 * of a lambda closure class, which would indicate that the variable is indeed captured.
 *
 * @param varRef The SgVarRefExp node representing the variable reference expression
 *               to be checked. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the variable is captured in a lambda function; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function may not work correctly when compiled with MSVC due to known issues.
 *
 * @pre The input varRef must not be NULL and should represent a valid SgVarRefExp node.
 *
 * @post None
 *
 * @note This function relies on the ROSE framework's AST traversal capabilities and assertions
 *       for correctness.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface sage;
