 *
 * This function checks the variant type of the input SgScopeStatement to determine
 * whether it has a simple children list. A simple children list is defined as one that
 * contains only basic blocks, class definitions, function definitions, global statements,
 * or namespace definition statements.
 *
 * The function uses a switch statement to check the variant type of the input scope.
 * If the variant type matches one of the cases for simple children lists, the function returns true.
 * Otherwise, it returns false. If the variant type is not recognized or handled, an error message
 * is printed to the console.
 *
 * @param scope The SgScopeStatement to check for a simple children list. Must be non-null.
 *
 * @return True if the input scope has a simple children list, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any checks on the contents of the children list,
 *       it only checks the type of the scope statement.
 *
 * @warning If the variant type of the input scope is not recognized or handled, an error message
 *          will be printed to the console and the function will return false.
 *
 * @pre The input scope must be non-null.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the input scope has a simple children list, false otherwise.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = ...; // assume some valid scope statement
 *           bool hasSimpleChildren = SageInterface::hasSimpleChildrenList(scope);
