/**
 * @fn std::set<SgStatement*> SageInterface::collectTransformedStatements(SgNode* node)
 *
 * @brief Collects statements marked as transformed in a given AST.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) rooted at the input
 * node and identifies all statements that have been marked as transformed.
 * The traversal is performed using a preorder strategy, visiting each node
 * before its children. A statement is considered transformed if it has been
 * explicitly flagged as such during some previous analysis or transformation
 * phase.
 *
 * @param node The root node of the AST to be traversed for collecting transformed statements.
 *
 * @return A set of SgStatement pointers representing all statements in the input
 *         AST that have been marked as transformed. If no such statements are found,
 *         an empty set is returned.
 *
 * Warnings:
 * - The function does not perform any error checking on the input node; it is
 *   assumed to be a valid pointer to an SgNode object representing the root of
 *   a well-formed AST.
 *
 * Preconditions:
 * - The input node must represent the root of a well-formed AST.
 *
 * Postconditions:
 * - All statements marked as transformed in the input AST have been collected
 *   and returned in the output set.
 *
 * Notes:
 * - This function is primarily intended for debugging purposes, allowing users to
 *   inspect which statements in an AST have undergone some transformation or
 *   analysis phase.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...;  // obtain the root node of some AST
 *           std::set<SgStatement*> transformedStmts;
 *           transformedStmts = SageInterface::collectTransformedStatements(root);
 *           for (auto stmt : transformedStmts) {
 *             // process each transformed statement as needed
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn std::set<SgStatement*> SageInterface::collectModifiedStatements(SgNode* node)
 *
 * @brief Collects statements marked as modified in a given Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) node.
 *
 * This function traverses the AST rooted at the input node and identifies all statements that have been marked as modified. 
 * The traversal is performed using a preorder strategy, ensuring that parent nodes are visited before their children. 
 * Statements found to be modified are accumulated in a set data structure and returned upon completion of the traversal.
 *
 * @param node The root node of the AST subtree to be traversed for modified statements.
 *
 * @return A set of SgStatement pointers representing the statements marked as modified within the input AST subtree.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any validation on the input node. It is assumed that the caller has ensured the node is a valid part of an AST.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be a non-null pointer to a valid SgNode object, typically obtained from parsing source code using the ROSE compiler infrastructure.
 *
 * @post The returned set contains all statements marked as modified within the subtree rooted at the input node. 
 *       If no modified statements are found, an empty set is returned.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse the AST and may cause stack overflow for very deep trees.
 *       Additionally, this function does not handle cycles in the AST; it assumes a tree structure without any back edges.
 *
 * @example The following example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Assuming we have an SgNode* representing a parsed source file
 *           SgNode* rootNode = parseSourceFile("example.cpp");
 *
 *           // Create an instance of the SageInterface class
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *
 *           // Call the collectModifiedStatements function to gather modified statements
 *           std::set<SgStatement*> modifiedStmts = sage.collectModifiedStatements(rootNode);
 *
 *           // Process the modified statements as needed
 *           for (std::set<SgStatement*>::iterator it = modifiedStmts.begin(); it != modifiedStmts.end(); ++it) {
 *               SgStatement* stmt = *it;
 *               // Handle the statement...
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn std::set<SgLocatedNode*> SageInterface::collectModifiedLocatedNodes(SgNode* node)
 *
 * @brief Collects and returns a set of SgLocatedNodes that have been marked as modified.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) rooted at the given node, 
 * identifying all nodes that have their isModified flag set to true. These nodes are
 * collected into a set, which is then returned by this function.
 *
 * The traversal is performed in pre-order, meaning that for each internal node,
 * the function first visits the node itself and then recursively traverses its child nodes.
 *
 * @param node The root of the AST to be traversed. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A set of SgLocatedNode pointers representing all modified nodes found in the traversal.
 *
 * @warning If the input AST contains a large number of modified nodes, 
 *          this function may consume significant memory and/or time.
 *
 * @pre The input node must not be NULL. The isModified flag for each node in the AST
 *      has been correctly set prior to calling this function.
 *
 * @post The returned set contains all SgLocatedNodes that were marked as modified 
 *       during the traversal of the input AST.
 *
 * @note This function uses a custom AstSimpleProcessing class, LocatedNodeTraversal,
 *       to perform the actual node traversal and collection. This allows for
 *       efficient handling of large ASTs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root of an AST
 *           std::set<SgLocatedNode*> modifiedNodes = SageInterface::collectModifiedLocatedNodes(root);
 *           for (std::set<SgLocatedNode*>::iterator it = modifiedNodes.begin(); it != modifiedNodes.end(); ++it)
 *             {
 *               SgLocatedNode* node = *it;
 *               // process the modified node as needed
 *             }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::resetModifiedLocatedNodes(const std::set<SgLocatedNode*> & modifiedNodeSet)
 *
 * @brief Resets the modification status of a set of located nodes in the IR.
 *
 * This function iterates over a given set of SgLocatedNode objects and marks each node as modified by setting its isModified flag to true. 
 * The input set is expected to contain valid pointers to SgLocatedNode objects that are part of the Intermediate Representation (IR). 
 * This operation is typically used to update the IR after modifications have been made to the source code.
 *
 * @param modifiedNodeSet A constant reference to a set of SgLocatedNode pointers representing the nodes to be marked as modified. The set must not contain null pointers or invalid node references.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the input set or its contents. It assumes that the caller has ensured the correctness and consistency of the data being passed in.
 *
 * @pre The input set modifiedNodeSet must contain only valid pointers to SgLocatedNode objects that are part of the IR. 
 *      The function also expects that the isModified flag of each node in the set is currently set to false, as this operation will overwrite any existing value.
 *
 * @post After executing this function, all nodes in the input set modifiedNodeSet will have their isModified flag set to true.
 *
 * @note This function has no side effects other than updating the modification status of the specified IR nodes. It does not modify any other part of the IR or external state.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           std::set<SgLocatedNode*> modifiedNodes;
 *           // ... populate the set with valid SgLocatedNode pointers ...
 *           SageInterface::resetModifiedLocatedNodes(modifiedNodes);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::reportModifiedStatements( const string & label, SgNode* node )
 *
 * @brief Reports the nodes in the AST that are marked as modified (isModified flag).
 *
 * This function is useful for debugging the token-based unparsing. It prints out
 * information about the modified statements, including their filenames and class names.
 *
 * The function first checks if the input node is an SgSourceFile or an SgGlobal node,
 * and if so, it prints out the corresponding filename. Then it collects all the modified
 * statements in the AST using the collectModifiedStatements() function and iterates over
 * them to print out their filenames and class names.
 *
 * @param label A string label used for debugging purposes.
 * @param node The input SgNode that may be an SgSourceFile or an SgGlobal node.
 *
 * @return None. This function does not return any value.
 *
 * @warning If the input node is NULL, this function will terminate with a ROSE_ASSERT failure.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be a valid SgNode in the AST.
 *
 * @post The function will print out information about the modified statements to the console.
 *
 * @note This function is primarily used for debugging purposes and should not be called
 *       unnecessarily, as it may slow down the program execution.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SageInterface::reportModifiedStatements("MyLabel", someNode);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::reportModifiedLocatedNodes( const string & label, SgNode* node )
 *
 * @brief Reports the nodes in the AST that are marked as modified (isModified flag).
 *
 * This function is useful for debugging the token-based unparsing. It collects and prints
 * information about the modified located nodes in the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) based on the
 * provided label and SgNode.
 *
 * The function first checks if the provided node is an instance of SgSourceFile or SgGlobal,
 * and retrieves the corresponding source file name. Then, it collects all modified located nodes
 * using the collectModifiedLocatedNodes() function and iterates over them to print their
 * information, including filename, memory address, and class name.
 *
 * @param label A string label used for debugging purposes.
 * @param node The SgNode instance from which to start collecting modified located nodes.
 *
 * @return None (void function).
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error handling on the input parameters. It assumes
 *          that the provided node is valid and that the label string is properly formatted.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be a valid SgNode instance, and the label string must be properly formatted.
 *
 * @post The function will print information about the modified located nodes to the console.
 *
 * @note This function is primarily used for debugging purposes and may produce verbose output.
 *
 * @example
 *          @code
 *           // Create a sample SgNode instance (e.g., an SgSourceFile)
 *           SgNode* node = new SgSourceFile();
 *
 *           // Call the reportModifiedLocatedNodes() function with a label and the node instance
 *           SageInterface::reportModifiedLocatedNodes("Sample Label", node);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::translateToUseCppDeclarations(SgNode* n)
 *
 * @brief Translates a given SgNode to use C++ declarations.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) subtree rooted at the
 * given SgNode and translates it to use C++ declarations. It uses a recursive
 * algorithm implemented through the CppTranslationTraversal class, which is an
 * AstSimpleProcessing subclass. The traversal visits each node in the AST,
 * checks if it's a scope statement, and if so, processes it using the
 * translateScopeToUseCppDeclarations function.
 *
 * @param n The SgNode representing the root of the AST subtree to be translated.
 *          Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None. This function does not return any value.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the AST in-place and may cause unintended
 *          side effects if not used carefully.
 *
 * @pre The input SgNode must represent a valid AST subtree.
 *
 * @post The AST subtree rooted at the input SgNode is modified to use C++
 *       declarations.
 *
 * @note This function uses a postorder traversal, meaning that it visits the
 *       child nodes before their parent node. This is done to ensure that all
 *       necessary transformations are applied to the child nodes before they
 *       are visited by the parent node.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root of the AST subtree
 *            SageInterface::translateToUseCppDeclarations(root);
 *          @endcode
 */

/**
 * @fn SageInterface::translateScopeToUseCppDeclarations(SgScopeStatement* scope)
 *
 * @brief Translates a given scope to use C++ declarations.
 *
 * This function processes the statements in the provided SgScopeStatement and 
 * translates them to use C++ declarations. It handles both declaration-only scopes
 * (e.g., global scope, namespace definitions, class definitions) and scopes that contain
 * non-declaration statements (e.g., SgBasicBlock). The translation process involves
 * attaching preprocessor directives to the statements in the scope.
 *
 * @param scope The SgScopeStatement to be translated. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input scope by inserting preprocessor directives.
 *          It also removes attached preprocessing information from the statements.
 *
 * @pre The input scope must be a valid SgScopeStatement.
 *
 * @post The input scope is modified to use C++ declarations. Attached preprocessing
 *       information is removed from the statements in the scope.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to process the statements in the scope.
 *       It also relies on the skipTranslateToUseCppDeclaration() function to determine
 *       which directives should be skipped during translation.
 *
 * @example
 *   SgScopeStatement* scope = ...;
 *   SageInterface::translateScopeToUseCppDeclarations(scope);
 */

/**
 * @fn std::vector<SgC_PreprocessorDirectiveStatement*> SageInterface::translateStatementToUseCppDeclarations(SgStatement* statement, SgScopeStatement*)
 *
 * @brief Translates a given statement to use C++ declarations.
 *
 * This function takes an input statement and translates it to use C++ declarations. 
 * It iterates through the attached preprocessing information of the statement, 
 * creates a directive for each non-skipped preprocessor directive, and returns a list of these directives.
 *
 * @param statement The input statement to be translated.
 * @param scopeStatement The scope statement associated with the input statement.
 *
 * @return A vector of SgC_PreprocessorDirectiveStatement pointers representing the translated directives.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input statement has attached preprocessing information. 
 *          If not, an empty vector will be returned.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must have attached preprocessing information.
 *
 * @post The returned vector contains all non-skipped preprocessor directives from the input statement's attached preprocessing information.
 *
 * @note This function does not handle skipped preprocessor directives (i.e., those that are not translated into IR nodes). 
 *       If such handling is required, additional logic will need to be added.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* statement = ...; // Initialize the input statement
 *           SgScopeStatement* scopeStatement = ...; // Initialize the associated scope statement
 *           std::vector<SgC_PreprocessorDirectiveStatement*> directiveList = SageInterface::translateStatementToUseCppDeclarations(statement, scopeStatement);
 *           for (auto& directive : directiveList) {
 *             printf("Translated Directive: %s\n", directive->get_prettyPrint().c_str());
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::printOutComments(SgLocatedNode* locatedNode)
 *
 * @brief Prints out comments attached to a given SgLocatedNode.
 *
 * This function is used for debugging purposes. It takes an SgLocatedNode as input and prints out any comments that are attached to it.
 *
 * The comments are retrieved using the getAttachedPreprocessingInfo() method of the SgLocatedNode class, which returns a pointer to an AttachedPreprocessingInfoType object. If this object is not NULL, the function iterates over its contents using an iterator and prints out each comment.
 *
 * For each comment, the function prints out its relative position (before or after) and its string value. Additionally, it prints out the number of lines in the comment and the column number of the end of the string.
 *
 * @param locatedNode The SgLocatedNode for which to print out comments. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function is intended for debugging purposes only and should not be used in production code.
 *
 * @pre The input SgLocatedNode must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The function will have printed out any comments attached to the input SgLocatedNode.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to check that the input SgLocatedNode is not NULL and that each comment in the AttachedPreprocessingInfoType object is not NULL. If these assertions fail, the program will terminate.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an SgLocatedNode object
 *           SgLocatedNode* node = ...;
 *
 *           // Attach some comments to the node
 *           AttachedPreprocessingInfoType* comments = new AttachedPreprocessingInfoType();
 *           comments->add(new PreprocessingInfo("Comment 1", PreprocessingInfo::before));
 *           comments->add(new PreprocessingInfo("Comment 2", PreprocessingInfo::after));
 *           node->setAttachedPreprocessingInfo(comments);
 *
 *           // Print out the comments
 *           SageInterface::printOutComments(node);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::skipTranslateToUseCppDeclaration(PreprocessingInfo* currentPreprocessingInfo)
 *
 * @brief Determines whether to skip translation of a preprocessing directive.
 *
 * This function evaluates the type of preprocessing directive and decides
 * whether it should be skipped during translation. It checks for specific types
 * of directives such as C-style comments, C++-style comments, Fortran-style 
 * comments, blank lines, and linkage specifications.
 *
 * @param currentPreprocessingInfo A pointer to the PreprocessingInfo object containing information about the current directive.
 *                                  Must not be NULL. The function will assert if this parameter is NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the translation of the preprocessing directive should be skipped; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle cases where the input PreprocessingInfo pointer is NULL. It asserts in such cases.
 *
 * @pre The input PreprocessingInfo object must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the translation of the preprocessing directive should be skipped.
 *
 * @note This function uses a simple if-else statement to evaluate the type of directive and make its decision.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           PreprocessingInfo* info = new PreprocessingInfo();
 *           // ... assume some initialization of info ...
 *           bool skipTranslation = SageInterface::skipTranslateToUseCppDeclaration(info);
 *           if (skipTranslation) {
 *             std::cout << "Skipping translation..." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "Not skipping translation..." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::outputFileIds(SgNode* node)
 *
 * @brief Reports the file id values of all located nodes in the AST subtree represented by the input node.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) subtree rooted at the given SgNode and reports the file id values of all located nodes. 
 * It uses a preorder traversal strategy to visit each node in the tree, checking if it is an instance of SgLocatedNode or SgInitializedName.
 *
 * @param node The root node of the AST subtree to be traversed.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error handling on the input node. It assumes that the node is a valid pointer to a SgNode object.
 *
 * @pre The input node must be a non-null pointer to a SgNode object.
 *
 * @post The file id values of all located nodes in the AST subtree are reported to the standard output.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive traversal strategy, which may lead to stack overflow for very large AST subtrees.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* node = ...; // obtain a pointer to a SgNode object
 *           SageInterface::outputFileIds(node);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::updateDefiningNondefiningLinks(SgFunctionDeclaration* func, SgStatementPtrList& stmtList)
 * @brief Updates defining and non-defining links for a given function.
 *
 * This function iterates over a list of statements to find the same function declaration
 * list, including the input function itself. It then updates the defining and non-defining
 * links for each function declaration in the list.
 *
 * @param func The input function for which to update defining and non-defining links.
 * @param stmtList The list of statements to iterate over.
 */

/**
 * @fn SageInterface::collectReadWriteRefs(SgStatement* stmt, std::vector<SgNode*>& readRefs, std::vector<SgNode*>& writeRefs, bool useCachedDefUse)
 *
 * @brief Collects the read and write references for a given statement.
 *
 * This function analyzes the side effects of a given statement in terms of its
 * read and write references. It uses the ROSE compiler infrastructure to perform
 * this analysis. The results are stored in two vectors: one for read references
 * and another for write references.
 *
 * @param stmt The statement for which the read and write references are to be collected.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 * @param readRefs A vector to store the read references of the given statement.
 *                 This parameter is passed by reference and will be modified by this function.
 * @param writeRefs A vector to store the write references of the given statement.
 *                  This parameter is passed by reference and will be modified by this function.
 * @param useCachedDefUse A boolean flag indicating whether to use cached def-use information or not.
 *
 * @return True if the analysis was successful, false otherwise. Note that a return value
 *         of false does not necessarily indicate an error; it may simply mean that the
 *         function called another function that was not annotated with Qing's RWset annotation method.
 *
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the given statement is part of a valid ROSE AST.
 *          If this assumption is violated, the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must be non-NULL and part of a valid ROSE AST.
 * @post The readRefs and writeRefs vectors will contain the read and write references
 *       of the given statement, respectively. Note that these vectors may not be exhaustive;
 *       they only contain those references that could be determined through static analysis.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE compiler infrastructure to perform its analysis.
 *       It also relies on Qing's RWset annotation method for accurate results.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // some statement in a ROSE AST
 *           std::vector<SgNode*> readRefs;
 *           std::vector<SgNode*> writeRefs;
 *           bool useCachedDefUse = true;
 *           SageInterface::collectReadWriteRefs(stmt, readRefs, writeRefs, useCachedDefUse);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::collectReadWriteVariables(SgStatement* stmt, set<SgInitializedName*>& readVars, set<SgInitializedName*>& writeVars, bool coarseGrain)
 *
 * @brief Collects the variables that are read and written by a given statement.
 *
 * This function analyzes the given statement and identifies the variables that are accessed (read or written) within it. 
 * It uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the abstract syntax tree (AST) of the statement and collects references to variables. 
 * The collected variables are then converted to their corresponding SgInitializedName objects, which represent the names of the variables in the source code.
 *
 * @param stmt The statement for which the read and write variables are to be collected. Must not be NULL.
 * @param readVars A set that will store the variables that are read by the statement. 
 *                 The set is expected to be empty when passed to this function, and it will be populated with SgInitializedName objects representing the names of the read variables.
 * @param writeVars A set that will store the variables that are written by the statement. 
 *                  The set is expected to be empty when passed to this function, and it will be populated with SgInitializedName objects representing the names of the written variables.
 * @param coarseGrain A boolean flag indicating whether a coarse-grained analysis should be performed. If true, the analysis may not capture all variable accesses, but it will be faster. Default value is true.
 *
 * @return True if the collection was successful, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input statement is valid and well-formed. 
 *          It does not perform any error checking on the input statement, and it may produce incorrect results or crash if the input statement is invalid.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must be non-NULL and well-formed.
 *
 * @post The readVars and writeVars sets will contain SgInitializedName objects representing the names of the variables that are read and written by the statement, respectively.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the AST of the statement, which may lead to stack overflow for very large statements.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // obtain a statement somehow
 *           set<SgInitializedName*> readVars;
 *           set<SgInitializedName*> writeVars;
 *           bool success = SageInterface::collectReadWriteVariables(stmt, readVars, writeVars);
 *           if (success) {
 *             cout << "Read variables: ";
 *             for (set<SgInitializedName*>::iterator iter = readVars.begin(); iter != readVars.end(); ++iter) {
 *               cout << (*iter)->get_name() << " ";
 *             }
 *             cout << endl;
 *             cout << "Write variables: ";
 *             for (set<SgInitializedName*>::iterator iter = writeVars.begin(); iter != writeVars.end(); ++iter) {
 *               cout << (*iter)->get_name() << " ";
 *             }
 *             cout << endl;
 *           } else {
 *             cerr << "Error collecting read and write variables." << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::collectReadOnlyVariables(SgStatement* stmt, std::set<SgInitializedName*>& readOnlyVars, bool coarseGrain)
 *
 * @brief Collects read-only variables from a given statement.
 *
 * This function analyzes the given statement and collects the names of variables that are only read but not written. 
 * It uses two approaches: (1) precise analysis by collecting read-write variables using collectReadWriteVariables() and then computing the difference between read and write sets, 
 * or (2) a simple type-based approach if the first one fails.
 *
 * @param stmt The statement to be analyzed for read-only variables. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @param readOnlyVars A set of SgInitializedName pointers that will store the names of read-only variables found in the given statement.
 *
 * @param coarseGrain Optional parameter indicating whether a coarse-grained analysis should be performed (default is true).
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning If collectReadWriteVariables() fails, a simple type-based approach may not find all read-only variables.
 *
 * @pre stmt must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post readOnlyVars will contain the names of read-only variables found in the given statement.
 *
 * @note The function uses two approaches to collect read-only variables: precise analysis and simple type-based analysis. 
 *       If the first approach fails, the second one is used as a fallback.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // some statement
 *           std::set<SgInitializedName*> readOnlyVars;
 *           SageInterface sageInterface;
 *           sageInterface.collectReadOnlyVariables(stmt, readOnlyVars);
 *           for (std::set<SgInitializedName*>::iterator i = readOnlyVars.begin(); i != readOnlyVars.end(); ++i) {
 *             SgInitializedName* var = *i;
 *             // do something with the read-only variable
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::collectReadOnlySymbols(SgStatement* stmt, std::set<SgVariableSymbol*>& readOnlySymbols, bool coarseGrain/*=true*/)
 *
 * @brief Collects read-only symbols from a given statement.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) of the input statement
 * and identifies variables that are only read but not written. These variables
 * are then collected in a set for further analysis or processing.
 *
 * The algorithm used is based on a recursive traversal of the AST, starting from
 * the given statement. It considers all possible paths in the AST and checks
 * each variable access to determine if it's read-only.
 *
 * @param stmt The input statement whose AST will be traversed for read-only symbols.
 *             Must not be NULL.
 * @param readOnlySymbols A set of SgVariableSymbol objects that will store the
 *                        collected read-only variables. This set will be modified
 *                        by this function.
 * @param coarseGrain If true, a more efficient but less precise algorithm is used,
 *                    which may lead to false positives (i.e., variables reported as
 *                    read-only when they're not). Default value is true.
 *
 * @return None. This function modifies the input set of read-only symbols directly.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input statement's AST has already been
 *          built and is accessible through the ROSE framework.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must have a valid AST, and the readOnlySymbols set must be empty.
 *
 * @post The readOnlySymbols set will contain all read-only variables found in the
 *       input statement's AST. If coarseGrain is true, this set may also include false positives.
 *
 * @note This function uses an internal temporary set to store intermediate results,
 *       which can lead to increased memory usage for large inputs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // obtain a statement from the AST
 *           std::set<SgVariableSymbol*> readOnlyVars;
 *           SageInterface::collectReadOnlySymbols(stmt, readOnlyVars);
 *           for (std::set<SgVariableSymbol*>::const_iterator iter = readOnlyVars.begin();
 *                iter != readOnlyVars.end(); ++iter) {
 *             // process each read-only variable
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isUseByAddressVariableRef(SgVarRefExp* ref)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a variable reference is used by address in an expression.
 *
 * This function determines whether the given variable reference is being used
 * as an operand for the unary '&' operator, or as a parameter of a function call
 * where the corresponding parameter has a reference type. It checks two main cases:
 * (1) if the parent node of the variable reference is an SgAddressofOp node,
 * indicating that it's being used as an operand for the unary '&' operator;
 * and (2) if the parent node is an SgExprListExp, indicating a function call,
 * in which case it checks whether the parameter type at the corresponding index
 * has a reference type.
 *
 * @param ref The variable reference to check. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the variable reference is used by address in the expression; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgVarRefExp node is valid and points to a
 *          non-NULL parent node. Failure to meet this precondition may result in undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input SgVarRefExp node must not be NULL, and its parent node must also not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the variable reference is used by address; otherwise, it returns false.
 *
 * @note This function does not handle cases where there are type casting operations between
 *       the variable reference and its parent node. It also assumes that the SgFunctionCallExp
 *       node has a valid SgFunctionRefExp child node.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Assuming SageInterface is an instance of the class with this method
 *           SgVarRefExp* varRef = getSomeVariableReference();
 *           bool result = SageInterface.isUseByAddressVariableRef(varRef);
 *           if (result) {
 *             std::cout << "Variable reference used by address." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *             std::cout << "Variable reference not used by address." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::collectUseByAddressVariableRefs (const SgStatement* s, std::set<SgVarRefExp*>& varSetB)
 *
 * @brief Collects variable references used by address in a given statement.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree of the input statement and
 * identifies variable references that are used as addresses. It uses various
 * checks to determine if a reference is used by address, including checking for
 * use as an operand of the unary & operator or as a parameter in a function call.
 *
 * @param s The statement to be analyzed for variable references used by address.
 *          Must not be NULL.
 * @param varSetB A set of variable references that are used by address. This set is updated
 *                with the variable references found in the input statement.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input statement has been parsed and
 *          its abstract syntax tree is available for traversal.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must be a valid, non-NULL pointer to an SgStatement object.
 *      The varSetB parameter must be a valid, non-NULL pointer to a set of SgVarRefExp objects.
 *
 * @post The varSetB set will contain all variable references used by address in the
 *       input statement. If no such references are found, the set will remain unchanged.
 *
 * @note This function uses various heuristics and may not detect all possible cases
 *       of variable references used by address.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // obtain a statement from somewhere
 *           std::set<SgVarRefExp*> varRefs;
 *           SageInterface::collectUseByAddressVariableRefs(stmt, varRefs);
 *           // varRefs now contains all variable references used by address in stmt
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn LivenessAnalysis* SageInterface::call_liveness_analysis(SgProject* project, bool debug)
 *
 * @brief Performs liveness analysis on a given SgProject.
 *
 * This function performs liveness analysis on the input SgProject using
 * the DefUseAnalysis class. It first checks if the analysis has already been performed,
 * and if so, returns the cached result. Otherwise, it creates a new instance of LivenessAnalysis,
 * runs the def-use analysis, and then performs liveness analysis on each function body in the project.
 *
 * @param project The SgProject to perform liveness analysis on. Must not be NULL.
 * @param debug If true, enables debugging output. Defaults to false.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the LivenessAnalysis instance containing the results of the analysis.
 *         Returns NULL if an error occurs during analysis.
 *
 * @warning The function may abort and exit the program if an error occurs during analysis.
 *
 * @pre The input project must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned LivenessAnalysis instance will contain the results of the analysis.
 *
 *       If an error occurs during analysis, the function will abort and exit the program.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to perform liveness analysis on each function body.
 *       It also caches the result of the analysis to avoid redundant computation.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgProject* project = ...; // create or load an SgProject instance
 *           LivenessAnalysis* liv = SageInterface::call_liveness_analysis(project, true);
 *           if (liv != NULL) {
 *             // use the results of the liveness analysis
 *           } else {
 *             // handle error
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::getLiveVariables(LivenessAnalysis* liv, SgForStatement* loop, std::set<SgInitializedName*>& liveIns, std::set<SgInitializedName*> & liveOuts)
 *
 * @brief Calculates the live variables for a given loop in the control flow graph.
 *
 * This function determines the set of live variables at the entry and exit points
 * of a loop in the control flow graph. It uses the liveness analysis provided by
 * the LivenessAnalysis class to compute the live-in and live-out sets for the loop.
 *
 * The function takes as input the loop statement, its corresponding node in the
 * control flow graph, and references to two sets that will store the live-in and
 * live-out variables. It returns no value but modifies the input sets accordingly.
 *
 * @param liv A pointer to a LivenessAnalysis object that provides liveness information for the program.
 * @param loop A pointer to the SgForStatement representing the loop in the abstract syntax tree.
 * @param liveIns A reference to a set of SgInitializedName pointers that will store the live-in variables for the loop.
 * @param liveOuts A reference to a set of SgInitializedName pointers that will store the live-out variables for the loop.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input loop statement is valid and has been correctly analyzed by the LivenessAnalysis class.
 *          If the loop statement is not valid or has not been properly analyzed, the results may be incorrect or undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input loop statement must be a valid SgForStatement node in the abstract syntax tree.
 *      The LivenessAnalysis object must have been correctly initialized and provide accurate liveness information for the program.
 *
 * @post The liveIns set will contain the variables that are live at the entry point of the loop, and the liveOuts set will contain the variables
 *       that are live at the exit point of the loop. These sets may be empty if no variables are live at these points.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the control flow graph and compute the live-in and live-out sets for the loop.
 *       It assumes that the LivenessAnalysis class provides accurate liveness information for the program, which is used to determine the
 *       live variables at each point in the control flow graph.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create a LivenessAnalysis object and initialize it with the program's abstract syntax tree
 *           LivenessAnalysis liv;
 *           liv.initialize(ast);
 *
 *           // Get the loop statement from the abstract syntax tree
 *           SgForStatement* loop = ast->get_first_for_stmt();
 *
 *
 *           // Create sets to store the live-in and live-out variables for the loop
 *           std::set<SgInitializedName*> liveIns, liveOuts;
 *
 *           // Call the getLiveVariables function to compute the live-in and live-out sets for the loop
 *           SageInterface::getLiveVariables(&liv, loop, liveIns, liveOuts);
 *
 *           // Print the live-in and live-out variables for the loop
 *           cout << "Live-in variables: ";
 *           for (std::set<SgInitializedName*>::iterator iter = liveIns.begin(); iter != liveIns.end(); ++iter) {
 *               cout << (*iter)->get_qualified_name().getString() << endl;
 *           }
 *
 *           cout << "Live-out variables: ";
 *           for
 *           @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::ReductionRecognition(SgForStatement* loop, std::set< std::pair <SgInitializedName*, OmpSupport::omp_construct_enum > > & results)
 *
 * @brief Recognizes reduction patterns in a for loop.
 *
 * This function analyzes the given for loop to identify variables that are used
 * as reductions. A reduction is recognized when a variable is assigned a value
 * based on its previous value, typically using an operator such as '+', '*',
 * 'min', or 'max'. The function returns a set of pairs containing the identified
 * reduction variables and their corresponding OpenMP construct types.
 *
 * @param loop The for loop to analyze for reductions.
 * @param results A set to store the recognized reduction variables and their
 *                 corresponding OpenMP construct types.
 *
 * @return None. The results are stored in the 'results' parameter.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input loop is a canonical for loop,
 *          i.e., it has an initializer, a condition, and an incrementor. If the
 *          loop does not meet this assumption, the function may produce incorrect
 *          results or crash.
 *
 * @pre The input loop must be a valid SgForStatement node in the ROSE AST.
 * @post The 'results' set contains all recognized reduction variables and their
 *       corresponding OpenMP construct types.
 *
 * @note This function uses various heuristics to recognize reductions, including:
 *       - Variables referenced at most twice within the loop body.
 *       - Assignments to a variable based on its previous value using an operator.
 *       - If statements that assign a value to a variable based on its previous value.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* loop = ...; // obtain the for loop node from the ROSE AST
 *           std::set< std::pair <SgInitializedName*, OmpSupport::omp_construct_enum > > results;
 *           SageInterface::ReductionRecognition(loop, results);
 *           // process the recognized reduction variables and their OpenMP construct types in 'results'
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::constantFolding(SgNode* r)
 *
 * @brief Performs constant folding optimization on a given SgNode.
 *
 * This function applies constant folding optimization to the input SgNode, 
 * which is a fundamental operation in compiler design that replaces constant expressions
 * with their computed values. It relies on the ConstantFolding class for the actual optimization.
 *
 * @param r The SgNode to be optimized using constant folding. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the validity of the input SgNode.
 *          It is the caller's responsibility to ensure that the node is properly initialized and valid.
 *
 * @pre The input SgNode must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The input SgNode may have been modified by the constant folding optimization.
 *
 * @note This function is only available when ROSE_USE_INTERNAL_FRONTEND_DEVELOPMENT is not defined.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgNode* node = ...; // Initialize node
 *            SageInterface::constantFolding(node);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn int SageInterface::instrumentEndOfFunction(SgFunctionDeclaration* func, SgStatement* s)
 *
 * @brief Instruments the end of a function by inserting statements before return statements.
 *
 * This function instruments the end of a given function by inserting statements
 * before each return statement. If no return statements are found, it appends the
 * statements to the end of the function body.
 *
 * The function first queries the function declaration for all return statements,
 * then iterates over them to insert the given statement before each one. If a return
 * statement has an expression that needs rewriting, it splits the expression before
 * inserting the statement. It also avoids reusing the same statement by copying it.
 *
 * @param func The SgFunctionDeclaration representing the function to instrument.
 *             Must not be NULL and must have a valid definition.
 * @param s    The SgStatement to insert before each return statement or at the end
 *             of the function body if no return statements are found. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The number of times the statement was inserted.
 *
 * @warning If the function declaration is empty or ill-formed, this function will abort.
 *
 * @pre The input function declaration and statement must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The function body has been modified to include the given statement before
 *       each return statement or at the end of the function body if no return statements
 *       are found.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input function declaration has a valid definition.
 *       If the function body is empty, it will abort.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* func = ...; // obtain a function declaration
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // create a statement to insert
 *           int result = instrumentEndOfFunction(func, stmt);
 *           cout << "Inserted statement " << result << " times." << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::removeJumpsToNextStatement(SgNode* top)
 *
 * @brief Removes jump statements to the next statement in a basic block.
 *
 * This function traverses an abstract syntax tree (AST) and removes jump statements
 * that point to the next statement in a basic block. It uses a recursive algorithm
 * to traverse the AST and identify such jumps.
 *
 * The function takes as input the root node of the AST, which is expected to be a valid
 * SgNode* object. It returns no value (void).
 *
 * @param top The root node of the abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST by removing jump statements.
 *
 * @pre The input AST must be well-formed and valid.
 *
 * @post The modified AST will have all jump statements to the next statement removed.
 *
 * @note This function uses a post-order traversal strategy, which means that it visits
 *       child nodes before their parent nodes. This is done to ensure that all jumps are
 *       properly removed from the AST.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* astRoot = ...; // assume a valid AST root node
 *           SageInterface::removeJumpsToNextStatement(astRoot);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::removeUnusedLabels(SgNode* top, bool keepChild)
 *
 * @brief Removes unused labels from an abstract syntax tree (AST).
 *
 * This function traverses the AST rooted at the given node and identifies
 * labels that are not referenced anywhere in the code. It then removes these
 * unused labels to simplify the AST.
 *
 * The removal process can be customized based on whether child statements of
 * the removed label should be preserved or not. If keepChild is true, the function
 * replaces each removed label with its corresponding child statement; otherwise,
 * it simply deletes the entire label statement.
 *
 * @param top The root node of the AST to be processed. Must not be NULL.
 * @param keepChild Optional flag indicating whether child statements should be preserved during removal (default: false).
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST in-place.
 *
 * @pre The input AST is valid and well-formed.
 *
 * @post All unused labels have been removed from the AST, and child statements are either preserved or deleted accordingly based on the keepChild flag.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the findUnusedLabels() method has already identified and returned a set of SgLabelStatement pointers representing the unused labels to be removed.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create an instance of SageInterface and a sample AST
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           SgNode* ast = ...;  // assume some initialization code here
 *
 *           // Remove unused labels from the AST, preserving child statements
 *           sage.removeUnusedLabels(ast, true);
 *
 *           // Alternatively, remove unused labels without preserving child statements
 *           sage.removeUnusedLabels(ast, false);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn std::set<SgLabelStatement*> SageInterface::findUnusedLabels (SgNode* top)
 *
 * @brief Finds and returns a set of unused labels in the given AST.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) rooted at the
 * specified SgNode* 'top' to identify all labels that are not referenced by any
 * goto statements. It uses a custom visitor class, FindUsedAndAllLabelsVisitor,
 * which inherits from AstSimpleProcessing and overrides its visit() method.
 *
 * The function first initializes two sets: one for used labels (i.e., those
 * referenced by goto statements) and another for all labels encountered during
 * the traversal. It then traverses the AST in preorder, visiting each node to
 * determine whether it's a goto statement or a label statement. If it's a goto
 * statement, its target label is added to the set of used labels; if it's a label
 * statement, it's added to the set of all labels.
 *
 * After traversal, the function iterates over the set of used labels and removes
 * each corresponding label from the set of all labels. The remaining labels in
 * this set are considered unused.
 *
 * @param top The root node of the AST to be traversed for finding unused labels.
 *
 * @return A set of SgLabelStatement* objects representing the unused labels in
 *         the given AST.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input AST is valid and well-formed.
 *          If the AST contains ill-formed constructs, the behavior of this
 *          function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input AST rooted at 'top' must be valid and well-formed.
 *
 * @post The returned set of SgLabelStatement* objects represents all unused
 *       labels in the given AST. The sets of used and all labels are updated
 *       accordingly.
 *
 * @note This function uses a preorder traversal strategy, which means that it
 *       visits each node before its children. This allows for efficient
 *       identification of unused labels by first collecting all labels and then
 *       removing those referenced by goto statements.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create a sample AST with some labels and goto statements
 *           SgNode* top = ...;  // Assume 'top' is the root of the sample AST
 *
 *           // Find unused labels in the AST
 *           std::set<SgLabelStatement*> unusedLabels;
 *           unusedLabels = SageInterface::findUnusedLabels(top);
 *
 *           // Print the names of unused labels
 *           for (std::set<SgLabelStatement*>::iterator i = unusedLabels.begin();
 *                i != unusedLabels.end(); ++i) {
 *             std::cout << "Unused label: " << (*i)->get_label().str() << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::removeConsecutiveLabels(SgNode* top)
 *
 * @brief Removes consecutive labels from goto statements in a given AST.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) rooted at the
 * specified node and removes consecutive labels from goto statements.
 * It iterates over all SgGotoStatement nodes, retrieves their corresponding
 * label statements, and then updates the label statement if it is followed
 * by another label statement in the same basic block.
 *
 * @param top The root node of the AST to be processed. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return None (void function).
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST by updating the labels of goto statements.
 *
 * @pre The input AST must be well-formed and valid.
 *
 * @post The input AST is modified to remove consecutive labels from goto statements.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input AST has already been parsed and validated.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgNode* astRoot = ...; // obtain the root node of the AST
 *            SageInterface::removeConsecutiveLabels(astRoot);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::mergeDeclarationAndAssignment(SgVariableDeclaration* decl, SgExprStatement* assign_stmt, bool removeAssignStmt /*= true*/)
 *
 * @brief Merges a variable assignment statement into a matching variable declaration statement.
 *
 * This function combines the functionality of a variable declaration and an assignment statement
 * into a single, unified declaration. It is used to simplify code by eliminating redundant statements.
 *
 * The merge operation involves updating the declaration with the assigned value from the statement.
 * If the removeAssignStmt parameter is set to true (default), the original assignment statement is removed.
 *
 * @param decl A pointer to an SgVariableDeclaration node representing the variable declaration statement.
 * @param assign_stmt A pointer to an SgExprStatement node representing the assignment statement.
 * @param removeAssignStmt An optional boolean parameter indicating whether to remove the original assignment statement (default: true).
 *
 * @return True if the merge operation is successful, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the declaration and assignment statements are compatible and can be merged.
 *          It does not perform any semantic checks or error handling for incompatible statements.
 *
 * @pre The input parameters decl and assign_stmt must point to valid SgVariableDeclaration and SgExprStatement nodes, respectively.
 * @post If removeAssignStmt is true, the original assignment statement is removed from the AST.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the abstract syntax tree (AST) by updating the declaration node and potentially removing the assignment statement node.
 *
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::mergeAssignmentWithDeclaration(SgExprStatement* assign_stmt, SgVariableDeclaration* decl, bool removeAssignStmt /*= true*/)
 *
 * @brief Merges an assignment statement with a variable declaration.
 *
 * This function checks if the given assignment statement can be merged with
 * a variable declaration. It performs sanity checks on both the assignment
 * statement and the variable declaration to ensure they are in the correct form.
 * If the checks pass, it merges the two by removing the assignment statement and
 * setting the initializer of the variable declaration to the right-hand side of
 * the assignment.
 *
 * @param assign_stmt The assignment statement to be merged. Must not be NULL.
 * @param decl The variable declaration to be merged with. Must not be NULL.
 * @param removeAssignStmt Optional parameter indicating whether to remove the assignment statement after merging. Defaults to true.
 *
 * @return True if the merge was successful, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST.
 *
 * @pre The assignment statement and variable declaration must be valid and not NULL.
 * @post If the merge is successful, the assignment statement will be removed from the AST and the variable declaration's initializer will be set to the right-hand side of the assignment.
 *
 * @note This function uses a fallback approach when the symbol of the variable declaration cannot be found in the symbol table. In this case, it compares the names of the variables instead.
 *
 * @example
 * @code
 *   SgExprStatement* assign_stmt = ...;
 *   SgVariableDeclaration* decl = ...;
 *   if (SageInterface::mergeAssignmentWithDeclaration(assign_stmt, decl)) {
 *     // merge was successful
 *   } else {
 *     // merge failed
 *   }
 * @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::mergeDeclarationWithAssignment(SgVariableDeclaration* decl, SgExprStatement* assign_stmt)
 *
 * @brief Merges a variable declaration with an assignment statement into a single declaration.
 *
 * This function takes a variable declaration and an assignment statement as input,
 * checks if they can be merged, and performs the merge operation. The merge is done
 * by copying the right-hand side of the assignment to the initializer of the variable
 * declaration, moving the declaration to the position of the assignment, and removing
 * the original assignment statement.
 *
 * @param decl A pointer to an SgVariableDeclaration object representing the variable declaration.
 *             Must not be NULL. The declaration should not have an existing initializer.
 * @param assign_stmt A pointer to an SgExprStatement object representing the assignment statement.
 *                    Must not be NULL and must be a form of var = xxx;. The left-hand side
 *                    operand of the assignment must match the variable being declared.
 *
 * @return True if the merge was successful, false otherwise. The function may return false
 *         if the input statements do not meet the required conditions or if an error occurs during
 *         the merge operation.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input statements and their parent nodes in the AST.
 *          It also moves preprocessing information attached to the original statements.
 *          Users should exercise caution when using this function, especially if they have
 *          already processed the AST or rely on the original statement structure.
 *
 * @pre The input statements must meet the required conditions specified above. The function
 *      assumes that the input statements are valid and well-formed.
 * @post If the merge is successful, the variable declaration will be modified to include an initializer,
 *       and the assignment statement will be removed from the AST.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any semantic checks on the merged statement. Users should
 *       verify that the resulting code is semantically correct.
 *
 * @example The following example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgVariableDeclaration* decl = ...; // create a variable declaration
 *           SgExprStatement* assign_stmt = ...; // create an assignment statement
 *           if (SageInterface::mergeDeclarationWithAssignment(decl, assign_stmt)) {
 *             // the merge was successful
 *           } else {
 *             // the merge failed
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgExprStatement* SageInterface::splitVariableDeclaration(SgVariableDeclaration* decl)
 *
 * @brief Splits a variable declaration into a separate assignment statement.
 *
 * This function takes a variable declaration as input, extracts the initializer 
 * expression if it exists, and creates a new assignment statement with the same 
 * left-hand side (variable reference) and the copied right-hand side (initializer 
 * expression). The original variable declaration is then modified to remove 
 * its initializer.
 *
 * @param decl A pointer to the SgVariableDeclaration object representing the 
 *             variable declaration to be split. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the newly created assignment statement, or NULL if the input 
 *         variable declaration did not have an initializer expression.
 *
 * @warning The function modifies the input variable declaration by removing its 
 *          initializer expression.
 *
 * @pre The input SgVariableDeclaration object must be non-NULL and valid.
 *
 * @post The returned assignment statement is inserted after the original variable 
 *       declaration in the AST, and the original variable declaration's initializer 
 *       expression is removed.
 *
 * @note This function supports all sorts of initializers, including aggregate 
 *       initializers. It also performs a deep copy of the right-hand side operand 
 *       to ensure that the new assignment statement has its own independent copy 
 *       of the expression.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgVariableDeclaration* decl = ...; // assume some variable declaration
 *           SgExprStatement* stmt = SageInterface::splitVariableDeclaration(decl);
 *           if (stmt != NULL) {
 *             // the assignment statement was successfully created and inserted
 *           } else {
 *             // the input variable declaration did not have an initializer expression
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn int SageInterface::splitVariableDeclaration(SgScopeStatement* scope, bool topLevelOnly)
 *
 * @brief Splits variable declarations in a given scope statement.
 *
 * This function splits variable declarations that are declared together into separate declarations.
 * It traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) of the input scope statement and identifies variable declarations
 * that need to be split. The splitting is performed based on the topLevelOnly parameter, which determines whether
 * only top-level variable declarations should be considered or all variable declarations regardless of their nesting level.
 *
 * @param scope The scope statement in which variable declarations are to be split.
 *              Must not be NULL and must represent a valid scope statement in the AST.
 *
 * @param topLevelOnly Flag indicating whether only top-level variable declarations should be considered for splitting.
 *                      If true, only variable declarations that are direct children of the input scope statement
 *                      will be considered. If false, all variable declarations regardless of their nesting level
 *                      will be considered. Defaults to true if not specified.
 *
 * @return The number of variable declarations that were split by this function.
 *
 * @warning This function does not handle cases where topLevelOnly is false. An assertion failure will occur in such cases.
 *
 * @pre The input scope statement must represent a valid scope statement in the AST and must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post Variable declarations that are direct children of the input scope statement (or all variable declarations if topLevelOnly is false)
 *        will be split into separate declarations. The number of splits performed is returned by this function.
 *
 * @note This function does not perform any semantic checks on the input AST and assumes that it represents valid C++ code.
 *       It also does not handle cases where the input scope statement has already been modified by other parts of the program.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgScopeStatement* scope = ...; // obtain a valid scope statement from the AST
 *           int count = SageInterface::splitVariableDeclaration(scope);
 *           cout << "Split " << count << " variable declarations." << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgAssignInitializer* SageInterface::splitExpression(SgExpression* from, string newName/* ="" */)
 *
 * @brief Splits an expression into a statement and assigns the result to a variable.
 *
 * This function takes an expression as input and transforms it into a statement
 * by assigning its result to a new variable. The name of this variable can be 
 * optionally specified; otherwise, a default name will be generated. The 
 * function also handles cases where the input expression is part of a larger 
 * expression or statement.
 *
 * @param from The expression to be split into a statement and assigned to a variable.
 *              Must not be NULL.
 * @param newName Optional parameter specifying the name of the new variable.
 *                 If empty, a default name will be generated.
 *
 * @return An SgAssignInitializer node representing the assignment of the 
 *          input expression to the new variable. Returns NULL if the function
 *          is disabled due to ROSE_USE_INTERNAL_FRONTEND_DEVELOPMENT being defined.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input expression is not part of a 
 *          larger expression or statement that would be affected by this 
 *          transformation. Also, the function does not perform any checks for 
 *          potential errors such as null pointer dereferences.
 *
 * @pre The input expression must not be NULL and must have a valid parent node.
 *      Additionally, if newName is specified, it must be a valid C++ identifier.
 *
 * @post A new variable declaration will be created with the assigned name and 
 *        type matching the input expression. This variable will be initialized
 *        with the result of the input expression. The original input expression
*         will be replaced by a reference to this new variable.
 *
 * @note If the function is disabled due to ROSE_USE_INTERNAL_FRONTEND_DEVELOPMENT 
 *       being defined, it will return NULL immediately without performing any 
 *       transformations.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* expr = ...; // some expression
 *           string newName = "myVar";
 *           SgAssignInitializer* ai = SageInterface::splitExpression(expr, newName);
 *           if (ai != NULL) {
 *               // The expression has been split into a statement and assigned to the new variable.
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::splitExpressionIntoBasicBlock(SgExpression* expr)
 *
 * @brief Splits a given expression into basic blocks.
 *
 * This function takes an expression as input and splits it into basic blocks,
 * which are then assigned to statements. The splitting is done using a recursive
 * algorithm, where each sub-expression is processed separately.
 *
 * @param expr The expression to be split into basic blocks.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the original expression by replacing it with
 *          a statement that represents the basic block.
 *
 * @pre The input expression must be non-null and valid.
 *
 * @post The original expression is replaced with a statement representing the
 *       basic block, and the generated statements are inserted into the AST.
 *
 * @note This function uses a custom StatementGenerator class to generate the
 *       statements for each basic block. The generator class is responsible for
 *       creating the necessary statements and inserting them into the AST.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* expr = ...; // create an expression
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           sage.splitExpressionIntoBasicBlock(expr);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::removeLabeledGotos(SgNode* top)
 *
 * @brief Removes labeled gotos from an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).
 *
 * This function traverses the AST rooted at the given node and replaces
 * labeled goto statements with their corresponding labels, effectively
 * removing them. It uses a recursive algorithm to navigate the tree.
 *
 * @param top The root node of the AST to process.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST in-place.
 *
 * @preconditions The input node is not null and represents a valid AST.
 *
 * @postconditions The labeled goto statements have been replaced with their corresponding labels.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input AST has already been parsed and validated.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* astRoot = ...; // assume a valid AST root node
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           sage.removeLabeledGotos(astRoot);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::changeBreakStatementsToGotos(SgStatement* loopOrSwitch)
 *
 * @brief Replaces break statements with goto statements in a given loop or switch statement.
 *
 * This function takes a loop or switch statement as input and replaces all its break
 * statements with equivalent goto statements. It does this by first identifying the
 * body of the loop or switch statement, then finding all the break statements within
 * that body. For each break statement found, it creates a new label statement and inserts
 * it after the loop or switch statement, then replaces the break statement with a goto
 * statement pointing to the newly created label.
 *
 * The function uses a recursive approach to traverse the abstract syntax tree (AST)
 * of the input code and identify the relevant statements.
 *
 * @param loopOrSwitch A pointer to an SgStatement representing the loop or switch
 *                     statement in which to replace break statements with goto
 *                     statements. Must be non-NULL and point to a valid loop or
 *                     switch statement.
 *
 * @return None (void function)
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input code by replacing break statements
 *          with goto statements. The original break statements are lost in this
 *          process.
 *
 * @pre The input loopOrSwitch must be non-NULL and point to a valid loop or switch
 *      statement.
 *
 * @post All break statements within the body of the input loop or switch statement
 *       have been replaced with equivalent goto statements.
 *
 * @note This function uses a static counter to generate unique label names for
 *       the newly created labels. This means that the function is not thread-safe
 *       and should not be used concurrently by multiple threads.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* loop = ...; // some loop statement
 *           SageInterface::changeBreakStatementsToGotos(loop);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfFor(SgForStatement* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of a for statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function takes a for statement as input and ensures that its body is a basic block. 
 * If the body is not already a basic block, it creates a new basic block containing the original body statements
 * and updates the for statement to use this new basic block as its body.
 *
 * @param fs The for statement whose body needs to be ensured to be a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block that is now the body of the input for statement.
 *
 * @warning If the input for statement is null, the function will not work correctly.
 *
 * @pre The input for statement must not be null.
 *
 * @post The body of the input for statement is guaranteed to be a basic block after this function returns.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the input AST and records the added SgBasicBlock for potential future undo operations.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgForStatement* fs = ...; // obtain a for statement from somewhere
 *           SgBasicBlock* bb = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfFor(fs);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(bb));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfUpcForAll(SgUpcForAllStatement* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the loop body of an UPC for-all statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function checks if the loop body of an UPC for-all statement is already a basic block,
 * and if not, creates a new basic block to serve as the loop body. It uses an auxiliary
 * function ensureBasicBlock_aux() to perform the actual work.
 *
 * @param fs The UPC for-all statement whose loop body is to be ensured as a basic block.
 *          Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block that serves as the loop body of the input UPC for-all statement.
 *
 * @warning If the input UPC for-all statement is NULL, this function will assert and terminate.
 *
 * @pre The input UPC for-all statement must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The loop body of the input UPC for-all statement will be a basic block.
 *
 * @note This function uses an auxiliary function ensureBasicBlock_aux() to perform the actual work.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgUpcForAllStatement* fs = ...; // assume fs is initialized properly
 *           SgBasicBlock* bb = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfUpcForAll(fs);
 *           assert(bb != NULL); // the loop body of fs should now be a basic block
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfWhile(SgWhileStmt* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of a while statement is wrapped in a basic block.
 *
 * This function takes a while statement as input and checks if its body is already
 * a basic block. If not, it creates a new basic block containing the original body,
 * sets this new basic block as the body of the while statement, and records the
 * transformation for potential later undoing.
 *
 * The function returns the basic block that serves as the body of the while statement.
 *
 * @param fs A pointer to the SgWhileStmt node representing the while statement whose body is to be ensured as a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgBasicBlock node serving as the body of the input while statement. This may be either an existing or newly created basic block, depending on the original state of the while statement's body.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input while statement is valid and well-formed; it does not perform any checks for this.
 *
 * @pre The input while statement fs must exist and have a non-null body.
 *
 * @post The body of the input while statement fs will be a basic block, either existing or newly created.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the input while statement's AST representation by potentially adding a new basic block node.
 *
 * @example
 * @code
 *   // Create an example while statement with a non-basic-block body
 *   SgWhileStmt* ws = SageBuilder::buildWhileStmt(...);
 *   
 *   // Ensure its body is wrapped in a basic block
 *   SgBasicBlock* bb = ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfWhile(ws);
 *   
 *   // Now the while statement's body should be a basic block
 * @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfDoWhile(SgDoWhileStmt* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of a do-while statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function transforms the body of a do-while statement into a basic block if it's not already one. 
 * It uses the SageBuilder to create a new basic block and replaces the original body with it.
 * The newly created basic block is then recorded for potential later undoing of this transformation.
 *
 * @param fs The do-while statement whose body is to be transformed into a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block that represents the body of the do-while statement. 
 *         This will either be the original body if it was already a basic block, or the newly created one.
 *
 * @warning If the input fs is null, this function may crash or produce undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input fs must be a valid SgDoWhileStmt pointer.
 *
 * @post The body of the do-while statement pointed to by fs will be a basic block.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the AST and records the transformation for potential later undoing.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgDoWhileStmt* doWhileStmt = ...; // some do-while statement
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           SgBasicBlock* bodyAsBasicBlock = sage.ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfDoWhile(doWhileStmt);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(bodyAsBasicBlock));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfSwitch(SgSwitchStatement* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of a switch statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function checks if the body of a given switch statement is already a basic block. 
 * If not, it creates a new basic block containing the current body and sets it as the new body of the switch statement.
 * The newly created basic block is also recorded for potential future undo operations.
 *
 * @param fs The switch statement whose body needs to be ensured as a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block that serves as the body of the switch statement. 
 *         This can either be the original body if it was already a basic block, or a newly created basic block.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input switch statement by potentially replacing its body with a new basic block.
 *
 * @pre The input switch statement must not be null.
 *
 * @post The body of the input switch statement is guaranteed to be a basic block after this function has been called.
 *
 * @note This function uses SageBuilder to create a new basic block if necessary, and records the created basic block for potential future undo operations.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgSwitchStatement* switch_stmt = ...; // Assuming some switch statement
 *           SgBasicBlock* body_block = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfSwitch(switch_stmt);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(body_block));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfCaseOption(SgCaseOptionStmt* cs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of a case option statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function checks if the body of a given case option statement is already
 * a basic block. If not, it creates a new basic block containing the original
 * body and replaces the old body with the new one. The newly created basic
 * block is then recorded for potential future undo operations.
 *
 * @param cs The case option statement whose body needs to be ensured as a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block that serves as the body of the given case
 *         option statement. This can either be the original body if it was
 *         already a basic block, or a newly created one.
 *
 * @note The function modifies the input case option statement by replacing its
 *       body with a new basic block if necessary. The old body is not deleted,
 *       but instead becomes part of the new basic block.
 *
 * @pre The input case option statement must be valid and have a non-null body.
 *
 * @post The body of the given case option statement is guaranteed to be a basic
 *       block after this function returns. The returned pointer remains valid
 *       until the case option statement or its body is modified again.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks on the contents of the
 *          original body before wrapping it into a new basic block. If the
 *          original body contains invalid statements, they will still be part
 *          of the new basic block and may cause issues later on.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgCaseOptionStmt* cs = ...; // obtain a case option statement
 *           SgBasicBlock* bb = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfCaseOption(cs);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(bb));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfDefaultOption(SgDefaultOptionStmt * cs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of a default option statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function takes a default option statement as input and checks if its body
 * is already a basic block. If not, it wraps the existing body in a new basic block,
 * updates the parent-child relationships, and records the transformation for potential future undo operations.
 *
 * @param cs The input default option statement whose body needs to be ensured as a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block that is now the body of the default option statement.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input default option statement is valid and has a non-null body.
 *           If this assumption does not hold, undefined behavior may occur.
 *
 * @pre The input default option statement must have a valid body.
 *
 * @post The body of the default option statement is guaranteed to be a basic block after this function returns.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the input default option statement by replacing its body with a new basic block if necessary.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgDefaultOptionStmt* cs = ...; // assume some valid default option statement
 *           SgBasicBlock* b = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfDefaultOption(cs);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(b));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsTrueBodyOfIf(SgIfStmt* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the true body of an if statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function checks if the true body of an if statement (fs) is already a basic block.
 * If not, it creates a new basic block wrapping the existing statements and replaces
 * the original statements with this new basic block. The newly created basic block
 * is then recorded for potential future undo operations.
 *
 * @param fs The input if statement whose true body needs to be ensured as a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the SgBasicBlock that represents the true body of the if statement.
 *         This may be either the original basic block (if it existed) or a newly created one.
 *
 * @warning If the input if statement is null, this function will likely crash or produce undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input if statement (fs) must not be null.
 *
 * @post The true body of the input if statement (fs) is guaranteed to be a basic block after calling this function.
 *
 * @note This function modifies the input if statement by replacing its true body with a new basic block if necessary.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgIfStmt* if_stmt = ...; // assume some if statement is created or obtained
 *           SgBasicBlock* true_body_block = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsTrueBodyOfIf(if_stmt);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(true_body_block));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsFalseBodyOfIf(SgIfStmt* fs , bool createEmptyBody /* = true*/)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the false body of an if statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function checks if the false body of an if statement is already a basic block. 
 * If it is not, and the input flag createEmptyBody is set to true (default), 
 * this function creates a new empty basic block as the false body of the if statement.
 * Otherwise, if the flag is set to false, it returns NULL without modifying the if statement,
 * but only if there was no existing false body or the existing false body is a null statement.
 *
 * @param fs The input if statement whose false body is to be ensured as a basic block.
 *          Must not be NULL.
 * @param createEmptyBody A flag indicating whether an empty basic block should be created 
 *                        as the false body of the if statement when it does not already exist. 
 *                        Defaults to true.
 *
 * @return The SgBasicBlock that is ensured as the false body of the input if statement, 
 *         or NULL if createEmptyBody is set to false and there was no existing false body 
 *         or the existing false body is a null statement.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input if statement by setting its false body 
 *          to a new basic block if necessary. It also records the added SgBasicBlock 
 *          for potential future undo operations.
 *
 * @pre The input if statement fs must not be NULL.
 * @post If createEmptyBody is set to true, the returned SgBasicBlock is guaranteed to be non-NULL.
 *
 * @note This function uses SageBuilder::buildBasicBlock() to create a new basic block 
 *       from the existing false body of the if statement when necessary. It also calls 
 *       recordNormalizations() to save the added SgBasicBlock for potential future undo operations.
 *
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfCatch(SgCatchOptionStmt* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of a catch statement is wrapped in a basic block.
 *
 * This function checks if the body of a given catch statement is already a basic block. 
 * If not, it creates a new basic block wrapping the existing body and sets it as the new body of the catch statement.
 *
 * @param fs The catch option statement whose body needs to be wrapped in a basic block.
 *           Must be non-null and have a valid body that can be converted into a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block wrapping the body of the catch statement. 
 *         If the input is invalid or the operation fails, the return value is undefined.
 *
 * @warning The function modifies the input catch option statement by replacing its body with a new basic block.
 *          It also sets the parent of the new basic block to the catch statement.
 *
 * @pre The input catch option statement must be non-null and have a valid body that can be converted into a basic block.
 *
 * @post The body of the input catch statement is guaranteed to be a basic block after this function returns.
 *
 * @note This function uses SageBuilder::buildBasicBlock() to create a new basic block if necessary, 
 *       and sets the parent of the new basic block using set_parent().
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgCatchOptionStmt* catch_stmt = ...; // assume some valid catch statement
 *           SgBasicBlock* bb = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfCatch(catch_stmt);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(bb));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfOmpBodyStmt(SgOmpBodyStatement* fs)
 *
 * @brief Ensures that the body of an OpenMP body statement is a basic block.
 *
 * This function checks if the body of an OpenMP body statement is already a basic block. 
 * If not, it constructs a new basic block containing the original body and replaces the original body with the new basic block.
 *
 * @param fs The OpenMP body statement whose body needs to be ensured as a basic block.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the basic block that serves as the body of the input OpenMP body statement.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input OpenMP body statement by replacing its original body with a new basic block if necessary.
 *
 * @pre The input OpenMP body statement must be valid and have a non-null body.
 *
 * @post The body of the input OpenMP body statement is guaranteed to be a basic block after this function returns.
 *
 * @note This function performs a recursive operation by constructing a new basic block containing the original body if necessary. However, it does not recursively traverse the entire AST.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgOmpBodyStatement* ompBodyStmt = ...; // obtain an OpenMP body statement
 *           SgBasicBlock* basicBlock = SageInterface::ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfOmpBodyStmt(ompBodyStmt);
 *           ROSE_ASSERT(isSgBasicBlock(basicBlock));
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void cleanupNontransformedBasicBlockNode(SgNode* n) 
 * @brief Replaces normalized basic blocks with non-transformed ones.
 *
 * This function takes a Sage node as input, finds all normalized basic blocks
 * inside it, and replaces them with their non-transformed counterparts. It also
 * updates the parent-child relationships between nodes accordingly.
 *
 * @param n The Sage node to process.
 */

/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::recordNormalizations(SgStatement* s)
 *
 * @brief Records where normalization has been done for token-based unparsing.
 *
 * This function keeps track of the SgBasicBlock nodes that have undergone normalization,
 * allowing for denormalization to be performed later as necessary. It is used to generate
 * minimal diffs during the unparsing process.
 *
 * The function takes a statement (s) and checks if it is an instance of SgBasicBlock.
 * If so, it adds the block to the list of added basic block nodes.
 *
 * @param s The statement that may contain normalization. Must be non-null.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any error checking on the input parameter (s).
 *          It assumes that the caller has ensured the parameter is valid and non-null.
 *
 * @pre The input parameter (s) must be a valid SgStatement object.
 *
 * @post If the input statement (s) is an instance of SgBasicBlock, it will be added to
 *       the list of added basic block nodes.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to verify that the input parameter (s)
 *       can be cast to a non-null SgBasicBlock pointer. If this assertion fails,
 *       program execution will terminate.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* statement = ...; // Obtain an SgStatement object
 *           SageInterface sageInterface;
 *           sageInterface.recordNormalizations(statement);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn int SageInterface::normalizeArrowExpWithAddressOfLeftOperand(SgNode* root, bool transformationGeneratedOnly)
 *
 * @brief Normalizes arrow expressions with address of left operand in an AST.
 *
 * This function traverses the abstract syntax tree (AST) rooted at @p root and 
 * searches for arrow expressions where the left operand is an address-of operator
 * applied to a variable reference. It then transforms these expressions into dot 
 * expressions by replacing the arrow expression with a new dot expression that 
 * has copies of the original operands.
 *
 * The function returns the number of transformations performed.
 *
 * @param root The root node of the AST to be traversed.
 * @param transformationGeneratedOnly If true, only consider nodes generated during source code transformation. Defaults to true.
 *
 * @return The number of arrow expressions transformed into dot expressions.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the input AST by replacing arrow expressions with dot expressions.
 *
 * @pre The input AST is well-formed and @p root is a valid node in the AST.
 *
 * @post The input AST has been modified to replace arrow expressions with dot expressions where applicable.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive descent to traverse the AST and may cause stack overflow for very large inputs.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SageInterface sage;
 *            SgNode* root = ...; // obtain the root node of an AST
 *            int transformations = sage.normalizeArrowExpWithAddressOfLeftOperand(root);
 *            cout << "Transformations performed: " << transformations << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::isBodyStatement (SgStatement* s)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given statement is a body statement.
 *
 * This function checks if the input SgStatement pointer represents a body
 * statement within its parent node. A body statement is defined as a
 * statement that serves as the body of a control flow construct, such as a loop,
 * conditional, or switch statement.
 *
 * The function uses a recursive approach to traverse the abstract syntax tree
 * and determine if the input statement matches any known body statements.
 *
 * @param s The SgStatement pointer to be checked. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the statement is a body statement; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input statement has already been
 *          validated as a non-NULL, valid SgStatement object.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must have a parent node in the abstract syntax tree.
 *
 * @post If this function returns true, the statement is guaranteed to be a body
 *       statement within its parent node.
 *
 * @note This function uses a switch statement to handle different types of
 *       statements. New cases may need to be added if additional statement types
 *       are introduced in the future.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgStatement* stmt = ...; // obtain an SgStatement pointer
 *            bool isBodyStmt = SageInterface::isBodyStatement(stmt);
 *            if (isBodyStmt) {
 *              // handle the statement as a body statement
 *            }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn void SageInterface::changeAllBodiesToBlocks(SgNode* top, bool createEmptyBody /*= true*/)
 *
 * @brief Changes all bodies in the given SgNode to basic blocks.
 *
 * This function traverses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) rooted at the given node
 * and ensures that every body is a basic block. A basic block is a sequence of
 * statements with no control flow between them, except possibly for the last
 * statement in the block which may be a jump or return.
 *
 * The function uses a visitor pattern to traverse the AST. It visits each node
 * and checks if it needs to be modified to ensure that its body is a basic block.
 * If necessary, it calls other functions (e.g., ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfFor,
 * ensureBasicBlockAsBodyOfWhile, etc.) to perform the actual modifications.
 *
 * @param top The root node of the AST to traverse.
 * @param createEmptyBody A flag indicating whether an empty body should be created
 *                       if one does not already exist. Defaults to true.
 *
 * @return None
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the AST in place and does not return a new tree.
 *
 * @pre The input node is a valid SgNode.
 *
 * @post All bodies in the AST rooted at the input node are basic blocks.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to traverse the AST. It may cause
 *       a stack overflow if the input tree is very deep.
 *
 */
/**
 * @fn SgBasicBlock* SageInterface::makeSingleStatementBodyToBlock(SgStatement* singleStmt)
 *
 * @brief Converts a single statement into a basic block to serve as the body of its parent statement.
 *
 * This function takes a pointer to an SgStatement and returns a pointer to an SgBasicBlock.
 * It ensures that the input statement is not NULL, is not already a basic block,
 * and is a body statement. The function then navigates through different types
 * of parent statements (e.g., for loops, while loops, if-else statements) to create
 * or retrieve the corresponding basic block serving as their bodies.
 *
 * @param singleStmt A pointer to an SgStatement that needs to be converted into a basic block.
 *                   Must not be NULL and must be a body statement.
 *
 * @return A pointer to an SgBasicBlock representing the body of the parent statement
 *         containing the input statement. Returns NULL if the conversion fails.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input statement has already been confirmed
 *          to be a valid body statement and its parent statement is properly formed.
 *          Failure to meet these preconditions may result in undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre singleStmt must not be NULL, must not be a basic block itself,
 *      and must be confirmed as a body statement before calling this function.
 *
 * @post If successful, the returned SgBasicBlock is guaranteed to represent
 *       the body of the parent statement containing the input statement.
 *
 * @note The implementation relies on a series of assertions at the beginning
 *       of the function to ensure that the preconditions are met. It also uses
 *       a switch-case block to handle different types of parent statements,
 *       each requiring distinct handling for creating or retrieving their bodies.
 *
 */
/**
 * @fn unsigned long long SageInterface::getIntegerConstantValue(SgValueExp* expr)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the integer constant value from a given SgValueExp expression.
 *
 * This function extracts the integer constant value from an input SgValueExp
 * expression, which represents a constant value in the ROSE AST. The function
 * handles different types of constants (e.g., char, unsigned char, short, etc.) and
 * returns their corresponding values as an unsigned long long.
 *
 * @param expr A pointer to an SgValueExp object representing the constant expression.
 *             Must be non-null.
 *
 * @return The integer constant value extracted from the input expression. Returns 0
 *         if the function encounters an invalid or unsupported expression type.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any overflow checks on the returned value.
 *          It is the caller's responsibility to ensure that the result fits within
 *          the range of an unsigned long long.
 *
 * @pre The input expression must be a valid SgValueExp object representing a constant value.
 *
 * @post The function returns the integer constant value if the input expression is valid;
 *       otherwise, it prints an error message and asserts false.
 *
 * @note This function uses a switch statement to handle different types of constants
 *       based on their variant type. It also includes a default branch that handles
 *       unsupported or invalid expression types by printing an error message and asserting false.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgValueExp* expr = ...; // Initialize the expression object
 *           unsigned long long value = SageInterface::getIntegerConstantValue(expr);
 *           printf("The integer constant value is: %llu\n", value);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::getDependentDeclarations(SgStatement* stmt)
 *
 * @brief Returns a list of dependent declarations for a given statement.
 *
 * This function computes the list of dependent declarations for any input statement.
 * Dependent declarations include functions called, types referenced in variable declarations, etc.
 *
 * The function uses a recursive algorithm to traverse the abstract syntax tree (AST) and collect
 * the dependent declarations. It also sorts the collected declarations based on their appearance order
 * in the source code.
 *
 * @param stmt The input statement for which the dependent declarations are to be calculated.
 *             Must be a valid SgStatement pointer.
 *
 * @return A list of dependent declarations (SgDeclarationStatement pointers) for the input statement.
 *
 * @warning If the input statement is null or invalid, the function may produce undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input statement must be a valid SgStatement pointer.
 *
 * @post The returned list of dependent declarations is sorted based on their appearance order in the source code.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm and may cause stack overflow for very large input statements.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgStatement* stmt = ...; // assume stmt is initialized with a valid statement
 *           SageInterface sage;
 *           vector<SgDeclarationStatement*> dependentDecls = sage.getDependentDeclarations(stmt);
 *           for (vector<SgDeclarationStatement*>::const_iterator iter = dependentDecls.begin();
 *                iter != dependentDecls.end(); ++iter) {
 *             cout << "Dependent declaration: " << (*iter)->class_name() << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgCommaOpExp* SageInterface::insertBeforeUsingCommaOp(SgExpression* new_exp, SgExpression* anchor_exp)
 *
 * @brief Inserts a new expression before an existing anchor expression using a comma operator.
 *
 * This function inserts a new expression before an existing anchor expression in the abstract syntax tree (AST) by creating a new SgCommaOpExp node. The new expression is inserted as the left-hand side operand of the comma operator, and the anchor expression becomes the right-hand side operand.
 *
 * @param new_exp The new expression to be inserted before the anchor expression. Must not be NULL.
 * @param anchor_exp The existing anchor expression in the AST. Must not be NULL and must not be the same as new_exp.
 *
 * @return A pointer to the newly created SgCommaOpExp node, or NULL if the insertion fails.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input expressions are valid and well-formed. It does not perform any error checking on the inputs.
 *
 * @pre The anchor expression must have a non-NULL parent node in the AST.
 *
 * @post The newly created SgCommaOpExp node is inserted into the AST, replacing the original anchor expression.
 *
 * @note This function uses a shallow copy of the original anchor expression. If the original expression is modified later, the changes will not be reflected in the new SgCommaOpExp node.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create two sample expressions
 *           SgExpression* exp1 = buildIntVal(5);
 *           SgExpression* exp2 = buildIntVal(10);
 *
 *           // Insert exp1 before exp2 using a comma operator
 *           SgCommaOpExp* result = insertBeforeUsingCommaOp(exp1, exp2);
 *
 *           // Print the resulting AST
 *           printAST(result);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgCommaOpExp* SageInterface::insertAfterUsingCommaOp (SgExpression* new_exp, SgExpression* anchor_exp, SgStatement** temp_decl /* = NULL */, SgVarRefExp** temp_ref /* = NULL */)
 *
 * @brief Inserts an expression after another using a comma operator.
 *
 * This function inserts the given expression `new_exp` after the given anchor
 * expression `anchor_exp` in the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) of the source code,
 * using a comma operator. It creates a new temporary variable declaration and
 * uses it to store the value of the anchor expression, then replaces the anchor
 * expression with the new comma operator expression.
 *
 * The function takes four parameters:
 * - `new_exp`: The new expression to be inserted after the anchor expression.
 * - `anchor_exp`: The anchor expression in the AST where the new expression is to be inserted.
 * - `temp_decl`: A pointer to a pointer of type SgStatement, which will hold the newly created temporary variable declaration. Can be NULL if not needed.
 * - `temp_ref`: A pointer to a pointer of type SgVarRefExp, which will hold the reference to the newly created temporary variable. Can be NULL if not needed.
 *
 * @param new_exp The new expression to be inserted after the anchor expression.
 *                Must not be NULL.
 * @param anchor_exp The anchor expression in the AST where the new expression is to be inserted.
 *                   Must not be NULL and must not be the same as `new_exp`.
 * @param temp_decl A pointer to a pointer of type SgStatement, which will hold the newly created temporary variable declaration. Can be NULL if not needed.
 * @param temp_ref A pointer to a pointer of type SgVarRefExp, which will hold the reference to the newly created temporary variable. Can be NULL if not needed.
 *
 * @return The newly created comma operator expression that replaces the anchor expression in the AST.
 *
 * @warning This function modifies the AST of the source code and may have unintended consequences if not used carefully.
 *
 * @pre The input parameters `new_exp` and `anchor_exp` must not be NULL, and `anchor_exp` must not be the same as `new_exp`.
 * @post The anchor expression in the AST is replaced with the new comma operator expression, and the temporary variable declaration and reference are created if requested.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive algorithm to create the new comma operator expression and may cause stack overflow for very deep recursion.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgExpression* anchor_exp = ...; // some expression in the AST
 *           SgExpression* new_exp = ...; // some new expression to be inserted
 *           SgStatement** temp_decl = NULL;
 *           SgVarRefExp** temp_ref = NULL;
 *           SgCommaOpExp* result = insertAfterUsingCommaOp(new_exp, anchor_exp, temp_decl, temp_ref);
 *          @endcode
 */

/**
 * @brief Empty function or ill-formed function detected.
 */

/**
 * @fn std::pair<SgStatement*, SgInitializedName*> wrapFunction(SgFunctionDeclaration& definingDeclaration, NameGen nameGen)
 *
 * @brief Wraps a given function declaration with a new statement and initial declaration.
 *
 * This function generates a new pair consisting of an SgStatement* and an
 * SgInitializedName*, effectively wrapping the input SgFunctionDeclaration
 * within these constructs. The purpose is to transform or augment the original
 * function declaration for further processing, analysis, or transformation in
 * the context of source code manipulation.
 *
 * @param definingDeclaration The SgFunctionDeclaration object that needs to be wrapped.
 *                             Must be a valid and complete function declaration.
 * @param nameGen             A NameGen object used to generate names within the wrapping process.
 *                             Should provide meaningful and unique names for the generated constructs.
 *
 * @return A pair containing an SgStatement* representing the wrapped statement and
 *         an SgInitializedName* representing the initial declaration. Both elements
 *         are newly created as a result of this function's operation.
 *
 * @warning The caller is responsible for ensuring that the input SgFunctionDeclaration
 *          does not contain any syntax errors or semantic inconsistencies, as these
 *          may lead to undefined behavior during the wrapping process.
 *
 * @pre The input SgFunctionDeclaration must be a valid and complete function declaration,
 *      adhering to the syntax rules of the programming language being manipulated.
 *
 * @post A new pair of constructs (statement and initial declaration) is created, which
 *       encapsulates the original function declaration. These new constructs are ready for
 *       further processing or analysis.
 *
 * @note This function does not modify the original SgFunctionDeclaration object but instead
 *       generates new objects to achieve the wrapping effect.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Assuming we have a valid SgFunctionDeclaration and NameGen
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* funcDecl = ...;
 *           NameGen nameGenerator = ...;
 *
 *           std::pair<SgStatement*, SgInitializedName*> wrappedPair =
 *               wrapFunction(*funcDecl, nameGenerator);
 *
 *           // Now 'wrappedPair' contains the new statement and initial declaration
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgInitializedName& SageInterface::getFirstVariable(SgVariableDeclaration& vardecl)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the first variable from a given variable declaration.
 *
 * This function returns the first variable declared in the input variable
 * declaration. It assumes that at least one variable is present in the
 * declaration and does not perform any checks for empty declarations.
 *
 * The function uses the ROSE Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) infrastructure to
 * navigate the variable declaration and retrieve its first variable. This
 * function is typically used in static analysis and source code transformation
 * contexts where access to individual variables within a declaration is required.
 *
 * @param vardecl A reference to the SgVariableDeclaration object containing the
 *                variable(s) of interest. Must not be null, and must contain at least
 *                one variable.
 *
 * @return A reference to the first SgInitializedName object in the input variable
 *         declaration.
 *
 * @warning This function does not perform any checks for null or empty inputs,
 *          and assumes that the caller has validated the input prior to invocation.
 *
 * @pre The input variable declaration must contain at least one variable, i.e.,
 *      vardecl.get_variables().size() > 0.
 *
 * @post The returned SgInitializedName object is guaranteed to be non-null and
 *       corresponds to the first variable declared in the input variable declaration.
 *
 * @note This function uses a const reference to the input variable declaration
 *       to avoid unnecessary copies or modifications to the original AST structure.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *            SgVariableDeclaration* vardecl = ...; // assume initialized
 *            SgInitializedName& firstVar = getFirstVariable(*vardecl);
 *            cout << "First variable: " << firstVar.get_name() << endl;
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool SageInterface::hasTemplateSyntax(const SgName & name)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given name contains template syntax.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given SgName object represents a name with
 * template syntax. It checks for the presence of '<' and '>' characters in the
 * string representation of the name, which are indicative of template syntax in C++.
 *
 * @param name The SgName object to be checked for template syntax.
 *
 * @return True if the given name contains template syntax, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function is designed to avoid misidentifying "operator<()" as valid
 *       template syntax. It also handles a special case where the name string is "<unnamed>".
 *
 * @warning The implementation of this function may not cover all possible cases of
 *          template syntax in C++ names, and may need to be updated for future versions
 *          of the language.
 *
 * @pre The input SgName object must be valid and properly initialized.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the given name contains template syntax.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgName name("myTemplateClass");
 *           if (SageInterface::hasTemplateSyntax(name)) {
 *             printf("The name %s contains template syntax.\n", name.getString().c_str());
 *           } else {
 *             printf("The name %s does not contain template syntax.\n", name.getString().c_str());
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SageInterface::collectSourceSequenceNumbers(SgNode* astNode)
 *
 * @brief Collects source sequence numbers from an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) node.
 *
 * This function traverses the AST rooted at the given node and collects source
 * sequence numbers. The traversal is performed in a preorder manner, visiting
 * the current node before its children. The collected source sequence numbers are
 * stored in a set data structure to ensure uniqueness.
 *
 * @param astNode The root node of the Abstract Syntax Tree to traverse.
 *                Must be a non-null pointer to a valid SgNode object.
 *
 * @return A set of unique source sequence numbers found during the traversal.
 *
 * @warning The function assumes that the input AST node is well-formed and
 *          correctly represents the source code. If the AST is malformed,
 *          the behavior of this function is undefined.
 *
 * @pre astNode != NULL
 *
 * @post The returned set contains all unique source sequence numbers found in
 *       the subtree rooted at astNode.
 *
 * @note This function uses a preorder traversal strategy, which may not be
 *       suitable for all use cases. If a different traversal order is required,
 *       consider using a custom traversal algorithm or modifying this function
 *       accordingly.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgNode* astRoot = ...; // obtain the root node of the AST
 *           SageInterface::collectSourceSequenceNumbers(astRoot);
 *           // process the collected source sequence numbers
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool HasNoThrowAssign(const SgType * const inputType)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type has an assignment operator with no throw guarantee.
 *
 * This function determines whether the assignment operator of a given type
 * guarantees not to throw any exceptions. It recursively checks all member
 * functions in the class hierarchy, looking for an assignment operator with
 * the noexcept attribute (or its GNU equivalent).
 *
 * @param inputType The SgType object representing the type to be checked.
 *
 * @return True if the assignment operator of the given type guarantees not to throw any exceptions; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function does not support C++11's noexcept expressions yet. It only checks for the GNU attribute nothrow.
 *
 * @pre The inputType parameter must not be null.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the assignment operator of the given type guarantees not to throw any exceptions.
 *
 * @note This function uses a recursive approach to check all member functions in the class hierarchy. It may exhibit quadratic behavior for large class hierarchies.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Initialize myType with some type
 *           if (HasNoThrowAssign(myType)) {
 *               std::cout << "Assignment operator of " << myType->get_name().getString() << " has no throw guarantee." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "Assignment operator of " << myType->get_name().getString() << " may throw exceptions." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool HasNoThrowCopy(const SgType * const inputType)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a type has a no-throw copy constructor.
 *
 * This function checks if the given type has a copy constructor that does not throw any exceptions. 
 * It first strips away typedef and array types, then checks if the remaining type has a trivial copy constructor. 
 * If not, it iterates over all member functions in the class hierarchy of the given type to find a copy constructor with the noexcept attribute.
 *
 * @param inputType The type for which to check the no-throw copy constructor property.
 *
 * @return True if the type has a no-throw copy constructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function may return incorrect results if the class hierarchy of the given type contains constructors that cannot be parsed correctly.
 *
 * @pre inputType must not be null.
 *
 * @post The result is true if and only if the type has a no-throw copy constructor.
 *
 * @note This function does not support C++11 noexcept expressions yet.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // assume some type is assigned here
 *           if (HasNoThrowCopy(myType)) {
 *               printf("The type has a no-throw copy constructor.\n");
 *           } else {
 *               printf("The type does not have a no-throw copy constructor.\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool HasNoThrowConstructor(const SgType * const inputType)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type has a no-throw constructor.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given type has a constructor that does not throw any exceptions. 
 * It recursively checks all constructors in the class hierarchy of the input type, ensuring they are either trivial or marked with the noexcept attribute.
 *
 * @param inputType A pointer to an SgType object representing the type to check for a no-throw constructor.
 *                  Must be non-null and represent a valid type.
 *
 * @return True if the input type has a no-throw constructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is properly initialized and represents a valid class or struct.
 *
 * @pre The input type must not be null and must represent a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @post If true is returned, it guarantees that the input type has at least one constructor that does not throw exceptions.
 *
 * @note This function only checks for constructors with no arguments. Constructors with arguments are not considered.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // assume a valid type is obtained
 *           if (HasNoThrowConstructor(myType)) {
 *               std::cout << "The type has a no-throw constructor." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The type does not have a no-throw constructor." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool HasTrivialAssign(const SgType * const inputType)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a type has trivial assignment.
 *
 * This function checks whether an assignment to an object of the given type can be
 * performed using a simple bitwise copy. The check is based on the properties of the
 * type, including its size and layout in memory.
 *
 * @param inputType A pointer to the SgType object representing the type to be checked.
 *
 * @return True if the assignment can be performed trivially, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is valid and correctly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input type must not be null or undefined.
 *
 * @post The result of this function does not depend on any external factors and
 *       remains consistent for a given input type.
 *
 * @note The actual implementation details of this function may vary depending on the
 *       specific requirements and constraints of the project.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           const SgType* myType = ...;  // Initialize the type object
 *           if (HasTrivialAssign(myType)) {
 *               std::cout << "Assignment is trivial." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "Assignment is not trivial." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool HasTrivialCopy(const SgType * const inputType)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type has a trivial copy constructor.
 *
 * This function determines whether the provided type has a trivial copy
 * constructor, which means that the type's objects can be copied using a simple
 * bitwise copy operation. The function takes into account various factors,
 * including the type's properties and its inheritance hierarchy.
 *
 * @param inputType A pointer to the SgType object representing the type to check.
 *                  Must not be null.
 *
 * @return True if the type has a trivial copy constructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function is used internally by the compiler and should not be
 *       called directly from user code. It is provided for informational
 *       purposes only.
 *
 * @warning The result of this function may change depending on the specific
 *          compilation options and target architecture.
 *
 * @pre inputType must not be null.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the type has a trivial copy constructor.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // assume some valid SgType object
 *           bool hasTrivialCopy = HasTrivialCopy(myType);
 *           if (hasTrivialCopy) {
 *               std::cout << "The type has a trivial copy constructor." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The type does not have a trivial copy constructor." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool HasTrivialConstructor(const SgType * const inputType)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a type has a trivial constructor.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given type has a trivial constructor,
 * i.e., a constructor that performs no action. The check is performed based on
 * the properties of the input type.
 *
 * @param inputType A pointer to the SgType object representing the type to be checked.
 *                  Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the type has a trivial constructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function is a wrapper around another overloaded version that
