/**
 * @fn bool isUnionType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is a union type.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given SgType represents a union type in the abstract syntax tree (AST).
 * It checks if the input type is an instance of SgClassType and then verifies if its declaration statement corresponds to a union declaration.
 *
 * @param t The SgType object to be checked. Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return True if the given type represents a union type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgType is valid and properly initialized.
 *
 * @pre The input SgType must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The function will return true if the type is a union type, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE library's AST traversal and manipulation APIs to inspect the given type.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* type = ...; // obtain an SgType object from somewhere
 *           if (isUnionType(type)) {
 *               std::cout << "The type is a union type." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The type is not a union type." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn SgType* getBaseFromType(SgType* t)
 *
 * @brief Retrieves the base type from a given type.
 *
 * This function recursively dereferences the input type until it reaches
 * a non-dereferenceable type, which is considered the base type. The base
 * type is then returned as the result of this function.
 *
 * @param t The type for which to retrieve the base type.
 *          Must not be NULL.
 *
 * @return The base type of the input type t. Returns t if it cannot be further dereferenced.
 *
 * @warning If the input type t is a pointer to a non-dereferenceable type,
 *          this function will return the original type t without modification.
 *
 * @pre The input type t must not be NULL.
 *
 * @post The returned base type is guaranteed to be non-NULL and valid for use in
 *       subsequent operations.
 *
 * @note This function assumes that the input type t has been properly initialized
 *       and is a valid SgType object. Failure to meet these conditions may result
 *       in undefined behavior or incorrect results.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Assume myType is properly initialized
 *           SgType* baseType = getBaseFromType(myType);
 *           // Use the retrieved base type as needed
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn static bool acceptsArguments(SgFunctionDeclaration* decl, std::list<SgType*> args, bool allowDefaultArgs=true)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given set of arguments matches the parameters of a function declaration.
 *
 * This function takes in a SgFunctionDeclaration pointer and a list of SgType pointers as input,
 * and checks if the types of the arguments match the types of the function's parameters. It also
 * considers default arguments if allowDefaultArgs is true.
 *
 * @param decl The SgFunctionDeclaration pointer representing the function to be checked.
 * @param args The list of SgType pointers representing the arguments to be matched with the function's parameters.
 * @param allowDefaultArgs A boolean flag indicating whether to consider default arguments or not. Default value is true.
 *
 * @return True if the types of the arguments match the types of the function's parameters, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning The function does not perform any checks on the validity of the input pointers.
 *
 * @pre The SgFunctionDeclaration pointer and the list of SgType pointers must be valid.
 *
 * @post None
 *
 * @note This function is used to check if a given set of arguments can be passed to a function without causing type mismatches.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           // Create a SgFunctionDeclaration pointer representing a sample function
 *           SgFunctionDeclaration* decl = ...;
 *           
 *           // Create a list of SgType pointers representing the arguments to be passed to the function
 *           std::list<SgType*> args;
 *           args.push_back(...);
 *           
 *           // Check if the arguments match the function's parameters
 *           bool result = acceptsArguments(decl, args);
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn vector<SgMemberFunctionDeclaration*> GetAllMemberFunctionsInClassHierarchy(SgType *type)
 *
 * @brief Walks over the call hierarchy and collects all member functions in a vector.
 *
 * This function traverses the class hierarchy of a given type and accumulates
 * all member functions into a vector. It uses the ClassHierarchyWrapper to 
 * determine the ancestor classes and then iterates through their members to find
 * the member functions.
 *
 * @param type The SgType representing the class for which to collect member functions.
 *             Must be a complete class type, i.e., not an incomplete or forward-declared type.
 *
 * @return A vector of SgMemberFunctionDeclaration pointers containing all member functions 
 *         found in the class hierarchy of the input type. If no member functions are found, 
 *         an empty vector is returned.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is a complete class type and does not
 *          perform any checks for incomplete or forward-declared types. It also assumes that
 *          the ClassHierarchyWrapper has been properly initialized before calling this function.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgType pointer, i.e., not null or invalid.
 *      The ClassHierarchyWrapper must have been properly initialized before calling this function.
 *
 * @post The returned vector contains all member functions found in the class hierarchy of the input type.
 *       If no member functions are found, an empty vector is returned.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ROSE_ASSERT macro to assert that the input type is a complete
 *       class type and that the ClassHierarchyWrapper has been properly initialized. If these
 *       assertions fail, the program will terminate with an error message.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
            SgProject* project = ...; // Assume a valid SgProject pointer
            SgType* type = ...; // Assume a valid SgType pointer representing a class
            vector<SgMemberFunctionDeclaration*> memberFunctions = GetAllMemberFunctionsInClassHierarchy(type);
            foreach (SgMemberFunctionDeclaration* func, memberFunctions) {
                printf("Found member function: %s\n", func->get_name().c_str());
            }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn vector<SgVariableDeclaration*> GetAllVariableDeclarationsInAncestors(SgType *type)
 *
 * @brief Walks over the call hierarchy and collects all data members in a vector.
 *
 * This function traverses the class hierarchy of a given type, starting from its
 * definition, and gathers all variable declarations found in the ancestors of this
 * type. The collected variable declarations are stored in a vector and returned.
 *
 * @param type The SgType object representing the class for which to collect data members.
 *             Must be an instance of SgClassType. If not, a ROSE_ASSERT is triggered.
 *
 * @return A vector containing all variable declarations found in the ancestors
 *         of the input type.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type has a complete class definition.
 *          If this assumption does not hold, a ROSE_ASSERT may be triggered at runtime.
 *
 * @pre The input type must have a valid SgClassDeclaration and SgClassDefinition associated with it.
 *
 * @post The returned vector contains all variable declarations found in the ancestors of the input type.
 *
 * @note This function uses the ClassHierarchyWrapper class to traverse the class hierarchy.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgProject* project = ...; // assume a valid ROSE project
 *           SgClassType* type = ...;  // assume a valid SgClassType object
 *           vector<SgVariableDeclaration*> variables = GetAllVariableDeclarationsInAncestors(type);
 *           foreach (SgVariableDeclaration* var, variables) {
 *               cout << "Found variable declaration: " << var->get_name() << endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool CheckIfFunctionAcceptsArgumentIgnoreConstRefAndTypedef(SgMemberFunctionDeclaration* decl, SgType * args = NULL)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a member function accepts an argument of the class type, ignoring const and reference modifiers.
 *
 * This function verifies whether a given member function declaration accepts
 * exactly one argument that matches the type of its associated class,
 * disregarding any const or reference qualifiers. It also checks for typedefs.
 *
 * @param decl The SgMemberFunctionDeclaration object to be checked.
 * @param args Optional SgType pointer representing the expected argument type.
 *             If NULL, the function will use the type of the associated class instead.
 *
 * @return True if the member function accepts an argument matching the class type,
 *         false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input SgMemberFunctionDeclaration object
 *          is valid and has an associated class declaration. It does not perform
 *          any additional checks on the inputs.
 *
 * @pre The input SgMemberFunctionDeclaration object must have a non-NULL associated
 *      class declaration, which can be retrieved via get_associatedClassDeclaration().
 *
 * @post If the function returns true, it guarantees that the member function accepts
 *       exactly one argument of the expected type (either provided or inferred from
 *       the associated class).
 *
 * @note This function uses ROSE_ASSERT to verify certain preconditions and invariants.
 *       It does not handle cases where these assertions fail.
 *
 * @example Here's an example demonstrating how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgMemberFunctionDeclaration* decl = ...; // obtain the declaration object
 *           SgType* args = NULL;
 *           if (CheckIfFunctionAcceptsArgumentIgnoreConstRefAndTypedef(decl, args)) {
 *               std::cout << "The member function accepts an argument of the class type." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The member function does not accept an argument of the class type." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn static bool HasTrivialAssign(const SgType * const inputType, bool checkPod)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a type has a trivial assignment operator.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given type has a trivial assignment operator,
 * which is an assignment operator that performs a simple bitwise copy of the
 * entire object. The function takes into account various factors such as constness,
 * reference types, and the presence of user-declared assignment operators in the
 * class hierarchy.
 *
 * @param inputType A pointer to the type to be checked for trivial assignment.
 * @param checkPod If true, the function also checks if the type is a POD (Plain Old Data)
 *                 type, which has additional implications for its memory layout and
 *                 behavior.
 *
 * @return True if the type has a trivial assignment operator, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is valid and correctly formed.
 *          If the input type is invalid or ill-formed, the behavior of this function
 *          is undefined.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @post None.
 *
 * @note This function uses various heuristics to determine whether a type has a trivial
 *       assignment operator. These heuristics may not cover all possible cases, and
 *       the result of this function should be used with caution.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // some type
 *           bool hasTrivialAssign = HasTrivialAssign(myType, true);
 *           if (hasTrivialAssign) {
 *               printf("Type %s has a trivial assignment operator.\n", myType->get_name().getString());
 *           } else {
 *               printf("Type %s does not have a trivial assignment operator.\n", myType->get_name().getString());
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn static bool HasTrivialCopy(const SgType * const inputType, bool checkPod)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type has a trivial copy constructor.
 *
 * This function determines whether the input type has a trivial copy constructor,
 * which means that the type's objects can be copied using a simple bitwise copy operation.
 * It checks for the presence of user-declared constructors and other conditions that
 * would prevent the compiler from generating a default copy constructor.
 *
 * @param inputType The SgType object representing the type to check.
 * @param checkPod If true, also checks if the type is POD (Plain Old Data).
 *
 * @return True if the type has a trivial copy constructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is valid and properly initialized.
 *          It does not perform any error checking on the input parameters.
 *
 * @pre The inputType parameter must be non-null.
 *      The checkPod parameter must be either true or false.
 *
 * @post The return value indicates whether the input type has a trivial copy constructor.
 *
 * @note This function uses various heuristics to determine if a type has a trivial copy
 *       constructor, but it may not cover all possible cases. Use with caution.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // assume myType is initialized correctly
 *           bool hasTrivialCopy = HasTrivialCopy(myType, true);
 *           if (hasTrivialCopy) {
 *               printf("My type has a trivial copy constructor!\n");
 *           } else {
 *               printf("My type does not have a trivial copy constructor.\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn static bool HasTrivialConstructor(const SgType* const inputType, bool checkPod)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a class has a trivial constructor.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given class has a trivial constructor,
 * i.e., a constructor that performs no operations. It checks for the presence
 * of any user-declared constructors in the class hierarchy and returns false
 * if such a constructor is found. If checkPod is true, it also considers classes
 * with POD (Plain Old Data) types as having trivial constructors.
 *
 * @param inputType The SgType object representing the class to be checked.
 *                   Must not be null.
 * @param checkPod  A boolean flag indicating whether to consider POD types
 *                  as having trivial constructors. Defaults to false.
 *
 * @return True if the class has a trivial constructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the inputType is a valid SgType object
 *          representing a class. If this assumption does not hold, undefined
 *          behavior may occur.
 *
 * @pre The inputType must be a non-null pointer to an SgType object.
 *
 * @post None.
 *
 * @note This function uses the IsPod() and GetAllMemberFunctionsInClassHierarchy()
 *       functions internally. Their documentation should be consulted for
 *       further details on their behavior.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Obtain an SgType object representing a class
 *           bool hasTrivialConstructor = HasTrivialConstructor(myType, false);
 *           if (hasTrivialConstructor) {
 *               printf("The class has a trivial constructor.\n");
 *           } else {
 *               printf("The class does not have a trivial constructor.\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn static bool HasTrivialDestructor(const SgType * const inputType, bool checkPod)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type has a trivial destructor.
 *
 * This function determines whether the destructor of a given type is trivial,
 * i.e., it does not perform any operations. A trivial destructor is typically
 * generated by the compiler for types that do not have user-declared destructors.
 * The function also considers the case where the input type is a POD (Plain Old Data)
 * and whether to check for this condition.
 *
 * @param inputType The input type to be checked for a trivial destructor.
 *                   Must be a valid SgType pointer.
 * @param checkPod  A boolean flag indicating whether to consider the input type as a POD.
 *                  If true, the function returns immediately if the input type is a POD.
 *
 * @return True if the input type has a trivial destructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is valid and properly initialized.
 *           Failure to meet this precondition may result in undefined behavior.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgType pointer.
 *
 * @post The function returns true if the input type has a trivial destructor, false otherwise.
 *
 * @note This function uses the IsPod() function to check if the input type is a POD.
 *       It also uses the GetAllMemberFunctionsInClassHierarchy() function to retrieve all member functions
 *       of the input type and its base classes.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // Initialize myType to a valid SgType pointer
 *           bool hasTrivialDestructor = HasTrivialDestructor(myType, true);
 *           if (hasTrivialDestructor) {
 *               printf("My type has a trivial destructor.\n");
 *           } else {
 *               printf("My type does not have a trivial destructor.\n");
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool IsEmpty(const SgType * const inputType, bool checkIsClass)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given class or union type is empty.
 *
 * This function checks whether a given class or union type has any non-static data members 
 * that are not bit fields of length 0. It also verifies that the class does not have any virtual 
 * base classes and that all its member functions are non-virtual. The function recursively 
 * checks the emptiness of any nested types.
 *
 * @param inputType A pointer to the SgType object representing the type to be checked for emptiness.
 *                  Must not be NULL.
 * @param checkIsClass A boolean flag indicating whether to check if the input type is a class. If true, 
 *                     the function will return false immediately if the input type is not a class.
 *
 * @return True if the given class or union type is empty; false otherwise.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type has already been resolved and is complete.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be non-NULL and have been previously resolved.
 *
 * @post If true is returned, it guarantees that the input type does not contain any non-static 
 *        data members that are not bit fields of length 0, nor virtual base classes or member functions.
 *
 * @note This function uses recursive descent to check nested types. It may cause a stack overflow 
 *       if the input type has deeply nested structures.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgClassType* myClass = ...; // assume myClass is defined elsewhere
 *           bool isEmpty = IsEmpty(myClass, true);
 *           if (isEmpty) {
 *               std::cout << "The class is empty." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The class is not empty." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */
/**
 * @fn bool IsTrivial(const SgType * const inputType, bool checkTrivial)
 *
 * @brief Checks if a given type is trivial.
 *
 * This function determines whether a given type is considered trivial according to the C++ standard. 
 * A trivial type is a scalar type or a class type that has no non-trivial default constructors, copy constructors, move constructors, assignment operators, or destructors. 
 * Additionally, all base classes and members of the class must also be trivial.
 *
 * @param inputType The type to check for triviality.
 * @param checkTrivial If true, perform additional checks for POD (Plain Old Data) types.
 *
 * @return True if the type is trivial; false otherwise.
 *
 * This function performs a series of checks on the given type:
 * - Checks if the type is a scalar or pointer type.
 * - Verifies that all members and base classes are trivial.
 * - Ensures the class has no virtual functions, non-static data members with initializers, or non-trivial constructors, copy constructors, assignment operators, or destructors.
 *
 * @warning This function assumes that the input type is a valid SgType object. 
 *          If the input type is invalid or ill-formed, undefined behavior may occur.
 *
 * @pre The input type must be a valid SgType object.
 *
 * @post None
 *
 * @note This function uses various ROSE assertions to ensure internal consistency and correctness. 
 *       These assertions are enabled only in debug builds and do not affect the functionality of this function.
 *
 * @example This example demonstrates how to use this function:
 *          @code
 *           SgType* myType = ...; // assume a valid SgType object
 *           bool isTrivial = IsTrivial(myType, true);
 *           if (isTrivial) {
 *               std::cout << "The type is trivial." << std::endl;
 *           } else {
 *               std::cout << "The type is not trivial." << std::endl;
 *           }
 *          @endcode
 */